const THINKERS = [
  ["parmenides", "Parmenides", "", "early 5th c. BCE", "Eleatic philosopher who argued that being is one, whole and unchanging.", ["On Nature (fragments)"], [], { x: "Parmenides came from Elea, a Greek colony on the Tyrrhenian coast of southern Italy, and was active early in the fifth century BCE. Plato's dialogue Parmenides has him visit Athens with Zeno at about sixty-five and meet a very young Socrates, which would place his birth around 515. Speusippus reported that he gave Elea its laws, and a portrait head of the first century CE found there names him a natural philosopher and links him with the cult of Apollo the Healer. He wrote a single poem in hexameter verse, perhaps 800 lines long, of which about 160 survive in quotations; the title On Nature was probably added later. Sextus Empiricus preserves most of its opening, and Simplicius copied out the central argument (fragment 8) because the book had become rare.\n\nThe proem describes a journey. Mares draw the narrator's chariot, guided by the daughters of the Sun, to the gates of the paths of Night and Day; Justice opens them, and a goddess welcomes him. She promises he will learn both “the unshaken heart of well-rounded truth” and the opinions of mortals, in which there is no true reliability.\n\nShe sets out the ways of inquiry (fragment 2). One is that it is and cannot not be; the other, that it is not and must not be, is a path from which no report comes, since what is not can be neither known nor pointed out. Fragment 6 scorns mortals who “wander two-headed”, taking being and not-being as the same and not the same. Fragment 7 warns against letting habit force one to rely on an aimless eye and an echoing ear, and tells the listener to judge by reason. Fragment 8 then lists the signs on the true road: what is is ungenerated and imperishable, whole, of a single kind, unmoved and complete. It cannot come to be from what is not, cannot be divided, and is held within limits, “like the bulk of a well-rounded sphere”, equal in every direction from the center.\n\nThe second part of the poem, the Doxa, gives the cosmology of mortals, built on two forms, light and night. It describes the heavens, sun, moon and Milky Way, says the moon shines with light borrowed from the sun, and, according to later reports, identifies the morning and evening star as one body; it also gave an account of human reproduction. How the two parts fit is the central puzzle. Readers have taken Parmenides as a strict monist for whom only one unchanging thing exists (Guthrie); as an analyst of what can be thought or said (Russell, Owen); as setting the conditions any fundamental entity must meet (Mourelatos, Curd); as describing one reality in its intelligible and its perceptible aspects, the dominant ancient view; or as distinguishing necessary being from the contingent world of the cosmology (John Palmer).\n\nHis influence shaped the rest of Greek philosophy. Zeno's paradoxes defended him, and Melissus restated his monism. Empedocles, Anaxagoras and the atomists accepted that nothing comes into being from nothing and built pluralist physics to meet his demands. Plato portrayed him with reverence in the Parmenides and had the Eleatic Visitor of the Sophist test his teaching, and Aristotle discussed him among the first inquirers into being.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/parmenides/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Sanzio_01_Parmenides.jpg", alt: "Detail of Raphael's fresco The School of Athens showing a standing figure in a pale robe", cap: "The figure traditionally identified as Parmenides in Raphael's fresco The School of Athens (1509–1511), Vatican. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["socrates", "Socrates", "", "c. 470–399 BCE", "Athenian philosopher whose questioning of virtue, knowledge and the examined life profoundly shaped later philosophy.", ["Plato, Apology", "Plato, Euthyphro"], [], { x: "Socrates was born in Athens in 469 BCE, in the deme of Alopece. His father, Sophroniscus, was a stoneworker, and his mother, Phaenarete, a midwife. As a young man he read Anaxagoras's book eagerly, Plato reports. He married Xanthippe and had three sons, Lamprocles, Sophroniscus and Menexenus. He served as a hoplite at Potidaea (432–429), where he saved the wounded Alcibiades, at Delium (424), where he was admired in the retreat, and at Amphipolis (422).\n\nTwice he defied those in power. In 406, serving on the presiding committee of the Council, he refused to put to the vote an illegal proposal to try the generals of the Arginusae battle as a group. Under the Thirty, the oligarchy of 404–403, he refused an order to arrest Leon of Salamis. His closest associates included Alcibiades and Critias, a leader of the Thirty, which damaged his standing in the restored democracy.\n\nIn Plato's Apology Socrates tells how his friend Chaerephon asked the Delphic oracle whether anyone was wiser than Socrates, and the oracle said no one was. Knowing he had no wisdom of importance, he questioned politicians, poets and craftsmen, found that they believed they knew what they did not, and concluded that his wisdom lay in recognizing his ignorance. He took questioning as a mission from the god to improve the souls of his fellow citizens. His method, the elenchus, tests an interlocutor's definition (“What is piety?” in the Euthyphro) against their other beliefs until it collapses, and many conversations end in perplexity (aporia). He spoke of a daimonion, an inner sign that only ever held him back. His habit of professing ignorance while steering every discussion is called Socratic irony.\n\nIn 399 Meletus, joined by Anytus and Lycon, charged him with impiety: failing to recognize the city's gods, introducing new divinities and corrupting the young. Found guilty, he proposed a fine of thirty minae, and the jury voted for death. The execution waited about a month for a sacred ship to return from Delos; he refused Crito's plan for his escape and drank hemlock. At his trial, in Plato's account, he said that the unexamined life is not worth living for a human being.\n\nSocrates wrote nothing, which creates the Socratic problem. Aristophanes' comedy Clouds (423) shows a Socrates who studies nature and teaches tricks of argument, probably a composite of several intellectuals; Xenophon's Memorabilia shows a practical moralist; Plato, about twenty-five at the trial, gives the richest portrait, though his dialogues do not agree with one another. Gregory Vlastos's reading, which found the historical Socrates in Plato's early dialogues, dominated scholarship until the 1990s; others read each dialogue on its own terms. Doctrines such as the claim that no one does wrong willingly, so that wrongdoing comes from ignorance, belong to this contested Socrates. Almost every later school except the Epicureans counted him as a founder or model.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/socrates/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/socrates/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Socrates”" }], img: { file: "Socrates,_bust,_old_age_LCCN2017657365.jpg", alt: "Photograph of a marble bust of Socrates", cap: "Photograph of a bust of Socrates (Library of Congress). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["plato", "Plato", "", "c. 428–348 BCE", "Student of Socrates and founder of the Academy; theory of Forms.", ["Republic", "Symposium", "Phaedo", "Theaetetus"], ["socrates", "parmenides"], { x: "Plato lived in Athens from about 428 to 348 BCE, born into a prominent family. As a young man he belonged to the circle around Socrates, whose trial and execution in 399 BCE shaped his life's work. He founded the Academy in Athens, the first enduring school of philosophy in the West, where Aristotle studied for twenty years, and he is said to have visited Sicily to advise the rulers of Syracuse, an episode described in letters attributed to him whose authenticity most scholars doubt.\n\nNearly all his works are dialogues, and in most of them Socrates leads the conversation. Plato never speaks in his own voice, so his views have to be inferred from the arguments and the drama, and scholars debate how far his Socrates speaks for the historical Socrates and how far for Plato. The dialogues are often grouped as early, short Socratic conversations that test definitions of virtues and end in perplexity, such as the Euthyphro and Laches; middle, including the Phaedo, Symposium, Republic and Phaedrus; and late, including the Theaetetus, Sophist, Statesman, Timaeus, Philebus and Laws. The grouping itself is disputed.\n\nThe middle dialogues set out the theory of Forms. Beyond the changing things we perceive there are eternal, unchanging Forms, such as Beauty itself, Justice itself and Equality itself, which sensible things imitate or participate in, and which only reason can grasp. The Phaedo argues for the immortality of the soul, which is akin to the Forms and sees them best when freed from the body; the Symposium describes love as an ascent from beautiful bodies to Beauty itself.\n\nThe Republic asks what justice is and why it is better to be just than unjust. It answers by comparing a city and a soul, each with three parts, and defines justice as each part doing its own work under the rule of reason. The ideal city is ruled by philosopher-kings, educated to know the Form of the Good, which the book compares to the sun; the allegory of the cave depicts ordinary people as prisoners who mistake shadows for reality.\n\nIn later dialogues Plato subjected his own ideas to criticism: the Parmenides raises objections to the Forms, and the Theaetetus examines and rejects three definitions of knowledge. The Timaeus offers a cosmology in which a divine craftsman orders the world on the model of the Forms, and the Laws, his longest work, designs a practical legal code for a new city. Plato's influence on later philosophy, from Aristotle and the Neoplatonists to Christian and Islamic thought, has been immense.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/plato/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "The_bust_of_Plato,_in_a_niche_MET_DP878744.jpg", alt: "Print of a bust of Plato set in a niche", cap: "The bust of Plato, in a niche, a print in the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["aristotle", "Aristotle", "", "384–322 BCE", "Student of Plato and founder of the Lyceum; first systematic logic, ethics and biology.", ["Metaphysics", "Physics", "Nicomachean Ethics", "Politics"], ["plato"], { x: "Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in Stagira, in northern Greece, and at about seventeen went to Athens, where he studied in Plato's Academy for some twenty years, until Plato's death. He then spent several years in Asia Minor and on Lesbos, doing much of his biological research, and served as tutor to the young Alexander of Macedon. In 335 he returned to Athens and founded his own school, the Lyceum. After Alexander's death in 323, facing hostility in Athens, he withdrew to Chalcis, where he died the next year.\n\nOf roughly two hundred treatises he wrote, about thirty-one survive. Most read as lecture notes or working drafts; the polished dialogues he wrote for a wider public are lost. His method typically begins from the appearances (phainomena) and the reputable opinions (endoxa) of others, sets out the puzzles they raise, and works through them.\n\nHe created the first systematic logic, the theory of the syllogism, collected in the works later called the Organon. In the Categories he distinguished ten basic kinds of being, with substance first among them. A full explanation of anything cites four causes: its matter, its form, the source of its change, and its end or purpose. Natural things, organisms above all, are composites of matter and form, change is the actualization of a potentiality, and nature acts for ends without being designed. The soul is the form of a living body. The Metaphysics studies being as being and the first causes, culminating in an unmoved mover.\n\nThe Nicomachean Ethics holds that the human good is eudaimonia, flourishing or happiness, an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue over a complete life. Virtues of character are stable dispositions to feel and act well, lying in a mean between excess and deficiency, as courage lies between cowardice and rashness, and they are guided by practical wisdom (phronesis). The highest life is that of contemplation.\n\nThe Politics declares that a human being is by nature a political animal, since only in a city can people live well, and classifies constitutions by whether one, few or many rule, and whether they rule for the common good. Aristotle also wrote on rhetoric, poetics, physics, astronomy, psychology and, at length, on animals. His works shaped philosophy in late antiquity, the Islamic world, where he was called the First Teacher, and the Latin Middle Ages, where he was simply the Philosopher.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-politics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Aristotle's Political Theory”" }], img: { file: "Aristotle_with_a_Bust_of_Homer_MET_DP319026.jpg", alt: "Rembrandt's painting of Aristotle resting his hand on a bust of Homer", cap: "Rembrandt, Aristotle with a Bust of Homer, 1653 (Metropolitan Museum of Art). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["pyrrho", "Pyrrho of Elis", "Pyrrho", "c. 360–270 BCE", "Greek philosopher associated with the beginnings of the skeptical tradition named Pyrrhonism.", [], [], { x: "Pyrrho of Elis lived from about 365–360 to about 275–270 BCE. His most reliable teacher was Anaxarchus of Abdera, a Democritean, and with Anaxarchus he is said to have accompanied Alexander the Great's expedition to India. Diogenes Laertius reports that meeting the Indian “naked wise men” (gymnosophists) shaped his philosophy, a claim the evidence does little to support. His city honored him: Pausanias saw his statue in the marketplace of Elis, and Diogenes says Pyrrho was made high priest and that philosophers were exempted from taxes for his sake. He wrote nothing, and nothing suggests a Pyrrhonist movement after his immediate followers until the first century BCE.\n\nMany stories about him probably come from Antigonus of Carystus, a biographer fond of sensation. In one, Pyrrho ignored cliffs, carts and dogs, so that his friends had to keep him from harm; the later Pyrrhonist Aenesidemus objected that he acted sensibly enough. Others show remarkable composure, such as enduring surgery without flinching and staying calm in a storm at sea, and indifference to convention, such as doing the housework and washing a pig himself.\n\nThe best evidence comes from his follower Timon of Phlius, whose satirical verses, the Silloi (Lampoons), mocked other philosophers. A summary of Timon by Aristocles of Messene, quoted by Eusebius, sets out three questions for anyone who would be happy: what things are like by nature, what attitude we should take toward them, and what will come of taking it. Things are adiaphora, astathmēta and anepikrita, and so our sensations and opinions are neither true nor false. We should therefore be without opinions, uninclined and unwavering, saying of each thing that it no more is than is not (ou mallon). The result is first speechlessness (aphasia), then freedom from disturbance (ataraxia).\n\nThe three adjectives can be read as a claim about reality, that things are indifferent, unstable and indeterminate, or as a claim about us, that they are undifferentiable, unmeasurable and undecidable. On the first reading Pyrrho held a metaphysics of indeterminacy and was hardly a skeptic; on the second he was an early, unsophisticated skeptic. Some scholars, such as Jacques Brunschwig, credit only the threefold description to Pyrrho and the rest to Timon, and suggest that Pyrrho's concern was evaluative: whether things are good or bad. Cicero, who mentions Pyrrho about ten times, always on ethics and beside the Stoic Aristo, has him holding that virtue is the only good, probably a confusion.\n\nAenesidemus revived the name in the first century BCE, and Sextus Empiricus in the second century CE preserved the mature Pyrrhonist method: set opposing arguments of equal strength (isostheneia) against each other, suspend judgment, and find that tranquility follows, while living by appearances, laws and customs. Pyrrho's tranquility, as Aristocles reports it, followed a settled verdict on the nature of things, a difference that makes it risky to read Sextus's methods back into Pyrrho.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pyrrho/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Pyrrho_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg", alt: "Engraved portrait of Pyrrho", cap: "Pyrrho, an engraved portrait from Thomas Stanley's The History of Philosophy (17th century). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["epicurus", "Epicurus", "", "341–270 BCE", "Founder of the Garden, whose philosophy connects atomistic physics with pleasure, friendship and freedom from fear.", ["Letter to Menoeceus", "Principal Doctrines"], [], { x: "Epicurus was born in 341 BCE to Athenian parents, Neocles and Chaerestrata, and grew up on Samos. He came to Athens at eighteen, then joined his family at Colophon, where he studied with Nausiphanes, a follower of Democritus, a debt Epicurus later denied. After teaching and gathering followers at Mytilene and Lampsacus, he returned to Athens in 307/306 and bought a property with a garden, which gave his school its name. He died in 270 at about seventy. His will, preserved by Diogenes Laertius, provides for the children of his friends and names a successor. His followers lived as a community of friends, met each month to honor his memory and wore rings bearing his image.\n\nMost of his writing is lost. Book 10 of Diogenes Laertius's Lives preserves three letters, To Herodotus on physics, To Pythocles on celestial phenomena and To Menoeceus on ethics, together with the Principal Doctrines, a set of maxims; another collection, the Vatican Sayings, survives in a manuscript in the Vatican. His thirty-seven-book On Nature exists only in charred papyri from a villa at Herculaneum, whose library seems to have belonged to the Epicurean Philodemus. Lucretius's poem, Cicero's hostile dialogues and a huge inscription set up at Oenoanda by another Diogenes add much.\n\nKnowledge rests on criteria: sensations, preconceptions (general concepts formed from repeated perception) and feelings of pleasure and pain. Every sensation is true; error comes from the opinions we add. Claims about what cannot be seen are accepted when nothing observed counts against them. Nothing comes from nothing, and the universe is infinite: bodies made of indivisible atoms moving through void, in infinitely many worlds. Atoms have minimal parts though they cannot be split, and at times they swerve by a minimal amount without cause, which explains how collisions begin and, in Lucretius, makes free action possible. We perceive things through thin films that stream off their surfaces. The gods exist, blessed and immortal, and take no part in running the world.\n\nThe soul is a body of fine atoms spread through the body, and it disperses at death. So death is nothing to us: while we exist death is absent, and when death comes we no longer exist. The goal of life is pleasure, understood as freedom from bodily pain and from mental disturbance (ataraxia). Epicurus distinguishes the pleasure of a settled state of well-being from the pleasure of a process such as eating when hungry, and he sorts desires into the natural and necessary, the natural but unnecessary, and the empty, which aim at wealth, fame or immortality and can never be satisfied. Prudence weighs pleasures against pains, and a simple life supplies what nature needs.\n\nJustice is a compact neither to harm nor to be harmed, and it is nothing in itself apart from such agreements; the wise act justly because injustice brings fear of discovery and they want nothing that would tempt them. Friendship ranks among the greatest goods: it may begin in usefulness, but it comes to be valued for itself, and the wise person will die for a friend if need be. Epicurus advised staying out of politics where possible, a stance Plutarch later attacked in an essay on the Epicurean maxim “live unnoticed”. Opponents slandered him as a sensualist; Diogenes Laertius, and even hostile witnesses, describe a man of great kindness.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epicurus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Marble_head_of_Epikouros_MET_DP123870.jpg", alt: "Marble head of Epicurus", cap: "Marble head of Epicurus, a Roman copy of a Greek portrait (Metropolitan Museum of Art). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["lucretius", "Lucretius", "", "c. 99–55 BCE", "Roman poet who set out Epicurean atomism in verse.", ["On the Nature of Things"], ["epicurus"], { x: "Titus Lucretius Carus lived from the mid-90s to the mid-50s BCE; Jerome dates his birth to 94. Almost nothing is known of his life. The only contemporary mention comes in a letter of February 54 BCE from Cicero to his brother Quintus, which praises the poem's brilliance and its craft. Jerome's story that a love potion drove Lucretius mad, that he wrote in intervals of sanity and that he killed himself is generally treated as unreliable, perhaps a product of Christian hostility to a poet who denied providence. The poem's addressee, Memmius, was most likely Gaius Memmius, praetor in 58 BCE.\n\nDe rerum natura (On the Nature of Things) sets out Epicurean physics in six books of Latin hexameters, arranged in pairs. Books 1 and 2 establish that nothing comes from nothing, that the universe is infinite and made of atoms and void, and refute Heraclitus, Empedocles and Anaxagoras; they describe atomic motion, the swerve and the image of dust dancing in a sunbeam. Books 3 and 4 offer some thirty proofs that the soul is mortal, argue that death is nothing to us, and explain perception through films (simulacra) shed by objects, along with thought, dreams and a harsh treatment of erotic love. Books 5 and 6 cover the mortality of our world, the origins of life and of human society and religion, then weather, earthquakes, magnets and disease, ending with the plague of Athens adapted from Thucydides.\n\nThe poem opens with a hymn to Venus and soon tells of Agamemnon sacrificing his daughter Iphigenia, to show the evils religion has driven people to. Epicurus appears as a hero who traveled in mind past the flaming walls of the world, and in Book 5 as a god. Verse, Lucretius explains, is the honey a doctor smears on the rim of a cup so that children will drink bitter medicine. He is our main surviving source for the swerve, which he uses to explain how atoms collide and how voluntary motion escapes the chain of fate. His symmetry argument holds that the endless time before our birth, which troubles no one, is a mirror of the time after death.\n\nThe poem seems complete but lacks a final revision. Passages repeat with variations, Book 5 promises a long account of the gods' dwellings that never comes, and the ending, on the plague, offers no Epicurean consolation, which has prompted long debate over whether Lucretius meant to end there.\n\nVergil echoed him in the Georgics (“happy the one who could learn the causes of things”), while the Christian writer Lactantius called him mad. The poem survived in two ninth-century manuscripts now in Leiden, and Poggio Bracciolini found a copy in 1417. Denis Lambin's edition of 1563 praised him as the sweetest Roman poet, Montaigne drew on Book 3 for his essay on learning to die, and Pierre Gassendi's Christian atomism drew heavily on him. With so much of Epicurus lost, the poem remains the fullest account of Epicurean physics.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Lucretius_drawn_by_Michael_Burghers.png", alt: "Engraved portrait of Lucretius", cap: "Lucretius, drawn by the engraver Michael Burghers (died 1727). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["epictetus", "Epictetus", "", "c. 50–135 CE", "Stoic teacher, born a slave, who taught attention to what is in our power.", ["Discourses", "Handbook (Enchiridion)"], ["socrates"], { x: "Epictetus was born in the 50s CE at Hierapolis, a Greek city in Asia Minor, and was a slave of Epaphroditus, an administrator at Nero's court. While enslaved he studied with the Stoic Musonius Rufus. Once freed, he taught philosophy in Rome until Domitian's edict of 89 banished philosophers from Italy, and he then founded a school at Nicopolis in Epirus, in northwestern Greece, where he taught until his death around 135. He walked with a limp, which ancient writers put down to arthritis or to abuse in slavery. He never married, but late in life he adopted a child whose parents could not support it.\n\nHe wrote nothing for publication. His student Arrian of Nicomedia recorded his teaching in the Discourses, of which four of eight or more books survive, and abridged it in the Handbook (Encheiridion); a few further fragments are quoted by other authors. The Discourses keep the voice of a teacher talking to students and visitors, by turns persuading, refuting and instructing.\n\nEverything turns on the line between what is up to us and what is not. Up to us is our volition (prohairesis): our judgments, choices, desires and aversions. Our bodies, property, reputation, offices and other people's actions are externals. Volition is “by nature unimpeded”, so no one can take our freedom, and volition is what each of us really is. Only a good state of volition, virtue, is good; externals are indifferent, though they are to be handled with care and skill, as ballplayers care about the ball while knowing it does not matter in itself. Human beings, unlike animals, can examine an impression (phantasia) before assenting to it, and the right use of impressions is the whole task. Medea killed her children because she judged it to her advantage; show her the error, and she would stop.\n\nStudents work through three fields of study: desire and aversion, so that they want only what is up to them; impulse and appropriate action, which concerns duties belonging to one's relationships as child, parent, sibling or citizen; and assent, the training of judgment, with logic serving to secure the other two. Passions such as grief, fear and envy come from the false judgment that happiness lies in externals. Epictetus rejects being unfeeling “like a statue”, and teaches loving others as one loves a fragile cup, knowing it may break. Zeus is rational, benevolent and present in the order of nature, and human minds are fragments of his. Socrates and Diogenes the Cynic are his models, and he recommends reviewing one's day each night.\n\nMarcus Aurelius never heard him but counted himself a follower, and in the third century Origen remarked that Epictetus had more readers among ordinary people than Plato. Simplicius wrote a long commentary on the Handbook in the sixth century; Poliziano translated it into Latin in 1497, and Christianized versions circulated in monasteries. Justus Lipsius and Guillaume du Vair found his Stoicism compatible with Christianity, Pascal judged its confidence in self-improvement arrogant, and Descartes's personal ethics follows Epictetus's division between what depends on us and what does not.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epictetus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Epicteti_Enchiridion_Latinis_versibus_adumbratum_(Oxford_1715)_frontispiece.jpg", alt: "Engraving of Epictetus", cap: "Frontispiece of an edition of Epictetus's Handbook in Latin verse (Oxford, 1715). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["sextus", "Sextus Empiricus", "Sextus", "2nd–3rd c. CE", "Physician and Pyrrhonist whose writings preserve ancient skepticism.", ["Outlines of Pyrrhonism", "Against the Mathematicians"], ["pyrrho"], { x: "Sextus Empiricus probably lived in the second or early third century CE. Almost nothing is known of his life. His name records that he belonged to the Empirical school of medicine, which based treatment on observation and avoided claims about hidden causes. His surviving writings are the most complete account of ancient Pyrrhonian skepticism, the tradition that traced itself to Pyrrho of Elis.\n\nThey comprise the Outlines of Pyrrhonism, in three books, and the eleven books known as Against the Mathematicians. The first book of the Outlines describes Pyrrhonism and distinguishes it from other schools, including the Academic skeptics; the other two books and the later work turn its methods against the doctrines of the dogmatists in logic, physics and ethics, and against the liberal arts, grammar, rhetoric, geometry, arithmetic, astrology and music.\n\nSkepticism, Sextus wrote, is an ability to set out oppositions among appearances and judgments in any way whatever. Faced with arguments of equal force on both sides of a question, the skeptic suspends judgment (epochē), and tranquillity (ataraxia) follows, as a shadow follows a body. The skeptic did not set out to be tranquil by suspending judgment; it came about by chance, as when the painter Apelles, failing to paint a horse's foam, threw his sponge at the picture and so produced the effect.\n\nTo generate oppositions Sextus collected the skeptical modes. The Ten Modes, associated with Aenesidemus, show that things appear differently to different animals, people, senses, circumstances and cultures. The Five Modes, associated with Agrippa, show that any attempt to justify a claim leads to disagreement, infinite regress, relativity, arbitrary assumption or circularity. Skeptical phrases such as “I determine nothing” report how things appear to the skeptic without asserting anything.\n\nThe skeptic still lives, following appearances: the guidance of nature, the compulsion of feelings, the laws and customs of his society, and the teaching of the arts. Scholars disagree about whether this means the Pyrrhonist has ordinary beliefs or none at all. The Latin translation of the Outlines in the 1560s helped shape the skepticism of Montaigne and the problems that Descartes set out to answer.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sextus-empiricus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/skepticism-ancient/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Ancient Skepticism”" }] }],
  ["augustine", "Augustine of Hippo", "Augustine", "354–430", "North African bishop who joined Christian theology with Platonism.", ["Confessions (c. 400)", "The City of God (413–426)"], ["plato"], { x: "Augustine was born on 13 November 354 at Thagaste in Roman North Africa, now Souk Ahras in Algeria, to a devout Christian mother, Monnica, and a father, Patricius, who was baptized only on his deathbed. He studied rhetoric at Madauros and Carthage, and at about eighteen Cicero's lost dialogue Hortensius turned him toward philosophy. For some nine years he was a “hearer” among the Manichaeans, and for about fourteen he lived with a partner who bore him a son, Adeodatus. Moving to Italy, he became professor of rhetoric at Milan, where he heard Bishop Ambrose preach and read “books of the Platonists” in Latin translation, probably Plotinus and Porphyry. In the summer of 386 he converted to an ascetic Christianity and gave up his career; Ambrose baptized him at Easter 387. Back in Africa he was ordained priest at Hippo Regius in 391 and became its bishop around 396. He died on 28 August 430, while the Vandals besieged the city.\n\nHe left about a hundred books, three hundred letters and six hundred sermons. Against the Academics (386) answers the skeptics, and his later works name things no doubt can touch: one's own existence, how things appear, and the truths of mathematics. In the City of God (11.26) he argues “if I am mistaken, I exist”, since someone who does not exist cannot be mistaken, an argument that anticipates Descartes's cogito, though Augustine built no system on it. Truths of mathematics, logic and morals are learned by consulting an inner teacher, Christ present in the mind, who lights up intelligible things as the sun lights visible ones; this is his doctrine of illumination. Faith comes first in time, and understanding is what it seeks.\n\nAgainst the Manichaean picture of two warring principles, he held that whatever exists is good insofar as it exists, so evil is a privation of good, a lack or corruption, and wrongdoing begins in a will that turns from higher goods to lower ones. On Free Choice of the Will defends human responsibility. From the mid-390s, reading Paul, he came to hold that since Adam's fall human beings cannot will the good without God's grace. In the controversy with Pelagius, who held that human nature can avoid sin by its own effort, he insisted on that dependence; African bishops condemned Pelagianism in 418, and his last decade was spent in debate with Julian of Aeclanum. Virtue, for him, is rightly ordered love: God is to be enjoyed for his own sake, created things used for the sake of God.\n\nThe Confessions, written in the later 390s as a prayer addressed to God, tells his life up to his conversion and then turns to inquiry. Book X explores memory as a vast inner storehouse in which the mind searches for God. Book XI asks what time is: the past no longer exists, the future does not yet exist, and the present has no extent, yet we measure time. His answer is that time is a stretching of the mind (distentio animi): a present of things past in memory, a present of things present in attention, and a present of things future in expectation. On the Trinity finds an image of God in the mind's memory, understanding and will.\n\nThe City of God, begun in 412 after the Visigoths sacked Rome, answers pagans who blamed Christianity for the disaster. Two loves have made two cities, the earthly city by love of self extending to contempt of God, the heavenly by love of God extending to contempt of self, and the two are intermingled throughout history. He also argued that bishops might compel the Donatists to return to the Catholic Church. Often counted the first medieval philosopher, Augustine held an authority in Latin theology that went almost unchallenged through the Middle Ages; the Confessions shaped the tradition of autobiography, and his views on sin, grace and sexuality have drawn criticism from humanists, liberals and feminists.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/augustine/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://iep.utm.edu/augustine/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Augustine” (time in Book XI)" }, { src: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/110110.htm", srcName: "Augustine, Confessions, Book X (memory), New Advent translation" }], img: { file: "Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg", alt: "Painting of Augustine writing, holding a flaming heart", cap: "Philippe de Champaigne, Saint Augustine, about 1645–50 (Los Angeles County Museum of Art). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["confucius", "Confucius (Kongzi)", "Confucius", "trad. 551–479 BCE", "Teacher whose sayings on ritual, humaneness and government founded the Confucian tradition.", ["Analects (compiled by his followers)"], [], { x: "“Confucius” is a Latin form of Kong Fuzi, “Master Kong”, more often called Kongzi. Tradition dates him to 551–479 BCE. He was born in Zou, in modern Shandong, just south of the state of Lu; his father, from a noble Lu clan known for its mastery of ritual, died when he was young, leaving the family poor though respected. The Zuozhuan chronicle names him director of corrections in Lu in entries for 509 and 500 BCE, Mencius says he kept granary accounts and livestock records, and the historian Sima Qian credits him with higher offices. Leaving Lu, he traveled among the states of Wei, Song, Chen, Cai and Chu looking for a ruler who would employ him, without success, and spent his later years teaching; Sima Qian counts seventy-seven direct disciples and some three thousand students.\n\nThe Analects (Lunyu), twenty chapters of short sayings and conversations, is the traditional record of his teaching, said by the historian Ban Gu to have been compiled by his first- and second-generation disciples. Some scholars doubt its authority, since it is rarely cited before the second century BCE. Manuscripts excavated in recent decades, from Guodian (1993) to a tomb of 59 BCE at Haihun that may preserve two lost chapters of another version, suggest that many more sayings of Confucius circulated, and that the Analects is one selection among them.\n\nAt the center of the Analects is ren, humaneness, explained as caring for others (12.22) and as overcoming oneself and returning to ritual (12.1). Li, ritual propriety, shapes desire and character, and ritual without reverence is worthless (3.26). With yi, rightness, which refuses wealth gained wrongly (7.16), zhi, wisdom, and xin, trustworthiness, these make up the character of the junzi, the exemplary person. Filial piety is the root of humaneness (1.2), and when a man testifies that his father stole a sheep, Confucius answers that in his community fathers and sons cover for each other, and uprightness lies in that (13.18). One maxim, do not impose on others what you would not want for yourself (15.24, also 12.2), is often compared to the Golden Rule, though some scholars consider such passages later additions.\n\nGovernment works through example. A ruler's virtue (de) moves the people as the wind bends the grass (12.19), and leading them by virtue and ritual instills a sense of shame that laws and punishments cannot (2.3). Confucius looked to the culture of the early Zhou dynasty (3.14) and described himself as a transmitter of the ancients (7.1). Heaven (tian), he said, had given him his virtue (7.23), and he cried that Heaven had forsaken him when his best disciple, Yan Hui, died (11.9).\n\nMencius and Xunzi developed opposed versions of his teaching. Under the Han, scholars of the Five Classics held office and Confucius received sacrifices in temples; in the Song, Zhu Xi (1130–1200) made the Analects one of the Four Books, which from the fourteenth century formed the basis of the civil service examinations and shaped education in China, Korea and Japan. Jesuit missionaries and Leibniz presented him to Europe as a rational moralist, while reformers of the May Fourth era attacked his hierarchies. Today philosophers compare his ethics with Aristotle's virtue ethics, or read it, with Henry Rosemont and Roger Ames, as an ethics of social roles.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/confucius/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Konfuzius-1770.jpg", alt: "European engraved portrait of Confucius", cap: "A European portrait of Confucius, 1770. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["laozi", "Laozi", "", "trad. 6th c. BCE", "Legendary author of the Daodejing, the founding text of Daoism.", ["Daodejing"], [], { x: "The traditional account of Laozi comes from the Han historian Sima Qian, writing around 100 BCE. It makes him Lao Dan, a keeper of archives at the Zhou court and an older contemporary of Confucius, who once sought his advice on ritual. Seeing the Zhou in decline, he left for the west; at the border pass the keeper asked him to record his teaching, and he wrote a book of some five thousand characters and was never seen again. Many scholars doubt that Laozi was a historical person, and even the name, “Old Master,” may be an honorific.\n\nThe text named after him, the Laozi or Daodejing, the classic of the way and its virtue, is now generally thought to have grown from collections of sayings circulating in the fourth century BCE and to have reached a fairly stable form by the mid-third century. Bamboo slips found at Guodian, in a tomb from around 300 BCE, contain the oldest known passages, and the silk manuscripts from Mawangdui, sealed in 168 BCE, place the part on virtue (de) before the part on the way (dao). The received text, in eighty-one short chapters, descends mainly from the editions of the commentators Wang Bi and Heshang Gong.\n\nThe Daodejing opens by declaring that the dao that can be spoken of is not the constant dao. Dao is the nameless source of all things, which gives birth to them and nourishes them without claiming them. It is associated with nonbeing (wu), emptiness and the feminine, and beings receive their de, their virtue or potency, from it. The text values ziran, naturalness or being so of oneself, and wuwei, nonaction, which means acting without forcing, self-seeking or contrived effort.\n\nIts images praise the soft and yielding over the hard: water, which overcomes the strongest things by giving way; the infant; the valley; the uncarved block. Reversal is the movement of the dao, so that what is pushed to an extreme turns into its opposite. The text criticizes the Confucian promotion of benevolence, righteousness and learning as signs that the way has been lost, and describes a sage ruler who governs by nonaction, keeping the people's desires few and letting them flourish without interference.\n\nThe Daodejing has been read as cosmology, as metaphysics, as mysticism, as a guide to life and as political advice, and has inspired thousands of commentaries, the “dark learning” (xuanxue) of the third and fourth centuries, and religious Daoism, which worships Laozi as a deity. It has been translated more often than almost any other book.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/laozi/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Lao_Tzu_-_Project_Gutenberg_eText_15250.jpg", alt: "Illustration of Laozi", cap: "Laozi, an illustration in E. T. C. Werner, Myths and Legends of China (1922). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["mencius", "Mencius (Mengzi)", "Mencius", "c. 372–289 BCE", "Confucian who argued that human nature is good.", ["Mencius"], ["confucius"], { x: "Mencius, Mengzi in Chinese, lived in the fourth century BCE, in the Warring States period, when the Zhou kings had lost real power to rival states. A Confucian, he traveled between their courts urging rulers to govern through benevolence, held office for a time in the state of Qi, and resigned after Qi's conquest of the state of Yan, which he had hoped would bring good government and which turned into oppression. The book that bears his name records his conversations with rulers, disciples and opponents.\n\nHe defended the Confucian way against two rival schools: the followers of Yang Zhu, who taught that one should not sacrifice a single hair to benefit the world, and the Mohists, who taught impartial care for all. Against the first he defended duties to others, and against the second the special obligations owed to family. Benevolence and righteousness, he told rulers, matter more than profit.\n\nMencius is best known for the claim that human nature is good. Anyone who suddenly saw a child about to fall into a well would feel alarm and compassion, not from any wish to win favor with the parents or reputation among neighbors. Such reactions show that everyone has four sprouts, the feelings of compassion, of shame or disdain, of deference, and of approving and disapproving, which, when cultivated, grow into the four virtues of benevolence, righteousness, ritual propriety and wisdom.\n\nPeople become bad when these sprouts are neglected or damaged by poor conditions, as a mountain stripped bare by axes and grazing still has the capacity to grow trees. Moral cultivation therefore requires reflection, effort, education and adequate material conditions, and it consists in extending the responses one already has, such as a king's pity for an ox led to sacrifice, to cases where one does not yet feel them. In a famous debate he rejected Gaozi's view that human nature is neither good nor bad.\n\nHis political thought held that a ruler's legitimacy depends on caring for the people, who are the true measure of Heaven's mandate; a tyrant who abuses them has forfeited his position and is a mere fellow who may be punished. Criticized by Xunzi, Mencius was later elevated by the Neo-Confucians, above all Zhu Xi, who made the Mengzi one of the Four Books at the core of Chinese education and called him the Second Sage after Confucius.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mencius/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Half_Portraits_of_the_Great_Sage_and_Virtuous_Men_of_Old_-_Meng_Ke_(孟軻).jpg", alt: "Painted half-length portrait of Mencius in robes", cap: "Mencius, from the album Half Portraits of the Great Sage and Virtuous Men of Old (National Palace Museum, Taipei). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["zhuangzi", "Zhuangzi", "", "c. 369–286 BCE", "Daoist writer of parables on spontaneity, skill and perspective.", ["Zhuangzi"], ["laozi"], { x: "Zhuangzi, Master Zhuang, was Zhuang Zhou, who lived in the late fourth century BCE. According to Sima Qian he came from the state of Meng and held a minor post, and stories in his book portray him declining office to keep his freedom. The book that carries his name was edited by Guo Xiang (died 312 CE) into thirty-three chapters, divided into the Inner Chapters, one to seven, usually taken to reflect Zhuangzi's own thought, and Outer and Miscellaneous Chapters by followers and others.\n\nThe book is written in parables, dialogues and fantastic stories, full of humor and paradox, with imaginary and historical speakers, including Confucius, who often voices Zhuangzi's own ideas. Its style is part of its philosophy, unsettling fixed ways of seeing.\n\nIts second chapter, “Discussion on Making Things Equal,” is its philosophical core. Judgments of “this” and “not this,” right and wrong, depend on the perspective from which they are made, so that Confucians and Mohists each affirm what the other denies. There are many ways (dao), and nature does not privilege one. The chapter ends with Zhuangzi dreaming he was a butterfly, and waking unsure whether he was Zhuang Zhou who had dreamed of being a butterfly or a butterfly dreaming of being Zhuang Zhou. Scholars read this as skepticism, as relativism, or as a perspectivism that frees one from attachment to any single viewpoint.\n\nOther stories show the value of skill and spontaneity. Cook Ding carves an ox so effortlessly that his knife stays sharp for nineteen years, because he follows the natural structure of the animal without forcing. A gnarled tree that is useless to carpenters lives out its natural years because no one cuts it down. The book's first chapter, “Free and Easy Wandering,” celebrates a freedom beyond conventional values, and its attitude to death, as when Zhuangzi drums and sings after his wife dies, treats life and death as transformations within the ongoing changes of nature.\n\nMany passages record debates with his friend the logician Hui Shi, such as whether Zhuangzi could know that the fish were happy. Traditionally paired with Laozi as a founder of Daoism, Zhuangzi is now read by many scholars as a philosopher in his own right, a naturalist with skeptical leanings. His thought helped shape Chan (Zen) Buddhism and much of Chinese literature and art.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/zhuangzi/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Zhuangzi-Butterfly-Dream.jpg", alt: "Chinese painting of Zhuangzi asleep, dreaming of a butterfly", cap: "Lu Zhi, Zhuangzi Dreaming of a Butterfly, 16th century. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["xunzi", "Xunzi", "", "c. 310–235 BCE", "Confucian who argued that human nature is bad and is reformed by ritual and learning.", ["Xunzi"], ["confucius"], { x: "Xunzi, Master Xun, was born around 310 BCE and died after 238 BCE, in the last decades of the Warring States period. He taught in the state of Qi, served as a magistrate in the state of Chu, and among his students were Han Fei and Li Si, who became leading figures of Legalism and of the Qin state that unified China. Long regarded as a deviant Confucian, he is now widely counted, with Confucius and Mencius, among the three great classical Confucians. The book that bears his name consists of essays on many subjects.\n\nAgainst Mencius, Xunzi declared that human nature is bad. By nature people are born with desires for profit and sensual satisfaction and with envy and hatred, and if these are followed without restraint they lead to strife and disorder. Goodness comes from wei, deliberate effort or artifice: the learned practices that transform raw nature, as warped wood must be steamed and pressed against a straightening board, and metal ground on a whetstone.\n\nRitual (li) and music are the central tools of this transformation. The ancient sage kings, seeing the chaos that unregulated desires produce, created ritual to apportion goods, mark social distinctions and give desires and emotions appropriate forms, so that they could be satisfied without conflict. Rituals of mourning, for example, give grief an ordered expression. Music shapes feeling and harmonizes people. Learning, under teachers and through the classics, never ends.\n\nXunzi held that Heaven (tian) follows constant patterns of its own and does not respond to human conduct. Eclipses, comets and strange events are no cause for fear, and prayers for rain do not bring it; good and bad fortune depend on how people order their affairs. His essay on rectifying names argued that names are fixed by convention and must be used correctly for good government, and criticized thinkers who confused them.\n\nThe heart-mind, he argued, can know the Way when it is empty, unified and still: open to new knowledge, able to hold many things together, and undisturbed by dreams and agitation. His chapter on “Dispelling Obsession” warned against minds fixated on one aspect of a matter, a fault he found in rival thinkers. Xunzi's thought shaped Han dynasty Confucianism, though later Neo-Confucians preferred Mencius.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/xunzi/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["nagarjuna", "Nāgārjuna", "", "c. 150–250 CE", "Founder of the Madhyamaka school of Buddhist philosophy.", ["Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way)"], [], { x: "Nāgārjuna lived around 150 to 250 CE, probably in southern India. Almost nothing is known of his life with any certainty, though later legends abound. A Buddhist monk, he founded the Madhyamaka, or Middle Way, school, one of the two major schools of Indian Mahāyāna philosophy.\n\nHis central work is the Mūlamadhyamakakārikā, the Root Verses on the Middle Way, in twenty-seven chapters. Other works attributed to him include the Vigrahavyāvartanī, the Dispeller of Disputes, which answers objections to his views, the Vaidalyaprakaraṇa, a critique of the categories of the Nyāya school, and the Ratnāvalī, the Precious Garland, a letter of advice to a king.\n\nHis great theme is emptiness (śūnyatā). All things are empty of svabhāva, intrinsic nature or own-being: nothing exists independently, by itself, as the foundational substance it appears to be. Everything exists in dependence on causes and conditions, on its parts, and on the concepts and designations through which it is grasped. Nāgārjuna presented emptiness as the true meaning of the Buddha's teaching of dependent origination.\n\nHis method was to show that every attempt to give things an independent nature leads to absurdity. The first chapter of the Root Verses considers whether a thing arises from itself, from something else, from both or from neither, and rejects all four, concluding that cause and effect are mutually dependent and empty. Similar arguments examine motion, the self, time and knowledge. Nāgārjuna denied that this chain of dependence ends in any foundational entity, and he rejected the charge that it amounts to nihilism: emptiness is itself empty, a corrective to misperception and no description of a hidden ultimate reality. He distinguished conventional truth, the everyday world of persons and things, from ultimate truth, their emptiness, and held that only through the conventional can the ultimate be taught.\n\nRealizing emptiness, as opposed to merely accepting it intellectually, is the way to liberation from suffering, since suffering arises from clinging to things as if they had independent existence. Madhyamaka shaped Indian philosophy for about a millennium, and through later interpreters such as Candrakīrti became central to Tibetan Buddhism and influential in Chinese and Japanese Buddhism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nagarjuna/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Nagarjuna_and_Aryadeva_as_Two_Great_Indian_Buddhist_Scholastics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg", alt: "Painting of Nagarjuna and his disciple Aryadeva", cap: "Nagarjuna and Aryadeva as Two Great Indian Buddhist Scholastics, a painting reproduced by the Google Art Project. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["shankara", "Śaṅkara", "", "8th c. CE", "Systematizer of Advaita Vedānta.", ["Commentary on the Brahmasūtras", "Upadeśasāhasrī"], [], { x: "Śaṅkara lived in the eighth century CE, though estimates range from the late seventh to the eighth. Biographies of him were written centuries later and are full of legend: they tell that he was born in Kaladi, in Kerala, renounced the world as a boy, studied with the teacher Govinda, traveled India debating rival thinkers, founded four monasteries and died young. He became the most authoritative philosopher of Advaita, or nondual, Vedānta, the school that interprets the Upaniṣads as teaching that the self and ultimate reality are one.\n\nHis accepted works are commentaries on the Brahmasūtras, on the principal Upaniṣads and on the Bhagavad Gītā, and the Upadeśasāhasrī, a treatise on teaching. Many of the hundreds of other works attributed to him are probably by later followers.\n\nBrahman alone is ultimately real: the single, unchanging ground of all existence, “one only, without a second,” in the words of the Upaniṣad. The world of names and forms depends on brahman as pots depend on clay, and it is māyā, an indeterminable appearance that is neither fully real nor simply unreal. Śaṅkara distinguished two levels: the empirical level, the shared world in which people act, which is real within its own frame, as a dream is real until one wakes, and the ultimate level of nondual brahman. At the empirical level, brahman qualified by attributes is Īśvara, God, the creator of the world.\n\nThe true self (ātman) is pure consciousness, the self-luminous witness of all experience, and it is identical with brahman. Through ignorance (avidyā) people superimpose on it false identities, “I am this body,” “I am this mind,” as someone in dim light mistakes a rope for a snake. Liberation (mokṣa) is the removal of this ignorance: the direct recognition of one's own nature, conveyed by the great sayings of the Upaniṣads, such as “you are that.” Ritual and ethical action can purify the mind and prepare it, but knowledge alone liberates, and one who knows can be liberated while still living.\n\nRival schools contested his views: Nyāya realists, Buddhists, and later Vedāntins such as Rāmānuja, who rejected the doctrine of māyā, and Madhva, who taught dualism; some opponents called him a crypto-Buddhist for the resemblance of his ideas to Mahāyāna thought. From the fourteenth century his reputation grew through hagiographies, and Advaita became the best-known form of Vedānta, central to modern Hindu thought.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/shankara/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adi_Shankara", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Adi Shankara”" }], img: { file: "Raja_Ravi_Varma_-_Sankaracharya.jpg", alt: "Painting of Śaṅkara seated with his disciples", cap: "Raja Ravi Varma, Śaṅkara with his disciples, 1904. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["ramanuja", "Rāmānuja", "", "c. 1017–1137", "Vedāntin who founded qualified non-dualism (Viśiṣṭādvaita).", ["Śrībhāṣya"], [], { x: "Rāmānuja was the chief philosopher of Viśiṣṭādvaita, qualified nondualism, one of the three main schools of Vedānta. Tradition gives his dates as 1017 to 1137, a lifespan of 120 years; recent scholarship places him around 1077 to 1157. He was born in Śrīperumbūdur, in South India, studied first with the Advaita teacher Yādavaprakāśa, and broke with him over the interpretation of scripture. He later became the successor of Yāmuna as leader of the Śrīvaiṣṇava community, devotees of Viṣṇu.\n\nHis main works are the Vedārthasaṃgraha, a summary of the meaning of the Vedas, the Śrībhāṣya, a commentary on the Brahmasūtras, and a commentary on the Bhagavad Gītā. Several devotional prose works attributed to him are of disputed authenticity.\n\nViśiṣṭādvaita affirms that all reality is one, brahman, and yet that individual selves, the world and their qualities are real. Brahman is identified with Viṣṇu-Nārāyaṇa, with his consort Lakṣmī, a personal God of infinite auspicious qualities, the inner self of all things. Rāmānuja explained the relation through the analogy of soul and body (śarīra-śarīrin): selves and the world are the body of God, wholly dependent on and inseparable from him, controlled by him and existing for his purposes, as a body is to its soul. Brahman is thus one, but qualified by its real modes.\n\nAgainst Śaṅkara's Advaita, Rāmānuja argued that a brahman without qualities could not be known or even spoken of, and that the doctrine of ignorance is incoherent: ignorance requires an individual who is ignorant, so individuality cannot itself be an illusion produced by ignorance. Scripture, read correctly, teaches the reality of the world and of a personal God.\n\nLiberation comes through bhakti, loving devotion, practiced as a discipline that combines meditation with dutiful action offered to God, and through God's grace; in the Śrīvaiṣṇava tradition prapatti, complete surrender to God, became central, and later schools divided over how far grace depends on human effort. In liberation the self keeps its individuality, enjoying communion with God as a distinct part of his body. Rāmānuja's theology shaped devotional Hinduism and the later theistic schools of Vedānta.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/ramanuja/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["farabi", "al-Fārābī", "", "c. 870–950", "Called the “Second Teacher” after Aristotle; philosopher of the virtuous city.", ["The Virtuous City", "Enumeration of the Sciences"], ["plato", "aristotle"], { x: "Abū Naṣr al-Fārābī was born around 870 CE, probably in Fārāb in Central Asia; whether his family was Turkic or Persian is debated, and recent research favors Persian origin. He spent most of his working life in Baghdad, where he studied logic with Christian scholars of the Aristotelian tradition, moved to Syria in 943, and died in Damascus around 950. Later philosophers called him the Second Teacher, after Aristotle, the First.\n\nHis Enumeration of the Sciences classified knowledge into language, logic, mathematics, physics, metaphysics, and ethics and politics. Logic was his special field. He commented on the whole Organon, which he took to include the Rhetoric and Poetics, distinguished the universal rules of logic from the particular rules of the grammar of any language, and treated future contingents and their bearing on divine foreknowledge and human freedom. His Great Book of Music, which combined mathematical theory with attention to the ear and to performance, is the most important musical treatise of medieval Islam.\n\nHis cosmology joined Aristotle to Neoplatonism. From the First Cause, God, a series of intellects emanates, each associated with a celestial sphere, down to the active intellect, which governs the sublunary world. Human beings realize their perfection when their intellect, actualized by the active intellect, comes to know the intelligibles, and in this lies true happiness.\n\nThe Opinions of the People of the Virtuous City and The Political Regime place this metaphysics at the base of political philosophy. The virtuous city is ordered like the cosmos and the healthy body, under a ruler who is a philosopher and, in his highest form, a prophet, whose perfected imagination translates philosophical truths into the images and symbols of religion for those who cannot grasp them by demonstration. Al-Fārābī described the imperfect cities, ignorant, wicked and errant, by the false goods they pursue, such as wealth, pleasure, honor and domination. Religion, on this view, is an imitation of philosophy.\n\nScholars disagree about whether this emanationist framework expresses his own views or is rhetorical, and his authorship of The Harmonization of the Opinions of the Two Sages, Plato and Aristotle, is disputed. His logic and philosophy shaped Ibn Sīnā, who said that he understood Aristotle's Metaphysics only after reading al-Fārābī, and the Andalusian philosophers and Maimonides.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-farabi/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-farabi-soc-rel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “al-Farabi's Philosophy of Society and Religion”" }] }],
  ["ibnsina", "Ibn Sīnā (Avicenna)", "Ibn Sīnā", "980–1037", "Persian philosopher and physician who distinguished essence from existence.", ["The Book of Healing", "The Canon of Medicine"], ["aristotle", "farabi"], { x: "Ibn Sīnā, known in Latin as Avicenna, was born around 970 or 980 in Afshana, near Bukhara, in Central Asia, and was a prodigy who mastered logic, mathematics, medicine and philosophy in his youth, with access to the library of the Samanid rulers. After the fall of the Samanids in 999 he moved from city to city across Iran, serving local rulers as physician and political adviser, in Gurganj, Jurjan, Rayy, Hamadan and Isfahan. He died in Hamadan in 1037. His autobiography, completed by his student al-Jūzjānī, tells that he read Aristotle's Metaphysics forty times without understanding it until he found a small book by al-Fārābī.\n\nHis major philosophical works are The Cure (al-Shifāʾ), a vast encyclopedia of logic, natural philosophy, mathematics and metaphysics, its shorter version The Salvation (al-Najāt), and the later Pointers and Reminders (al-Ishārāt wa-l-tanbīhāt). His Canon of Medicine was used as a textbook in European universities until the seventeenth century.\n\nIbn Sīnā built a unified system from Aristotelian, Neoplatonic and Ptolemaic elements. He distinguished the essence of a thing, what it is, from its existence, that it is: considering the essence of a horse does not tell us whether any horse exists, so existence must come to an essence from outside. Every contingent thing therefore depends on a cause for its existence, and the chain of causes must end in a Necessary Existent, a being whose essence is to exist, which is God. From God the world emanates through a hierarchy of intellects, the last of which, the active intellect, gives forms to the sublunary world and intelligibles to human minds.\n\nIn psychology he described a set of internal senses and argued for the immateriality of the rational soul with the thought experiment of the flying man: a person created in mid-air, with eyes covered, limbs apart and touching nothing, would still be aware of his own existence while unaware of having a body, so the self is something other than the body. He explained prophecy as a natural perfection of the rational soul.\n\nAl-Ghazālī's Incoherence of the Philosophers attacked his doctrines of the eternity of the world, God's knowledge of particulars and the soul's afterlife, and Ibn Rushd later defended a more strictly Aristotelian philosophy against both. Latin translations from the twelfth century made him a major influence on medieval Christian philosophy, including Aquinas and Duns Scotus, and his system remained the foundation of philosophy in the eastern Islamic world for centuries.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-sina/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/self-consciousness/#AnciMediDiscSelfCons", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Self-Consciousness” (the flying man)" }] }],
  ["ghazali", "al-Ghazālī", "", "1058–1111", "Theologian who attacked the philosophers on causation and the eternity of the world.", ["The Incoherence of the Philosophers (1095)", "Deliverance from Error"], [], { x: "Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī was born around 1056 near Ṭūs, in northeastern Iran, and studied with the leading Ashʿarite theologian al-Juwaynī at Nishapur. In 1091 the vizier Niẓām al-Mulk appointed him to teach at the Niẓāmiyya madrasa in Baghdad, the most prestigious post in the Sunni world. In 1095, in a personal crisis, he left the post, turned to Sufi practice and spent years in Damascus, Jerusalem and on pilgrimage. He later returned to teaching in Nishapur and died in Ṭūs in 1111. His autobiographical Deliverance from Error describes his search for certainty among theologians, philosophers, Ismāʿīlīs and Sufis.\n\nHe studied falsafa, the Aristotelian philosophy of al-Fārābī and Ibn Sīnā, closely. His Doctrines of the Philosophers gave a faithful account of Ibn Sīnā's system; translated into Latin and Hebrew, it led many medieval Europeans to take him for a follower of Avicenna.\n\nThe Incoherence of the Philosophers (Tahāfut al-falāsifa) examined twenty of the philosophers' teachings and argued that they had failed to demonstrate them. Three it condemned as unbelief: that the world has existed from eternity, that God knows particulars only in a universal way, and that there is no bodily resurrection. Al-Ghazālī accepted Aristotelian logic and demonstration, which he held could never truly contradict revelation, and he helped make logic a standard part of Islamic theology and law.\n\nThe seventeenth discussion of the Incoherence denied that the connection between what we call a cause and its effect is necessary. When cotton is brought into contact with fire, we observe that it burns, but we never observe that the fire makes it burn; God could create the burning without the fire, or withhold it. The argument defended the possibility of miracles and God's freedom. Whether al-Ghazālī held that God creates every event directly, a view called occasionalism, or allowed God to act through secondary causes, remains disputed.\n\nHis largest work, The Revival of the Religious Sciences (Iḥyāʾ ʿulūm al-dīn), joined Islamic law and worship to an ethics of the inner life, drawing on Sufism and on philosophical ethics, so that outward acts serve the transformation of the heart. Ibn Rushd answered him in The Incoherence of the Incoherence. Al-Ghazālī's rule for reconciling reason and revelation was widely accepted by later Muslim theologians, and he is among the most influential figures in Islamic thought.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["ibnrushd", "Ibn Rushd (Averroes)", "Ibn Rushd", "1126–1198", "Andalusian commentator on Aristotle who defended philosophy against al-Ghazālī.", ["The Incoherence of the Incoherence", "Decisive Treatise"], ["aristotle"], { x: "Abū al-Walīd Ibn Rushd, known in Latin as Averroes, was born in Córdoba in 1126 into a family of distinguished judges. He trained in law, medicine and philosophy, served the Almohad rulers of al-Andalus and North Africa as a judge and as chief physician, and at the request of the caliph Abū Yaʿqūb Yūsuf undertook to explain the works of Aristotle. Late in life he fell out of favor, was exiled for a time to Lucena, and he died in Marrakesh in 1198.\n\nHis commentaries on Aristotle came in three forms, short, middle and long, the long commentaries reproducing the text and explaining it line by line. They were so thorough that Latin scholars called him simply the Commentator. His aim was to recover Aristotle's own thought, purged of the Neoplatonic elements that al-Fārābī and Ibn Sīnā had mixed into it.\n\nThe Decisive Treatise argued that Islamic law obliges those capable of it to study philosophy, since the Qur'an commands reflection on creation, and that demonstrative truth cannot conflict with revealed truth. Where the apparent meaning of scripture seems to conflict with demonstration, it must be interpreted, though such interpretations should not be taught to the public. People reach truth in different ways, by demonstration, by dialectic or by rhetoric, and scripture speaks to all of them.\n\nThe Incoherence of the Incoherence (Tahāfut al-tahāfut) answered al-Ghazālī point by point. Ibn Rushd defended the eternity of the world and the reality of natural causes, arguing that to deny necessary connections between causes and effects would destroy the possibility of knowledge. His most controversial doctrine, the unity of the material intellect, held that there is a single intellect shared by all human beings, which seemed to deny the personal immortality of the soul.\n\nIn the Islamic world his works continued to be read, but his greatest influence was in Europe. Translated into Latin and Hebrew, his commentaries shaped university teaching of Aristotle, and Latin Averroism, associated with Siger of Brabant, provoked the Paris condemnations of 1270 and 1277. In Jewish philosophy, through Hebrew translations, his commentaries became the standard way of reading Aristotle.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-rushd/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Andrea_di_bonaiuto,_apotesosi_di_san_tommaso_d'aquino,_11_averroè.jpg", alt: "Fresco detail of Ibn Rushd in a turban", cap: "Ibn Rushd, a detail of Andrea di Bonaiuto's fresco of the triumph of Thomas Aquinas, 1366–67 (Santa Maria Novella, Florence). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["anselm", "Anselm of Canterbury", "Anselm", "1033–1109", "Archbishop of Canterbury and author of the ontological argument.", ["Monologion", "Proslogion (1077–78)"], ["augustine"], { x: "Anselm was born in 1033 near Aosta, on the borders of Burgundy and Lombardy. After years of travel he reached Normandy and in 1060 entered the Benedictine abbey of Bec, where he became prior in 1063 and abbot in 1078, and made the abbey a major center of learning. In 1093 he was enthroned as Archbishop of Canterbury. His insistence on the Church's independence from royal control brought him into conflict with William Rufus and Henry I, and twice into exile. He died in 1109.\n\nHis motto, faith seeking understanding, names his method. Faith, for Anselm, is a loving commitment of the will to God, and reason works within it to understand what is believed. Yet his arguments were meant to persuade even an unbeliever by reason alone. The Monologion (1075-76) argues from the different degrees of goodness and being in things to one supremely good and supremely great being through which everything else is good and exists.\n\nThe Proslogion (1077-78) offers a single argument from the idea of God as that than which nothing greater can be thought. Even the fool who denies God understands this idea; but a being that existed only in the understanding could be thought of as existing in reality as well, which would be greater; so that than which nothing greater can be thought must exist in reality. Kant later called arguments of this kind ontological.\n\nThe monk Gaunilo replied on the fool's behalf that the same reasoning would prove the existence of the most excellent island imaginable. Anselm's reply did not take up the island directly, and interpreters still disagree about whether he had a good answer to it. The same method, Anselm held, yields God's other attributes: omnipotence, justice, mercy, eternity and simplicity, since a being lacking any of them would be less than the greatest conceivable.\n\nAnselm defined truth, in statements, wills and things alike, as rightness: a thing is true when it does what it is for. Freedom of choice is the power to keep rightness of will for its own sake, a freedom God and the good angels have though they cannot sin. In Cur Deus Homo (1095-98) he argued that human sin is a debt to God's honor that only one who is both fully God and fully human could repay, and that this explains why God became man.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anselm/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Anselm_of_Canterbury's_\"Cur_Deus_Homo\"_mid_12th_century_manuscript.jpg", alt: "Page of a medieval manuscript of Anselm's Cur Deus Homo", cap: "A mid-twelfth-century manuscript of Anselm's Cur Deus Homo. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["aquinas", "Thomas Aquinas", "Aquinas", "1225–1274", "Dominican who joined Aristotle's philosophy with Christian theology.", ["Summa Theologiae", "On Being and Essence"], ["aristotle", "augustine", "ibnsina", "ibnrushd"], { x: "Thomas Aquinas was born around 1225 at Roccasecca, near Aquino, in southern Italy, into a noble family, and educated at the abbey of Monte Cassino and the University of Naples. Against his family's wishes he joined the new Dominican order; his brothers held him captive for about a year to dissuade him. He studied under Albert the Great in Paris and Cologne, taught theology twice as a master at the University of Paris, and spent much of his career in Italy. He died at Fossanova in 1274, before he was fifty, and was canonized in 1323.\n\nHis works include the Summa contra Gentiles, the Summa theologiae, which he left unfinished, disputed questions, commentaries on Aristotle and on the Bible, and short treatises such as On Being and Essence. Their aim was to show that reason and Christian faith form a single truth: natural reason, working from the senses, can prove that God exists and know much about God, while some truths, such as the Trinity and the Incarnation, are known only by revelation, though they do not contradict reason.\n\nThe Summa theologiae offers five ways of proving God's existence, from motion, efficient causation, contingency, degrees of perfection and the order of nature. Since we know God only through his effects, we know more of what God is not than of what he is, and words such as “good” or “wise” apply to God by analogy. Aquinas held that philosophy cannot demonstrate whether the world had a beginning, which must be accepted on faith. God, whose essence is his existence, is pure being, while in every creature essence and existence are distinct.\n\nFollowing Aristotle, Aquinas held that each substance is a composite of matter and a single substantial form, and that the soul is the form of the human body; the intellect, however, is immaterial and survives death, and the body will be restored at the resurrection. Knowledge begins in the senses, from which the agent intellect abstracts universal concepts, and he argued against the Averroist doctrine of a single intellect for all humans.\n\nHis ethics is eudaimonist: every human act aims at happiness, which consists ultimately in the vision of God. Natural law is the rational creature's participation in God's eternal law, and its first precept is that good is to be done and pursued and evil avoided. The moral life is formed by the cardinal virtues, the theological virtues of faith, hope and charity, and grace. Some of his positions were condemned at Paris and Oxford in 1277, but he was later named a Doctor of the Church, and Pope Leo XIII's call in 1879 for renewed study of his thought launched modern Thomism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aquinas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "St-thomas-aquinas.jpg", alt: "Painting of Thomas Aquinas holding a book and a church", cap: "Carlo Crivelli, Saint Thomas Aquinas, 1476 (National Gallery, London). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["ockham", "William of Ockham", "Ockham", "c. 1287–1347", "Franciscan logician and nominalist; “Ockham's razor” bears his name.", ["Summa Logicae"], ["aristotle"], { x: "William of Ockham was born around 1287 in Ockham, Surrey, and joined the Franciscan order. He studied and taught at Oxford and in London but never became a regent master, which earned him the title the Venerable Inceptor. In 1324 he was summoned to the papal court at Avignon to answer charges of heresy in his writings. There he sided with the Franciscan minister general Michael of Cesena against Pope John XXII in the dispute over apostolic poverty, and in 1328 the two fled to the protection of the emperor Ludwig of Bavaria. Excommunicated, Ockham spent the rest of his life in Munich writing on politics, and died in 1347.\n\nHis Summa of Logic set out a theory of signs and of mental language. Thought itself is a language: concepts are natural signs of things, and spoken and written words signify conventionally by being subordinated to concepts. His theory of supposition explained how terms stand for things in propositions.\n\nOckham was a nominalist. Only individual things exist; there are no universal natures in things, and universals are concepts, signs that apply to many individuals. He reduced Aristotle's ten categories to two kinds of real things, substances and qualities, treating the other categories as ways of speaking about them. He is known for the principle of parsimony called Ockham's razor, that plurality should not be posited without necessity; the familiar wording is a later formulation, though he used the principle constantly.\n\nIn the theory of knowledge he rejected the received doctrine of species, likenesses that pass from objects to the senses and intellect, and distinguished intuitive cognition, by which we know that a thing exists and is present, from abstractive cognition, which abstracts from existence. He emphasized God's absolute power: God can do anything that does not involve a contradiction, including producing in us an intuitive cognition of something that does not exist.\n\nHis ethics gives a central place to the will: acts gain moral worth from the intention with which they are done, and divine command defines obligation, though right reason also guides action. In his political works he argued that a pope can err and even fall into heresy, that spiritual and temporal power are distinct and independent, and that property and rights belong to people by natural law, ideas that later contributed to theories of natural rights.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ockham/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "William_of_Ockham_-_Logica_1341.jpg", alt: "Pen sketch of a friar in a manuscript margin", cap: "Sketch labelled “frater Occham iste” in a 1341 manuscript of his logic. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["bodin", "Jean Bodin", "Bodin", "1530–1596", "French jurist who gave the first systematic theory of sovereignty.", ["Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576)"], [], { x: "Jean Bodin was born near Angers around 1530, the son of a prosperous tailor. He entered the Carmelite order as a young man, studied philosophy in Paris and law at Toulouse, and went on to a career as a lawyer, magistrate and royal servant during the French Wars of Religion. At the Estates General of Blois in 1576 he defended the interests of the Third Estate. He joined the Catholic League in 1590 and died of plague at Laon in 1596.\n\nHis Method for the Easy Comprehension of History (1566) set out a method for universal history and tied the study of history to law and government. In his reply to Malestroit (1568) he argued that the influx of gold and silver was the main cause of the rising prices of his time, an early statement of the link between the quantity of money and the level of prices, and he opposed fixing the value of money by royal decree.\n\nHis main work, the Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576), gave the first systematic theory of sovereignty, which he defined as the absolute and perpetual power of a commonwealth. Sovereignty covers both internal rule and external relations such as war. The sovereign is absolute in the sense of being free from human positive law, including his own, yet remains bound by divine and natural law. Bodin classified states by who holds sovereignty, distinguished the form of a state from the way its offices are distributed, and separated despotism, which he allowed could be legitimate, from tyranny, which never is.\n\nBodin remained a Catholic while criticizing clerical abuses. He defended freedom of conscience and favored a broad natural religion, but treated toleration of public worship as a temporary means to restore civil peace and eventually reunite the kingdom in one faith. His treatise on witchcraft, On the Demon-Mania of Witches (1581), called for harsh prosecution and drew criticism even in his own day.\n\nThe Colloquium of the Seven, a dialogue on religion among seven speakers of different faiths published long after his death, is attributed to him, though his authorship is disputed. Later writers often read Bodin as the theorist of royal absolutism, and the Stanford Encyclopedia argues that this reading, shaped by the nineteenth-century concept of absolutism, misrepresents him.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/bodin/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Methodus_Jean_Bodin_1566.png", alt: "Title page of Bodin's Methodus, printed in 1566", cap: "Title page of Bodin's Methodus ad facilem historiarum cognitionem (1566). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["bacon", "Francis Bacon", "Bacon", "1561–1626", "English statesman who called for an experimental method in natural philosophy.", ["The Advancement of Learning (1605)", "Novum Organum (1620)"], [], { x: "Francis Bacon was born in London in 1561. He was a lawyer, a member of Parliament for decades, and a statesman who rose to Lord Chancellor in 1618. In 1621 Parliament impeached him for accepting bribes; he confessed, was briefly imprisoned, and withdrew from public life. He spent his last years writing and died in 1626.\n\nHis philosophical project was a Great Instauration, a renewal of all knowledge, planned in six parts. Knowledge was to be practical and beneficial, aimed at improving human life, and he held that human knowledge and human power coincide, since nature can be commanded only by obeying it. He blamed the scholastic philosophy of the universities, the alchemists and the reverence for ancient authorities for the stagnation of learning, and set out his program in The Advancement of Learning (1605) and its expanded Latin version (1623).\n\nThe Novum Organum (1620), the “new instrument” meant to replace Aristotle's logical works, begins with the sources of error that block knowledge, which Bacon called idols. The idols of the tribe arise from human nature in general, those of the cave from an individual's temperament and upbringing, those of the marketplace from the misleading use of words, and those of the theater from the dogmas of philosophical systems.\n\nIn place of the syllogism, which argues down from general axioms, and of simple enumeration of cases, Bacon proposed an eliminative induction. The investigator draws up tables of instances in which a phenomenon such as heat is present, instances in which it is absent, and instances in which it varies in degree, then excludes candidate explanations until the true form of the phenomenon remains. Carefully compiled natural histories were to supply the material for this work.\n\nHis utopian fragment New Atlantis, published after his death, imagined a society organized around a research institution. Bacon's ideas inspired the founders of the Royal Society in 1660 and early English experimental philosophy, and he has been regarded as a founder of empiricism, though his reputation declined in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries before scholars again gave him a central place in early modern thought.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/francis-bacon/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Francis_Bacon,_Viscount_St_Alban_from_NPG_(2).jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Francis Bacon", cap: "Portrait of Francis Bacon (National Portrait Gallery, London). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["hobbes", "Thomas Hobbes", "Hobbes", "1588–1679", "English materialist and theorist of the social contract and absolute sovereignty.", ["De Cive (1642)", "Leviathan (1651)"], [], { x: "Thomas Hobbes was born in Malmesbury, Wiltshire, in 1588 and studied at Oxford. He spent most of his life in the service of the Cavendish family, first as tutor, which gave him access to books, travel and other thinkers; he briefly worked as Francis Bacon's secretary and in Paris joined the circle of Marin Mersenne. Associated with the royalists, he lived in exile in Paris from 1640 to 1651, during the English civil wars. He died in 1679.\n\nHobbes was a thoroughgoing materialist. Human beings, minds included, are bodies in motion; sensation is a chain of motions from the object through the sense organs to the brain and heart, and talk of immaterial substances is meaningless. His works include a translation of Thucydides (1629), The Elements of Law (circulated 1640), De Cive (1642), Leviathan (1651) and De Corpore (1655). Reasoning, he held, is a kind of computation with words.\n\nHis political philosophy answers the experience of civil war. In a state of nature, without any common power to settle disputes, people of roughly equal strength, driven by desire for self-preservation, competition, mistrust and concern for reputation, would live in constant fear of violent death, without industry, culture or knowledge, in a war of every man against every man. Each has a right of nature to do whatever he judges necessary to preserve himself, which in practice becomes a right to everything.\n\nThe laws of nature are precepts of reason that direct people to seek peace when it can be had safely and to lay down part of that right. Sovereignty arises when people covenant with one another to obey a common power, or submit to a conqueror for protection. Hobbes argued that the sovereign's authority over legislation, judgment, war, taxation and doctrine must be undivided and unlimited, since divided or limited power invites disputes that lead back to war. Subjects nonetheless keep the right to defend their own lives, and the obligation to obey lasts only as long as the sovereign can protect them.\n\nHis religious views are disputed, read by some as atheism in disguise and by others as a form of Christianity; he called God a corporeal spirit and was skeptical of claims based on visions and miracles. Contemporaries such as Bishop Bramhall attacked his determinism and his politics. Interpreters still debate whether his moral theory is subjectivist, a kind of divine command theory or something else, and game theorists have modeled his state of nature as a problem of collective action.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hobbes-moral/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hobbes's Moral and Political Philosophy”" }], img: { file: "Thomas_Hobbes_(portrait).jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Thomas Hobbes in old age", cap: "John Michael Wright, portrait of Hobbes (National Portrait Gallery, London). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["descartes", "René Descartes", "Descartes", "1596–1650", "French mathematician and philosopher; modern rationalism and mind-body dualism.", ["Discourse on Method (1637)", "Meditations on First Philosophy (1641)", "Principles of Philosophy (1644)"], [], { x: "Descartes worked across mathematics, natural philosophy and metaphysics in seventeenth-century Europe. Educated at La Flèche, he developed a program for investigating nature through mathematical reasoning and mechanical explanation. The Discourse on Method (1637) introduced his method alongside scientific essays; the Meditations (1641) and Principles of Philosophy (1644) developed his metaphysical arguments.\n\nIn the First Meditation, Descartes tests beliefs by considering sensory error, dreams and the possibility of radical deception. The Second Meditation arrives at the certainty of the thinking subject's existence whenever it thinks. The later Meditations seek to establish the existence of God and a basis for trusting clear and distinct perception. The reasoning is presented as a sequence of meditative exercises, and Descartes published objections from contemporaries with his replies.\n\nDescartes distinguishes thought from extension as the principal attributes of mind and body respectively. His account of the human being nevertheless treats mind and body as closely united, particularly in sensation, emotion and action. Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia challenged him to explain how an unextended mind could affect a body, a difficulty that continues to shape discussion of Cartesian dualism.\n\nHis theory of ideas asks how mental representations relate to objects and how intellectual knowledge can be justified. In natural philosophy he proposed mechanical explanations of bodily processes and physical phenomena. His work in analytic geometry and optics belongs to the same broad effort to make inquiry systematic, although particular scientific hypotheses were later rejected.\n\nSpinoza and Leibniz each proposed their own answer to the mind-body problem he left behind. Scholars continue to debate the Cartesian circle, raised by Arnauld in the Objections, the role of divine guarantees in knowledge, and the relation between his metaphysical dualism and his account of embodied human experience. Useful primary passages include Meditations I–VI, Principles I and his correspondence with Elisabeth.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Frans_Hals_-_Portret_van_René_Descartes.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of René Descartes", cap: "After Frans Hals, portrait of Descartes (Louvre). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["elisabeth", "Elisabeth of Bohemia", "Elisabeth of Bohemia", "1618–1680", "Princess whose letters to Descartes pressed the problem of mind-body interaction.", ["Correspondence with Descartes (1643–49)"], ["descartes"], { x: "Elisabeth of Bohemia was born in 1618, the eldest daughter of Frederick V, Elector Palatine, and Elizabeth Stuart. When her father lost the throne of Bohemia in 1620, an event linked to the outbreak of the Thirty Years' War, the family went into exile in The Hague. She was educated in languages, logic, mathematics, politics, philosophy and the sciences. In 1660 she entered the Lutheran convent at Herford, became its abbess in 1667, managed its lands, and welcomed persecuted religious groups such as Labadists and Quakers. She died at Herford in 1680.\n\nIn 1643 she opened a correspondence with Descartes that lasted until his death. Her first letters asked how an immaterial soul, which on his account is only a thinking thing, could move a body, given that motion seemed to require contact and extension. She rejected his comparison with the scholastic notion of heaviness and pressed the question whether thought is wholly independent of the body. This exchange is now one of the classic statements of the problem of mind-body interaction.\n\nThe letters ranged far beyond that problem. She solved a geometrical problem of Apollonius that Descartes set her, and criticized his accounts of magnetism and of the weight of mercury in the Principles of Philosophy, which he dedicated to her in 1644. In 1645 and 1646 they read Seneca's On the Happy Life together, and she objected that his account of virtue and happiness neglected the role of fortune and the difficulty of measuring goods.\n\nHer request for a definition of the passions led Descartes to write The Passions of the Soul (1649). They also discussed Machiavelli's Prince and the problem of acting well as a ruler on incomplete information. Her other correspondents included John Pell, Constantijn Huygens, Anna Maria van Schurman, Quakers such as William Penn and Robert Barclay, and briefly Malebranche.\n\nHer own letters survived as eighteenth-century copies and were published in the nineteenth century. Scholars now read them as showing critical acuity and positive views of her own, though they disagree about what those views were: she has been read as a naturalistic dualist, as a Cartesian in natural philosophy but not in practical life, and as a thinker of practical government.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "1636_Elisabeth_of_Bohemia.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Elisabeth of Bohemia", cap: "Gerard van Honthorst, portrait of Elisabeth, 1636. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["spinoza", "Baruch Spinoza", "Spinoza", "1632–1677", "Dutch rationalist of one substance, God or Nature, and of freedom through understanding.", ["Theological-Political Treatise (1670)", "Ethics (1677)"], ["descartes", "hobbes"], { x: "Spinoza lived in the Dutch Republic and wrote during a period of intense religious and political dispute. Born into Amsterdam's Portuguese-Jewish community, he left his studies at seventeen to help run the family's importing business, and in 1656 the congregation placed him under a ban (herem); afterwards he supported himself partly by grinding lenses. The Theological-Political Treatise appeared anonymously in 1670; the Ethics was published after his death in 1677.\n\nThe Ethics presents its arguments through definitions, axioms, propositions and demonstrations. Spinoza argues that there is one substance, God or Nature, which exists and is conceived through itself. Finite things are modes of this substance, understood through its attributes; human beings know thought and extension. This metaphysics informs his rejection of explanations that assign purposes to nature as a whole.\n\nSpinoza describes the human mind as the idea of the body and develops an account of knowledge that distinguishes imagination (or opinion), reason and intuitive knowledge. He examines how inadequate ideas and external encounters contribute to passive affects. The striving of each thing to persevere in its being, or conatus, plays a central role in his explanation of desire and agency.\n\nIn the later parts of the Ethics, freedom is connected with understanding necessity and developing active affects. Spinoza examines the passions as natural phenomena subject to explanation and asks how people can increase their power to act. His account of intellectual love of God has prompted extensive disagreement about the nature of blessedness and the status of individual minds.\n\nThe Theological-Political Treatise advocates freedom of philosophizing and develops a historical approach to scriptural interpretation. His unfinished Political Treatise continues his investigation of collective power and institutions. Spinoza's reception ranges across Enlightenment debates, German idealism and contemporary philosophy; interpreters dispute the details of his necessitarianism, mind-body parallelism and political commitments.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch_Spinoza", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Baruch Spinoza” (lens grinding)" }], img: { file: "Spinoza.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Baruch Spinoza", cap: "Portrait of Spinoza, about 1665 (Herzog August Bibliothek, Wolfenbüttel). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["locke", "John Locke", "Locke", "1632–1704", "English empiricist and liberal theorist of natural rights and government by consent.", ["An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689)", "Two Treatises of Government (1689)"], ["descartes"], { x: "John Locke was born in 1632 and studied and taught at Oxford, where he also worked in medicine. From 1667 he served Lord Ashley, later Earl of Shaftesbury, as physician, secretary and political associate, and after the Rye House Plot he went into exile in Holland. He returned to England in 1689, after the Glorious Revolution, served on the Board of Trade, and died in 1704.\n\nAn Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689) examines the origin, extent and limits of human knowledge. Locke rejected innate ideas and argued that all ideas come from experience, through sensation of external things or reflection on the mind's own operations. Simple ideas combine into complex ones. He distinguished primary qualities, such as shape and motion, which resemble our ideas of them, from secondary qualities, such as color and taste, which are powers in objects to produce ideas in us. He held that our idea of substance is obscure, and that assent should be proportioned to the evidence.\n\nIn the second edition (1694) Locke added a chapter on identity. He separated the human being, an animal, from the person, a thinking being that can consider itself the same across times and is accountable for its actions, and held that personal identity consists in sameness of consciousness, reaching back as far as one's consciousness of past actions extends. His example of a prince's soul passing into a cobbler's body shows the person going where consciousness goes. Butler objected that the account is circular, and Reid that it violates the logic of identity.\n\nThe Two Treatises of Government (1689) refuted Robert Filmer's patriarchal defense of absolute monarchy. In the state of nature people are bound by a natural law, knowable by reason, that commands the preservation of humankind, and each may punish its violation. Property arises from labor, limited by provisos against spoilage and in favor of leaving enough and as good for others. People leave the state of nature by consent to escape being judges in their own cases; government is limited to the public good, and when it attacks the people's rights they may remove it.\n\nIn A Letter Concerning Toleration he argued that the magistrate has no commission to care for souls and that force cannot produce belief, so religion should be left free of coercion, though he excluded some groups from toleration. Scholars debate how his account of property relates to the dispossession of Native Americans and to his involvement in colonial administration. His empiricism shaped Berkeley and Hume, and his political theory the liberal and revolutionary traditions.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke-political/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Locke's Political Philosophy”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/locke-personal-identity/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Locke on Personal Identity”" }], img: { file: "JohnLocke.png", alt: "Painted portrait of John Locke", cap: "Godfrey Kneller, portrait of Locke, 1697 (Hermitage). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["malebranche", "Nicolas Malebranche", "Malebranche", "1638–1715", "French Cartesian priest who defended occasionalism.", ["The Search after Truth (1674–75)"], ["descartes", "augustine"], { x: "Nicolas Malebranche was born in Paris in 1638 and died there in 1715. He studied theology at the Sorbonne, joined the Oratory in 1660 and was ordained in 1664. That year, by a traditional story, he came upon Descartes's Treatise on Man and was so moved that he spent the next decade studying Cartesian philosophy. His thought joins Descartes to Augustine.\n\nThe Search after Truth (1674-75) studies the sources of error in the senses, imagination, understanding and passions, and the method for avoiding them. Its most distinctive doctrine is the vision in God: we perceive bodies through ideas, and these ideas exist in God, so that all knowledge of the material world depends on the mind's union with the divine intellect. In later works he spoke of the ideas of bodies as an intelligible extension in God.\n\nMalebranche is the best-known defender of occasionalism, the view that God is the only true cause. A true cause, he argued, must have a necessary connection with its effect, and the only such connection is between the will of an omnipotent being and what it wills. Bodies and minds are only occasional causes, the occasions on which God acts according to general laws. A second argument draws on the Cartesian doctrine that conserving the world from moment to moment is a continuous creation.\n\nHis Treatise on Nature and Grace (1680) explained evil in the world through the simplicity of God's ways: God acts mostly by general volitions that follow simple laws, and natural evils follow from those laws as the price of a wisdom that prefers simplicity. Particular volitions are reserved mainly for miracles. The Dialogues on Metaphysics and Religion (1688) summarize his system, and a Treatise on Ethics (1684) treats virtue as loving things in proportion to their perfection.\n\nHis views drew long controversies. Antoine Arnauld attacked his theory of ideas and his theology of grace, Pierre-Sylvain Régis disputed the nature of ideas, and Leibniz objected that occasionalism turns every natural event into a perpetual miracle. His rejection of disinterested pure love also set him against the quietist François Lamy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/malebranche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Nicolas_Malebranche_-_Versailles_MV_2929.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Nicolas Malebranche", cap: "Portrait of Nicolas Malebranche in the collection of the Palace of Versailles (MV 2929). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["leibniz", "G. W. Leibniz", "Leibniz", "1646–1716", "German rationalist and co-inventor of the calculus; monads and the principle of sufficient reason.", ["Theodicy (1710)", "Monadology (1714)", "New Essays on Human Understanding (written 1704)"], ["aristotle", "descartes", "spinoza"], { x: "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was born in Leipzig in 1646 and trained in both scholastic and humanist traditions. From 1672 to 1676 he lived in Paris, where he met Christiaan Huygens and developed the calculus. He then served the court of Hanover as librarian and historian for the rest of his life, while working on law, diplomacy, theology, physics and the reunion of the churches. His last years were embittered by the dispute with Newton's followers over who had invented the calculus first. He died in 1716.\n\nLeibniz published few books; much of his philosophy lies in essays and letters, including the Discourse on Metaphysics (1686), the New System (1695), the Monadology (1714) and correspondences with Antoine Arnauld and Samuel Clarke. His reasoning rests on a set of principles: the principle of contradiction, the principle of sufficient reason (nothing is without a reason why it is so and not otherwise), the identity of indiscernibles (no two things are exactly alike), the principle that the predicate of every true proposition is contained in the concept of its subject, the principle of the best, and the principle of continuity.\n\nOn his account each individual substance has a complete concept containing everything that will ever be true of it. Only true unities are substances, and these are simple, active, mind-like beings he called monads, each perceiving the whole universe from its own point of view. Bodies are well-founded phenomena grounded in monads. Created substances do not act on one another; mind and body agree because God created each to unfold according to its own laws in a pre-established harmony.\n\nAgainst Locke, in the New Essays on Human Understanding (written 1704), he defended innate ideas and distinguished necessary truths of reason from contingent truths of fact. He held that the mind has countless small perceptions of which it is not aware. Against Newton and Clarke he argued that space and time are systems of relations among things and events, with no existence apart from them.\n\nHe offered ontological and cosmological arguments for God's existence. In the Theodicy (1710), the one philosophical book he published in his lifetime, he argued that God, choosing among infinitely many possible worlds, created the best one, and that the evils in it are part of the best possible whole, a claim Voltaire mocked in Candide. His logic and metaphysics passed through Christian Wolff into German philosophy before Kant.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/leibniz/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz,_Bernhard_Christoph_Francke.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Leibniz in a long wig", cap: "Christoph Bernhard Francke, portrait of Leibniz, about 1695. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["berkeley", "George Berkeley", "Berkeley", "1685–1753", "Irish bishop who argued that for physical things to be is to be perceived.", ["A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710)", "Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous (1713)"], ["locke", "malebranche"], { x: "George Berkeley was born near Kilkenny, Ireland, in 1685, studied at Trinity College Dublin, became a fellow, and was ordained in the Anglican Church. He spent several years trying to found a college in Bermuda, living in Rhode Island while he waited for funding that never came, and was made Bishop of Cloyne in 1734. He died in Oxford in 1753.\n\nHis major philosophical works came early. An Essay Toward a New Theory of Vision (1709) argued that distance, size and position are not seen directly: visual ideas are signs, learned by experience, of the tactile ideas that go with them. A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) and Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous (1713) set out his immaterialism.\n\nBerkeley argued that the objects we perceive, apples, trees and houses, are ideas, and that ideas exist only in being perceived: for such things, to be is to be perceived. He attacked Locke's doctrine of abstract ideas, which he thought supported the notion of matter, and argued that the materialist's picture, on which ideas resemble or are caused by mind-independent matter, cannot be made intelligible, so that the idea of matter is empty. Materialism, he held, leads to skepticism and to atheism.\n\nWhat exists are minds, active spirits, and the passive ideas they perceive. God, an infinite spirit, causes the ideas of sense in us, and the regularity of these ideas, which we call the laws of nature, distinguishes real things from dreams and illusions. Things go on existing when no human perceives them, through God's perception or will, though each version of this answer has difficulties. Berkeley presented his view as a defense of common sense, since it keeps the things we see and touch as real while removing the hidden matter behind them.\n\nLater works include De Motu (1721), which treated Newtonian forces as useful mathematical hypotheses, The Analyst (1734), a critique of the foundations of the calculus, Alciphron (1732), a defense of Christianity, The Querist on the Irish economy, and Siris (1744), which promoted tar-water as a medicine. Often misunderstood in his own time, he influenced Hume and Kant.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/berkeley/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "George_Berkeley_by_John_Smibert.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of George Berkeley", cap: "John Smibert, portrait of Berkeley. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["hume", "David Hume", "Hume", "1711–1776", "Scottish empiricist, skeptical about causation, induction and the self.", ["A Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40)", "An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748)", "Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779)"], ["locke", "berkeley"], { x: "David Hume was a Scottish philosopher, historian and essayist associated with the eighteenth-century Enlightenment. His Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40) proposed an experimental approach to the study of human understanding and conduct. He later recast major parts of that project in the Enquiry concerning Human Understanding (1748) and the Enquiry concerning the Principles of Morals (1751). He also wrote influential essays and a multivolume History of England.\n\nHume distinguishes impressions from ideas and examines how ideas are connected through resemblance, contiguity and cause and effect. He asks what experience can justify about causal necessity and future events. His discussion of induction shows why reasoning from past regularities to future outcomes cannot itself be justified by an appeal to past success without circularity.\n\nIn his account of personal identity, Hume questions whether introspection reveals a simple, enduring self beyond particular perceptions. His treatment of belief explores the role of habit and imagination in ordinary reasoning. These analyses form part of a larger project that investigates both the limits of philosophical justification and the natural operations of human cognition.\n\nHume's moral philosophy examines sentiment, sympathy, utility and the qualities people approve in themselves and others. In the second Enquiry, he organizes virtues partly through their usefulness and agreeableness. His discussions of justice, convention and political obligation connect ethics with social practices and institutions. He also wrote on aesthetic judgment, including the essay Of the Standard of Taste.\n\nHume's writings on religion consider miracles, the origins of religious belief and arguments about divine design, particularly in the posthumously published Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion. Kant wrote that remembering Hume first interrupted his “dogmatic slumber”, and Hume's arguments remain central to later debates in epistemology and moral philosophy. Scholars disagree over how his skepticism fits with his constructive science of human nature and how his later Enquiries relate to the earlier Treatise.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hume/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-hume-causality/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Kant and Hume on Causality”" }], img: { file: "David_Hume_Ramsay.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of David Hume", cap: "Allan Ramsay, portrait of Hume (Scottish National Portrait Gallery). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["rousseau", "Jean-Jacques Rousseau", "Rousseau", "1712–1778", "Genevan theorist of inequality, the general will and education.", ["Discourse on the Origin of Inequality (1755)", "The Social Contract (1762)", "Emile (1762)"], ["hobbes", "locke"], { x: "Jean-Jacques Rousseau was born in Geneva in 1712. He worked in music and joined the circle of Diderot and d'Alembert in Paris, writing for their Encyclopédie. In 1750 his Discourse on the Sciences and Arts won the prize of the Academy of Dijon with the argument that the progress of the arts and sciences had corrupted morals. When Emile and The Social Contract appeared in 1762 both were condemned, and he spent his remaining years moving between places of refuge and writing autobiography. He died in 1778, and in 1794 the revolutionaries moved his remains to the Panthéon.\n\nHis central claim is that human beings are naturally good and are corrupted by society. The Discourse on the Origin of Inequality (1755) offers a conjectural history of how humans passed from a solitary state of nature, moved by self-preservation and compassion, into society, where property and dependence produced inequality and domination.\n\nRousseau distinguished amour de soi, the natural love of oneself that seeks preservation, from amour-propre, the self-regard formed in society by comparison with others, which seeks recognition and can become a source of vanity, envy and conflict, though it can also be channeled toward good ends. Compassion, the natural reluctance to see others suffer, can be stifled by the reasoning of civilized people.\n\nThe Social Contract asks how people can unite with others and yet remain as free as before. Legitimate law, Rousseau answered, comes from the general will of the citizens as a body, directed at the common good, and freedom consists in obeying a law one has prescribed to oneself; whoever refuses to obey the general will may be “forced to be free.” Sovereignty cannot be handed over to representatives. A civil religion would support the state, with toleration for all who accept its few dogmas.\n\nEmile proposes an education that lets the child learn from guided experience before the authority of books, and Julie, or the New Heloize (1761) was among the most widely read novels of its century. His Confessions and Reveries of the Solitary Walker are landmarks of modern autobiography. He influenced Kant, Hegel, Marx and later democratic theory; critics point to his exclusion of women from political life and to the authoritarian potential of the general will.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rousseau/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_(painted_portrait).jpg", alt: "Pastel portrait of Jean-Jacques Rousseau", cap: "Maurice Quentin de La Tour, portrait of Rousseau, 1753. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["kant", "Immanuel Kant", "Kant", "1724–1804", "German philosopher whose critical philosophy set the limits of reason and grounded morality in autonomy.", ["Critique of Pure Reason (1781/1787)", "Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785)", "Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790)"], ["leibniz", "hume", "rousseau"], { x: "Immanuel Kant was born in 1724 in Königsberg, in East Prussia, into an artisan family of Pietist faith, and spent his whole life in and near the city. He studied at its university, worked as a private tutor, and from 1755 taught there as an unsalaried lecturer on subjects from logic and metaphysics to physical geography. In 1770 he became professor of logic and metaphysics. After a decade of near silence he published the Critique of Pure Reason in 1781, and over the next twenty years transformed every field of philosophy. He retired from teaching in 1796 and died in 1804.\n\nHis early work tried to reconcile Leibnizian rationalism with Newtonian science; his Universal Natural History (1755) proposed what became the nebular hypothesis of the origin of the solar system. Reading Hume, he later wrote, interrupted his dogmatic slumber.\n\nThe Critique of Pure Reason (1781, revised 1787) asks how synthetic a priori knowledge is possible: knowledge that extends beyond what our concepts contain and yet holds necessarily, independently of experience, as in mathematics and the principle that every event has a cause. Kant's answer, his Copernican revolution, holds that objects must conform to our ways of knowing. Space and time are the forms of our sensible intuition, and the understanding supplies categories, such as substance and causality, that make experience of objects possible. We therefore know things only as they appear to us, never as they are in themselves, a position called transcendental idealism.\n\nThe same limits explain why reason, seeking the unconditioned, falls into illusion when it claims knowledge of the soul, of the world as a whole, where it produces antinomies, contradictory arguments of equal force, and of God. Denying such knowledge, Kant said, made room for faith. In moral philosophy, the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) and the Critique of Practical Reason (1788) ground morality in the autonomy of the rational will. The supreme principle of morality is the categorical imperative, which commands unconditionally: act only on a maxim you can will as a universal law, and treat humanity, in yourself and others, always as an end and never merely as a means. The moral law reveals our freedom and supports rational faith in God and immortality.\n\nThe Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790) treats judgments of beauty, which are disinterested yet claim everyone's agreement, the sublime, and the purposiveness we find in organisms. Kant also wrote on enlightenment, which he defined as humanity's release from its self-incurred immaturity, on history, religion and law, and in Toward Perpetual Peace (1795) proposed a federation of free states. His philosophy is the starting point of German idealism, neo-Kantianism and much of modern philosophy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Immanuel Kant", cap: "Johann Gottlieb Becker, portrait of Kant, 1768. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["burke", "Edmund Burke", "Burke", "1729–1797", "Irish statesman, theorist of the sublime and critic of the French Revolution.", ["A Philosophical Enquiry into the Sublime and Beautiful (1757)", "Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790)"], [], { x: "Edmund Burke was born in Dublin, the son of a prosperous attorney, and studied at Trinity College Dublin before going to London to train for the bar at the Middle Temple. Writing interested him more than law. In 1765 he became private secretary to the Marquis of Rockingham and entered the House of Commons, where he sat for nearly twenty-nine years, holding office only briefly, in 1782-83. He retired in 1794 and died in 1797.\n\nHis A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful (1757) gave the two ideas different origins. The sublime arises from ideas connected with self-preservation, with pain and danger felt at a safe distance, as in vastness, power, obscurity and terror; the beautiful arises from pleasure and the social passions, in smallness, smoothness and delicacy. The book also argued that abstract words such as “liberty” or “virtue” act on us through the feelings they arouse, which gives speakers great power over hearers.\n\nIn Parliament Burke spoke on British rule in North America, India and Ireland, arguing for conciliation with the American colonies in 1774 and 1775 and attacking abuses of power by the East India Company. Thoughts on the Cause of the Present Discontents (1770) defended political parties as groups of people united by shared principles.\n\nReflections on the Revolution in France (1790) attacked the revolution for treating liberty in the abstract as the only good, cut off from the other goods with which it must be combined, and for rebuilding institutions on a theory of equality. Burke favored gradual reform that works with inherited institutions and the accumulated experience embodied in them, and he was suspicious of political reasoning that considers things apart from their circumstances. The book provoked replies from Mary Wollstonecraft and Thomas Paine, among others.\n\nHis opposition to the revolution led to a break with some of his Whig colleagues in 1791, defended in An Appeal from the New to the Old Whigs. He is often called the founder of modern conservatism, but his thought has been claimed by many political camps, and he left no lasting school in Parliament.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/burke/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "EdmundBurke1771.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Edmund Burke", cap: "Studio of Joshua Reynolds, portrait of Burke, 1771. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["bentham", "Jeremy Bentham", "Bentham", "1748–1832", "English founder of utilitarianism and designer of the Panopticon.", ["An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789)", "Panopticon (1791)"], ["hume"], { x: "Jeremy Bentham was born in London in 1748. Trained in law, he turned away from practice to the reform of law and government, and became the philosopher most closely associated with the founding of modern utilitarianism. He died in 1832, and his preserved body, the auto-icon, is kept at University College London.\n\nHe first set out utility as the guiding principle of conduct and law in A Fragment on Government (1776), an attack on William Blackstone's praise of English law. An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) opens with the claim that nature has placed humankind under two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure. The principle of utility approves or disapproves of every action by its tendency to increase or diminish the happiness of those affected.\n\nTo weigh pleasures and pains Bentham proposed a calculus with dimensions such as intensity, duration, certainty, nearness, fecundity, purity and extent, the number of people affected. He treated self-interest as a reliable general guide for legislators and argued that laws and institutions could be designed to bring individual interests into line with the general happiness. Since extra wealth adds less to a rich person's happiness than to a poor one's, utility favors some redistribution, limited by the need to protect settled expectations and property.\n\nHe designed codes of civil and penal law. Civil law aims at security, subsistence, abundance and equality; punishment is justified mainly as deterrence, and by 1809 he had concluded that no offense deserved death. The Panopticon was his design for a circular prison in which every inmate could be watched from a central tower, a design Foucault later took as the emblem of disciplinary power. He rejected natural rights as “nonsense upon stilts” and argued for representative democracy, a free press and other securities against misrule.\n\nÉtienne Dumont's French edition of his writings (1802) spread his ideas across Europe and Latin America. John Austin and John Stuart Mill were his most important followers, and his approach shaped welfare economics and cost-benefit analysis. Critics from T. H. Green to Rawls and Nozick objected that summing happiness across people ignores the distinctness of persons.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/bentham/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Jeremy Bentham", cap: "Henry William Pickersgill, portrait of Bentham (detail). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["wollstonecraft", "Mary Wollstonecraft", "Wollstonecraft", "1759–1797", "English writer who argued for women's education and rational equality.", ["A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792)"], ["locke"], { x: "Mary Wollstonecraft was born in Spitalfields, London, in 1759, the second of seven children in a family whose fortunes steadily declined. Her schooling was irregular, but she read widely in the Bible, classical philosophy and modern writers. She worked as a lady's companion, a schoolteacher and a governess, and through the circle of the Dissenting minister Richard Price met the publisher Joseph Johnson, for whom she wrote, translated and reviewed.\n\nHer early books, Thoughts on the Education of Daughters (1787) and Original Stories from Real Life (1788), concerned education. When Edmund Burke attacked the French Revolution and Price's sermon in its favor, she replied within weeks with A Vindication of the Rights of Men (1790), arguing that rights are given by God and discoverable by reason, and attacking Burke's reverence for rank, tradition and the English constitution.\n\nA Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) argued that women are educated to be frivolous and dependent, which makes them unhappy and weakens society as a whole. Women, like men, are rational beings capable of virtue, and virtue requires reason and self-command. She called for the education of both sexes together, criticized the education Rousseau prescribed for Sophie in Emile, and argued that marriage should rest on friendship, that women need economic independence and occupations of their own, and that they should have political rights, since virtue can flourish only among equals.\n\nShe went to Paris in 1792 to observe the Revolution and wrote An Historical and Moral View of the French Revolution (1794). There she lived with Gilbert Imlay, with whom she had a daughter. Her Letters Written during a Short Residence in Sweden, Norway, and Denmark (1796) gave more weight to passion and imagination and warned against the corrupting effects of commercial wealth. In 1797 she married William Godwin and died that year after the birth of their daughter, the future Mary Shelley.\n\nGodwin's memoir of her life (1798) made her a figure of scandal for much of the nineteenth century, though she was still read in America, continental Europe and Brazil. Twentieth-century feminists claimed her as the first English feminist, and since the late twentieth century scholars have studied her as an Enlightenment moral and political philosopher in her own right.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wollstonecraft/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Mary_Wollstonecraft_by_John_Opie_(c._1797).jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Mary Wollstonecraft", cap: "John Opie, portrait of Wollstonecraft, about 1797 (National Portrait Gallery, London). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["fichte", "J. G. Fichte", "Fichte", "1762–1814", "German idealist who built philosophy on the self-positing I.", ["Foundations of the Entire Science of Knowledge (1794)", "Addresses to the German Nation (1808)"], ["kant"], { x: "Johann Gottlieb Fichte was born in 1762 in Rammenau, Saxony, into a poor family of ribbon weavers; a local nobleman paid for his schooling. Reading Kant in 1790 transformed him, since Kant seemed to reconcile his moral convictions with his doubts about freedom. His Attempt at a Critique of All Revelation (1792) was published anonymously and first taken for a work of Kant's, which made him famous when his authorship became known. In 1794 he took the chair of philosophy at Jena.\n\nAt Jena he set out his system, the Wissenschaftslehre or Doctrine of Science, which he presented in many versions and saw as the completion of Kant's transcendental idealism. Its task was to reconcile freedom with necessity, to show how free and responsible agents can exist in a causally ordered world, by deriving the necessary structures of experience from the freedom of the I. He held that the choice between idealism and dogmatism cannot be settled by theory alone and depends on practical commitment.\n\nIts first principle is that the I posits itself, an act and its product at once, which Fichte called a “fact/act.” The I can posit itself only as limited, and it encounters its limit in a check (Anstoss), its original finitude, to which it responds by positing a not-I. This replaced Kant's thing in itself as the source of the given. The I's response to its limits is an endless practical striving, which is why practical reason has priority in Fichte's system.\n\nIn the Foundations of Natural Right (1796-97) Fichte argued that a person can become conscious of being a free individual only by being summoned to free action by another, which makes mutual recognition a condition of selfhood, an idea later developed by Hegel. Right concerns external freedom and its protection by a state built on contracts; ethics, set out in the System of Ethics (1798), concerns the free self-determination of the will.\n\nAn essay identifying God with the moral order of the world led to the atheism controversy of 1798-99 and to Fichte's resignation from Jena. In Berlin he wrote popular works such as The Vocation of Man and, under French occupation, the Addresses to the German Nation (1808), which shaped his later reputation as a nationalist. He was the first elected rector of the University of Berlin and died in 1814. Long treated as a stepping stone between Kant and Hegel, he has been read with renewed interest since the late twentieth century.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/johann-fichte/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Friedrich_Bury_-_Johann_Gottlieb_Fichte_1801.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Johann Gottlieb Fichte", cap: "Friedrich Bury, portrait of Fichte, 1801. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["schleiermacher", "Friedrich Schleiermacher", "Schleiermacher", "1768–1834", "German theologian who founded general hermeneutics.", ["On Religion (1799)", "Hermeneutics and Criticism (1838)"], ["kant"], { x: "Friedrich Schleiermacher was born in Breslau in 1768 and educated among the Moravian Brethren, then at the University of Halle. In Berlin he belonged to the circle of early German Romantics around Friedrich Schlegel, began a translation of Plato's dialogues that remained standard for generations, and later helped plan the University of Berlin, where he taught theology and philosophy until his death in 1834.\n\nOn Religion: Speeches to Its Cultured Despisers (1799) defended religion against its educated critics by distinguishing it from metaphysics and from morality and describing it as a sense and taste for the infinite. In his dogmatic theology, The Christian Faith (1821-22), he described the essence of piety as the feeling of absolute dependence. He is often called the father of modern liberal Protestant theology.\n\nPhilosophically he is best known for hermeneutics, the theory of interpretation. Schleiermacher made it a universal discipline, applying to any text or speech, and held that misunderstanding is the normal state, so that understanding has to be sought deliberately. Interpretation combines a grammatical side, which reads a text through the shared rules of its language, with a psychological side, which grasps the author's individuality, and moves back and forth between parts and whole. The Stanford Encyclopedia stresses how much of this he took from Herder.\n\nFollowing Herder, he held that thought is bound to language, that meaning lies in the usage of words, and that people differ deeply in how they think. His essay on translation (1813) argued that a translator should bring the reader toward the author, bending the target language toward the original even at the cost of some strangeness; it remains a landmark of translation theory.\n\nHis lectures on dialectic treated knowledge as an ideal of agreement between thought and being, systematic coherence and agreement among all thinkers. In ethics he rejected the uniformity of Kant's moral law in favor of a cosmopolitanism that values individuality. His hermeneutics was taken up by Dilthey, whose biography of him helped make him a founding figure for the human sciences, and later by Heidegger and Gadamer.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schleiermacher/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Friedrich_Schleiermacher,_by_Ernst_Hader.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of Friedrich Schleiermacher", cap: "Ernst Hader, portrait of Schleiermacher. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["hegel", "G. W. F. Hegel", "Hegel", "1770–1831", "German idealist of dialectic, spirit and the system of philosophy.", ["Phenomenology of Spirit (1807)", "Science of Logic (1812–16)", "Elements of the Philosophy of Right (1820)"], ["aristotle", "spinoza", "kant", "fichte", "schelling"], { x: "Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was born in Stuttgart in 1770 and studied theology and philosophy at the Tübingen seminary, where he shared rooms with Hölderlin and Schelling. After years as a private tutor in Bern and Frankfurt he joined Schelling at Jena in 1801, and finished the Phenomenology of Spirit there in 1807 as Napoleon's armies took the city. He then edited a newspaper in Bamberg and directed a gymnasium in Nuremberg, taught at Heidelberg from 1816, and from 1818 held the chair of philosophy in Berlin, where he became the most famous philosopher in Germany. He died in 1831, during a cholera epidemic.\n\nHis major works are the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), the Science of Logic (1812-16), the Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences (1817), which sets out his system of logic, nature and spirit, and the Elements of the Philosophy of Right (1820). His lectures on the philosophy of history, aesthetics, religion and the history of philosophy were published after his death from his and his students' notes.\n\nThe Phenomenology traces a series of shapes of consciousness, from sense-certainty, perception and understanding through self-consciousness, reason and spirit to religion and absolute knowing. Each shape, tested by its own standard, proves inadequate and passes into the next, in a movement Hegel called determinate negation and sublation (Aufhebung), which cancels a position while preserving what was true in it. In its most famous section self-consciousness discovers that it depends on recognition by another self-consciousness; the struggle between lord and bondsman shows why one-sided recognition fails, and why freedom requires mutual recognition.\n\nThe Science of Logic develops the categories of thought, from being through essence to the concept, each generating the contradictions that lead to the next. The Philosophy of Right presents freedom as realized in a sequence of institutions: abstract right, in property and contract; morality, the inner standpoint of conscience; and ethical life (Sittlichkeit), the family, civil society and the state. Its preface declares that what is rational is actual and what is actual is rational, and that philosophy, like the owl of Minerva, comprehends its time only as it ends.\n\nInterpreters disagree about whether Hegel was a metaphysician of absolute spirit, a post-Kantian philosopher of self-critical reason, or something between. After his death his followers split into Right and Left Hegelians, the latter including Feuerbach and Marx, who turned his dialectic toward materialism. Rejected by Russell and Moore at the start of analytic philosophy, he has been read with renewed interest since the later twentieth century.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel-dialectics/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hegel's Dialectics”" }], img: { file: "1831_Schlesinger_Philosoph_Georg_Friedrich_Wilhelm_Hegel_anagoria.jpg", alt: "Painted portrait of G. W. F. Hegel", cap: "Jakob Schlesinger, portrait of Hegel, 1831 (Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["schelling", "F. W. J. Schelling", "Schelling", "1775–1854", "German idealist of the philosophy of nature and of identity.", ["System of Transcendental Idealism (1800)", "Philosophical Investigations into the Essence of Human Freedom (1809)"], ["spinoza", "kant", "fichte"], { x: "Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling was born in Leonberg, near Stuttgart, in 1775 and studied at the Protestant seminary in Tübingen, where he shared rooms and ideas with Hegel and Hölderlin. At Jena, where he became a professor at twenty-three, he joined the early Romantics around the Schlegels and Novalis. He later taught in Würzburg, Munich and Erlangen, and in 1841 was called to Berlin to take Hegel's former chair. He died in 1854.\n\nHis early work took up a problem Kant had left open, how nature and freedom are related. His philosophy of nature described nature as a dynamic productivity, whose products are temporary forms in a continuous process, and treated nature as a self-organizing whole from which mind emerges. In the System of Transcendental Idealism (1800) he traced the emergence of consciousness and presented art as the medium in which what philosophy cannot state directly becomes visible.\n\nIn his philosophy of identity, from about 1801, he held that subject and object, mind and nature, are grounded in an absolute identity prior to both. Hegel's preface to the Phenomenology of Spirit (1807) was widely read as a criticism of this view, and the friendship between them ended.\n\nThe Philosophical Investigations into the Essence of Human Freedom (1809) argued that a real account of human freedom must include the freedom to do evil, and traced evil to a dark ground in God distinct from God's existence. The unfinished Ages of the World, worked on for years afterward, developed conflicting forces within nature and mind.\n\nIn his late Berlin lectures Schelling criticized Hegel for trying to derive existence from reason. He distinguished negative philosophy, which grasps what things are, from positive philosophy, which begins from the contingent fact that anything exists at all, and lectured on mythology and revelation; Kierkegaard, Engels and Bakunin were among his early listeners. Long read as a link between Fichte and Hegel, he has been credited with influence on Kierkegaard, Marx, Nietzsche, Heidegger, Adorno and the psychoanalytic idea of the unconscious, and interest in his work has revived since the 1990s.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schelling/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Friedrich_Wilhelm_Joseph_Schelling,_1848_daguerreotype_-_cropped.jpg", alt: "Daguerreotype of Schelling in old age", cap: "Daguerreotype of Schelling, 1848. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["schopenhauer", "Arthur Schopenhauer", "Schopenhauer", "1788–1860", "German philosopher of the world as will; a pessimist who drew on Kant and Indian thought.", ["The World as Will and Representation (1818)"], ["plato", "kant"], { x: "Arthur Schopenhauer was born in Danzig in 1788 into a merchant family, and was trained for business until his father's death freed him to study. At Göttingen G. E. Schulze introduced him to Plato and Kant, and in Berlin he heard Fichte and Schleiermacher. His dissertation, On the Fourfold Root of the Principle of Sufficient Reason (1813), distinguished four forms of the principle, governing causes, concepts, mathematical objects and motives, and argued that confusing them leads to errors such as the cosmological argument.\n\nHis main work, The World as Will and Representation, appeared at the end of 1818. The world as we know it is representation: objects for a subject, ordered by space, time and causality, which Schopenhauer kept from Kant's categories as the forms of all experience. He accepted Kant's distinction between appearances and things in themselves, but held that we have one point of access to the inner nature of things, our own body, which we know both as an object and from within as will.\n\nGeneralizing from this, he held that the inner nature of the whole world is will, a blind and aimless striving, of which all individual things are expressions. Since the will is endless and never satisfied, life oscillates between pain and boredom, and the world is a scene of constant conflict. This made Schopenhauer the best-known philosophical pessimist.\n\nThere are ways out of the will's grip. In aesthetic contemplation we become, for a while, a will-less subject absorbed in the Platonic Ideas, the timeless forms of things; music, which copies the will itself, is the highest art. In compassion we recognize that the inner nature of others is the same as our own, the basis of morality. Most completely, the ascetic denies the will to live altogether, a state Schopenhauer found described in Christian mystics and in Indian texts.\n\nHe read the Upanishads and the Bhagavad Gītā from 1813-14 and admired Buddhist thought. His Berlin lectures, scheduled against Hegel's, drew few students, and he lived from 1833 in Frankfurt, largely ignored until Parerga and Paralipomena (1851) brought late fame. He died in 1860. He influenced Nietzsche, Wagner, Freud, Tolstoy, Proust, Beckett and Borges, among many others.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schopenhauer/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Arthur_Schopenhauer_by_J_Schäfer,_1859b.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Arthur Schopenhauer in old age", cap: "Johann Schäfer, photograph of Schopenhauer, 1859. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["johnaustin", "John Austin", "John Austin (jurist)", "1790–1859", "English legal positivist who defined law as the command of a sovereign.", ["The Province of Jurisprudence Determined (1832)"], ["bentham"], { x: "John Austin was born in 1790 into a Suffolk merchant family, served briefly in the army, and was called to the bar in 1818, though he took few cases and left practice in 1825. A member of Bentham's circle, he was appointed the first professor of jurisprudence at the new University of London. Attendance at his lectures dwindled and he resigned in 1835. He later served on the Criminal Law Commission and as a Royal Commissioner to Malta, but poor health and perfectionism kept him from finishing the larger works he planned. He died in 1859.\n\nHis lectures were published as The Province of Jurisprudence Determined (1832). After his death his wife, Sarah Austin, a translator and writer, edited and published a fuller set of his lectures. He is regarded as a founder of analytical jurisprudence and of legal positivism in English-speaking countries.\n\nAustin's command theory holds that laws properly so called are general commands issued by a sovereign and backed by a sanction, the threat of an evil for disobedience. The sovereign is the person or body whom the bulk of a society habitually obeys and who habitually obeys no one else. Judges who make law do so as delegates, issuing the sovereign's tacit commands. Rules of etiquette, fashion and international relations he classed as positive morality, law only by analogy.\n\nHe insisted that the existence of a law is one question and its merit another: a law may exist though it is unjust, and an unjust law is still law. This separation of law and morality became the core of legal positivism. In ethics he followed Bentham's utilitarianism, read the divine will in utilitarian terms, and has been seen as an early proponent of rule utilitarianism.\n\nCritics objected that many societies have no identifiable sovereign of his kind, that the theory cannot explain how law continues when one sovereign succeeds another, and that it fails to fit rules that confer powers, such as those for making contracts and wills. H. L. A. Hart's criticism in The Concept of Law (1961) was the most influential, and Hart's own theory was built in reply to Austin's.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/austin-john/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["feuerbach", "Ludwig Feuerbach", "Feuerbach", "1804–1872", "Left Hegelian who read God as a projection of the human essence.", ["The Essence of Christianity (1841)"], ["hegel"], { x: "Ludwig Feuerbach was born in 1804 and studied theology at Heidelberg before moving to Berlin to study philosophy with Hegel. His anonymous Thoughts on Death and Immortality (1830), with its satirical verses against the Pietists, ended his hopes of an academic career, and he lived for most of his life as an independent scholar in the village of Bruckberg. He died in poverty in 1872, having joined the Social Democratic Party two years earlier.\n\nHe began as a Hegelian with pantheist leanings. After D. F. Strauss's Life of Jesus (1835-36) split the Hegelian school, Feuerbach became one of the leading Young Hegelians, distanced himself from Hegel and called for a naturalistic account of religion.\n\nThe Essence of Christianity (1841) argued that the qualities attributed to God, such as love, wisdom and justice, are the qualities of the human species, projected outward and set over against human beings as a separate being. In worshipping God, humans worship their own essence without recognizing it, and so become estranged from themselves. His slogan was that theology is anthropology: the true content of religion is human nature, and its liberation means reclaiming what has been alienated.\n\nHe turned the same criticism on Hegel, treating speculative philosophy as theology in rational dress, and proposed a new philosophy grounded in the senses, in embodiment and finitude, and in the relation between an I and a Thou. In his later Lectures on the Essence of Religion and Theogony (1857) he traced religion to the feeling of dependence on nature and to the human wish for happiness.\n\nMarx and Engels took up his account of alienation and his materialism, and Marx's Theses on Feuerbach (1845) criticized him for understanding human beings contemplatively and ignoring practical, historical activity. Feuerbach also influenced later scientific materialists, and theologians including Karl Barth engaged with his critique of religion.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ludwig-feuerbach/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Ludwig_Andreas_Feuerbach.jpg", alt: "Portrait of Ludwig Feuerbach", cap: "Portrait of Ludwig Feuerbach. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["mill", "John Stuart Mill", "Mill", "1806–1873", "English liberal and utilitarian; defender of individual liberty and women's equality.", ["A System of Logic (1843)", "On Liberty (1859)", "Utilitarianism (1861)", "The Subjection of Women (1869)"], ["bentham"], { x: "John Stuart Mill was born in London in 1806 and educated at home by his father, James Mill, in an intense program designed to make him a leader of the radical reformers around Bentham; he was reading Greek at three. At about twenty he suffered a mental crisis, recovered partly through Romantic poetry, and afterward tried to join the insights of the Enlightenment and of Romanticism. He worked for the East India Company for some thirty-five years. His long companionship with Harriet Taylor, whom he married in 1851, shaped much of his work. He sat in Parliament as a Liberal from 1865 to 1868 and died in Avignon in 1873.\n\nMill was a thoroughgoing naturalist and empiricist. In A System of Logic (1843) he argued that all genuine knowledge comes from experience, that even mathematics and geometry rest on generalizations from experience, and that the syllogism adds nothing that is not already in its premises. He set out methods for discovering causes, among them the methods of agreement and difference. He held that human actions are causally determined but that people can still shape their own characters.\n\nIn Utilitarianism (1861) he defended the principle that actions are right in proportion as they promote happiness, offered a “proof” that happiness is the only thing desirable as an end, and distinguished higher pleasures, of the intellect and feeling, from lower ones, holding that those who know both prefer the higher. Morality, he argued, concerns what people may rightly be blamed or punished for.\n\nOn Liberty (1859) argues that society may interfere with an individual's liberty only to prevent harm to others, never for the person's own good alone. Silencing an opinion does harm whether it is true, false or partly true, since even false opinions keep truth from becoming dead dogma. Individuality, the development of one's own character and judgment, is an essential part of human well-being.\n\nThe Subjection of Women (1869) argued that the legal subordination of women was wrong in itself and an obstacle to human progress, that the supposed differences between the sexes largely reflect upbringing, and that marriage as then practiced resembled servitude. In Parliament Mill proposed votes for women. His Principles of Political Economy and Considerations on Representative Government were also widely read.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mill/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "John_Stuart_Mill_by_London_Stereoscopic_Company,_c1870.jpg", alt: "Photograph of John Stuart Mill", cap: "London Stereoscopic Company, photograph of Mill, about 1870. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["kierkegaard", "Søren Kierkegaard", "Kierkegaard", "1813–1855", "Danish thinker of faith, anxiety and the single individual.", ["Either/Or (1843)", "Fear and Trembling (1843)", "The Concept of Anxiety (1844)"], ["socrates"], { x: "Søren Kierkegaard was born in Copenhagen in 1813, the son of a devout and melancholy father, and raised in a Lutheran household also shaped by the Moravians. He took a degree in theology and wrote his dissertation On the Concept of Irony with Continual Reference to Socrates, but was never ordained. In 1841 he broke off his engagement to Regine Olsen, believing he could not make her happy, and never married. Mockery by the satirical paper The Corsair in 1845-46 drove him from public life. In his last years he attacked the Danish state church for betraying New Testament Christianity. He died in 1855, at forty-two.\n\nMost of his books appeared in the 1840s, many under pseudonyms such as Johannes de silentio, Johannes Climacus and Anti-Climacus, alongside signed religious discourses and Works of Love (1847). The pseudonyms practice indirect communication: ethical and religious truths cannot simply be handed over as doctrines, and the reader has to take them up in his or her own existence. Kierkegaard later described his whole authorship as an attempt to reintroduce Christianity into Christendom.\n\nEither/Or (1843) sets the aesthetic life, devoted to the interesting and the avoidance of boredom, against the ethical life of Judge William, built on commitment, marriage and choosing oneself. Fear and Trembling (1843) examines Abraham's readiness to sacrifice Isaac and asks whether faith can involve a teleological suspension of the ethical, a relation to God that cannot be justified in terms others can share. Abraham is the knight of faith, distinguished from the knight of infinite resignation and from the tragic hero.\n\nThe Concept of Anxiety (1844) describes anxiety as the dizziness of freedom, part of the human condition. The Sickness unto Death (1849) analyzes despair as the self's failure to become itself, in forms such as the despair of possibility and the despair of necessity. Kierkegaard held that faith requires inwardness, or subjectivity, and a decision that reflection alone can never produce, the leap; in Philosophical Fragments (1844) the incarnation is the absolute paradox.\n\nHe attacked Christendom, the cultural Christianity that assumed every Dane a Christian, and the leveling force of the public and the press; recent scholarship suggests his attacks on Hegel were aimed largely at Danish Hegelians. Often called the father of existentialism, a label the Stanford Encyclopedia finds misleading, he influenced Heidegger, Jaspers, Sartre, Marcel, Levinas and Wittgenstein, as well as Kafka, Ibsen and modern theology.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Søren_Kierkegaard_(1813-1855)_-_(cropped).jpg", alt: "Sketch of Søren Kierkegaard", cap: "Unfinished sketch of Kierkegaard by his cousin Niels Christian Kierkegaard, about 1840 (Royal Library, Copenhagen). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["marx", "Karl Marx", "Marx", "1818–1883", "German critic of political economy; with Engels, founder of historical materialism.", ["Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts (1844)", "The Communist Manifesto (1848, with Engels)", "Capital, vol. 1 (1867)"], ["aristotle", "hegel", "feuerbach"], { x: "Karl Marx was born in Trier, in the Rhineland, in 1818, into a Jewish family that had converted to Lutheranism. He studied law and philosophy in Bonn and Berlin, joined the Young Hegelians, and took his doctorate with a dissertation on Democritus and Epicurus. Barred from an academic career, he edited the Rheinische Zeitung until it was suppressed, then moved to Paris, where in 1844 he began his lifelong partnership with Friedrich Engels, and to Brussels. After the revolutions of 1848 he settled in London in 1849, where he lived in poverty, researched political economy in the British Museum, helped lead the International Working Men's Association, and died in 1883.\n\nHis early writings, including the Economic and Philosophical Manuscripts of 1844, describe alienation. Under capitalism workers are estranged from the product of their labor, which belongs to another; from their productive activity, which is forced on them; from other people; and from their species-being, the free, creative, social activity that is their human nature. In the Theses on Feuerbach (1845) he wrote that philosophers have only interpreted the world in various ways, and the point is to change it.\n\nHis theory of history, worked out with Engels in The German Ideology and summarized in the preface to A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy (1859), is historical materialism. Societies are founded on their economic structure, their relations of production, which correspond to a stage in the development of their productive forces; on this base rise legal and political superstructures and forms of social consciousness. When relations of production come to fetter the growth of productive forces, an epoch of social revolution begins. The Manifesto of the Communist Party (1848) opens with the claim that the history of all society so far is the history of class struggles.\n\nCapital, whose first volume appeared in 1867, analyzed the capitalist mode of production. Commodities exchange according to the labor socially necessary to produce them, and labor power is itself a commodity, sold for a wage equal to the cost of its reproduction; the worker produces more value than this wage, and the difference, surplus value, is the source of profit, which Marx called exploitation. Commodity fetishism names the way social relations between people appear as relations between things, prices and markets. Ideology consists of widely held beliefs that serve dominant interests by stabilizing class society.\n\nMarx wrote little about the communist society he expected to follow capitalism, criticizing utopian blueprints, though in the Critique of the Gotha Program (1875) he described its higher phase by the principle: from each according to ability, to each according to need. Scholars debate whether he criticized capitalism as unjust or rejected the language of justice altogether. His thought founded a political movement and transformed economics, history, sociology and philosophy, and it remains the subject of continuing reinterpretation, from Western Marxism to analytical Marxism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marx/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Karl_Marx_001.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Karl Marx", cap: "John Jabez Edwin Mayall, photograph of Marx, 1875. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["engels", "Friedrich Engels", "Engels", "1820–1895", "Marx's collaborator; writer on the working class, the family and dialectics.", ["The Condition of the Working Class in England (1845)", "The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State (1884)"], ["hegel", "marx"], { x: "Friedrich Engels was born in 1820 in Barmen, in Prussia, into a manufacturing family whose firm also had operations in England. He completed no university degree, though while doing military service in Berlin in 1841 he sat in on lectures and joined the Young Hegelians; he wrote anonymous pamphlets defending Hegel against Schelling. He met Karl Marx in 1842 and began working closely with him in 1844.\n\nHis “Outlines of a Critique of Political Economy” (1844) helped turn Marx toward economics, and The Condition of the Working Class in England (1845), a study of the social effects of industrialization, is often considered his masterpiece. With Marx he wrote The German Ideology (1845-46, published long after) and the Manifesto of the Communist Party (1848). After the defeat of the revolutions of 1848-49 both moved to England, where Engels worked in his father's firm, partly to support the Marx family, and helped lead the International Working Men's Association founded in 1864.\n\nAnti-Dühring (1878), a polemic against the philosopher Eugen Dühring, gave the first systematic account of the outlook Marx and Engels shared, and its excerpt Socialism: Utopian and Scientific (1880) became one of the most widely read socialist texts. Engels framed the history of philosophy as a struggle between idealism and materialism, presented Marx's achievement as joining materialism with dialectics, and in the unfinished Dialectics of Nature treated nature as a historical process in which motion is the basic mode of matter.\n\nThe Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State (1884) placed kinship within the material basis of society and linked the subordination of women to the rise of private property and class society. Ludwig Feuerbach and the End of Classical German Philosophy (1886) gave his account of German idealism and first published Marx's Theses on Feuerbach. In late letters he called historical materialism a guide to study and rejected a mechanical reading of it.\n\nAfter Marx's death in 1883 he edited the second and third volumes of Capital from Marx's manuscripts. He died in 1895. Scholars still debate whether he represented Marx faithfully, whether his dialectics of nature extends Hegel's method beyond its proper reach, whether his account of history is determinist, and how far his editing of Capital reflects Marx's intentions; Lukács's criticism in 1923 was an early statement of the case.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/engels/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Friedrich_Engels_portrait_(cropped).jpg", alt: "Photograph of Friedrich Engels", cap: "Photograph of Engels. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["dilthey", "Wilhelm Dilthey", "Dilthey", "1833–1911", "German philosopher of the human sciences and of understanding (Verstehen).", ["Introduction to the Human Sciences (1883)"], ["kant", "schleiermacher"], { x: "Wilhelm Dilthey was born in 1833 in Biebrich on the Rhine and began by studying theology at Heidelberg, as his family expected, before turning to philosophy in Berlin, where two students of Schleiermacher, Trendelenburg and Boeckh, shaped him. He held his first professorship in Basel from 1867, later took the Berlin chair once held by Hegel, and died in 1911. The first volume of his Life of Schleiermacher appeared in 1870.\n\nHis Introduction to the Human Sciences (1883) called for a critique of historical reason, to do for history and the human world what Kant had done for natural science. The human sciences, such as history, philology, law, economics and psychology, differ from the natural sciences in their aim: the natural sciences explain phenomena causally through laws, while the human sciences seek to understand (verstehen) the structures and meanings of human and historical life.\n\nUnderstanding starts from lived experience (Erlebnis) and proceeds by interpreting its expressions, the gestures, words, actions, texts and institutions in which human life objectifies itself. In Ideas for a Descriptive and Analytic Psychology (1894) Dilthey argued for a psychology that describes the connected whole of mental life, against explanatory psychology that builds it up from hypothetical elements.\n\nHis essay “The Rise of Hermeneutics” (1900) made the theory of interpretation the link between philosophy and history, and his most important late work, The Formation of the Historical World in the Human Sciences (1910), analyzed the structures of historical life. Life itself, he held, is the final context of all meaning, value and purpose, and there are no unconditional values beyond it.\n\nDilthey studied worldviews as attempts to unify the meaning of life, and concluded that metaphysical systems have only relative validity, a position critics charged with historicism and relativism. He shaped Husserl, Heidegger, Cassirer, Gadamer and Ricoeur, and the distinction between explaining and understanding remains central to debates about the method of the social sciences.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dilthey/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Wilhelm_Dilthey.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Wilhelm Dilthey", cap: "Photograph of Dilthey. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["brentano", "Franz Brentano", "Brentano", "1838–1917", "Austrian philosopher who made intentionality the mark of the mental.", ["Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint (1874)"], ["aristotle"], { x: "Franz Brentano was born in 1838 in Marienberg am Rhein into a Catholic family of writers and scholars; the Romantic poets Clemens Brentano and Bettina von Arnim were his uncle and aunt. He wrote his dissertation on the several senses of being in Aristotle (1862), was ordained a priest in 1864, and taught at Würzburg. Troubled by the dogma of papal infallibility proclaimed in 1870, he left the priesthood and his professorship, and in 1874 was appointed to a chair in Vienna.\n\nThat year he published the first volume of Psychology from an Empirical Standpoint. Philosophy, he held, should follow the method of natural science, observation and description, and psychology should rest on inner perception. He distinguished genetic psychology, the experimental study of the causes of mental life, from descriptive psychology, the analysis of consciousness from the first-person point of view, a distinction Husserl later took up.\n\nHis best-known thesis is that intentionality is the mark of the mental. Every mental phenomenon contains an object within itself, in what the scholastics called intentional inexistence: in presentation something is presented, in judgment something is affirmed or denied, in love something is loved. Late in life he described intentionality as a quasi-relation, which holds even when its object does not exist. Mental phenomena fall into three classes: presentations, judgments, and phenomena of love and hate, which include emotion and will.\n\nIn The Origin of Our Knowledge of Right and Wrong (1889) he grounded ethics in correct emotion: a thing is good when love of it is correct, just as a judgment is true when a judge with evidence would make it. In his last period he held that only individual things exist, a view called reism. Marrying in 1880 cost him his Vienna professorship, since the law barred former priests from marriage, and he spent his last decades in Florence and Zurich, where he died in 1917.\n\nHe was a charismatic teacher. His students included Carl Stumpf, Anton Marty, Alexius Meinong, Christian von Ehrenfels, Kazimierz Twardowski, Tomáš Masaryk, Sigmund Freud and Edmund Husserl, and from them came phenomenology, Meinong's theory of objects, Gestalt psychology and the Lvov-Warsaw school of logic. Recent philosophers of mind have returned to his accounts of consciousness and intentionality.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/brentano/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Franz_Brentano_portrait.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Franz Brentano", cap: "Photograph of Brentano. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["peirce", "C. S. Peirce", "Peirce", "1839–1914", "American logician, founder of pragmatism and of modern semiotics.", ["“The Fixation of Belief” (1877)", "“How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878)"], ["kant"], { x: "Charles Sanders Peirce was born in Cambridge, Massachusetts, in 1839, the son of Benjamin Peirce, a Harvard professor of mathematics. From 1859 to 1891 he worked for the United States Coast Survey, mainly on geodetic and pendulum measurements, and the experience of scientific measurement and its errors shaped his rejection of determinism. He taught logic at Johns Hopkins from 1879 to 1884, where his students included John Dewey and Christine Ladd, until the post ended abruptly. He never held another academic position, and he died in poverty in Milford, Pennsylvania, in 1914.\n\nIn two papers of 1877-78, “The Fixation of Belief” and “How to Make Our Ideas Clear,” Peirce argued that inquiry begins with the irritation of doubt and aims at settled belief, compared the methods of tenacity, authority and a priori reasoning with the method of science, and stated the pragmatic maxim: our idea of anything is our idea of its conceivable practical effects. When William James and others popularized pragmatism in their own way, Peirce renamed his version “pragmaticism,” a name he hoped was ugly enough to be safe from kidnappers.\n\nHe was a fallibilist and an anti-foundationalist: any belief may turn out to be mistaken, but inquiry carried on long enough by a community of inquirers tends toward the truth. His first rule of reason was not to block the way of inquiry. He divided reasoning into deduction, induction and abduction, the inference that forms an explanatory hypothesis, and saw scientific method as a cycle of all three.\n\nPeirce made major contributions to logic, including the logic of relations, a notation for quantifiers developed independently of Frege, and the existential graphs, a two-dimensional diagrammatic logic. His theory of signs rests on a triadic relation of sign, object and interpretant, and classifies signs as icons, which resemble their objects, indices, which are causally connected to them, and symbols, which signify by convention or habit.\n\nHis three universal categories, Firstness (quality), Secondness (brute reaction) and Thirdness (law and mediation), organize much of his thought. In metaphysics he held that chance is real (tychism), that continuity is fundamental (synechism), and that universals are real. Much of his work remained unpublished in his lifetime, and the early collected editions were poorly organized; chronological editions have since made it far more accessible.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Charles_Sanders_Peirce_theb3558.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Charles Sanders Peirce", cap: "Photograph of Peirce (NOAA Photo Library). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["james", "William James", "William James", "1842–1910", "American psychologist and pragmatist of truth as what works in experience.", ["The Principles of Psychology (1890)", "The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902)", "Pragmatism (1907)"], ["peirce"], { x: "William James was born in New York City in 1842, the eldest child of the theologian Henry James Sr. and the brother of the novelist Henry James. He was educated in New York and Europe, tried painting, then turned to science, joined Louis Agassiz's expedition to Brazil in 1865, and earned a medical degree at Harvard in 1869, though he never practiced. After a period of severe depression he began teaching at Harvard in 1872, where he established one of the first psychology laboratories in America. He died in 1910.\n\nThe Principles of Psychology (1890), twelve years in the writing, became a classic. It describes consciousness as a continuous stream, with its own rhythms of resting places and transitions, and with relations and vague fringes given directly in experience. It treats habit as grounded in the nervous system and as the flywheel of society, and proposes that bodily changes come before the feeling of an emotion, a view close to one proposed independently by Carl Lange.\n\nIn “The Will to Believe” (1896) James argued that when an option is live, forced and momentous and evidence cannot settle it, we may rightly let our passional nature decide, and that some truths, such as trust within a group, can come about only if we first believe them. The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902), from his Gifford Lectures, studied individual religious experience, contrasting the healthy-minded with the sick soul and naming four marks of mystical states: ineffability, noetic quality, transiency and passivity.\n\nPragmatism (1907) presented pragmatism as a method for settling disputes by asking what practical difference each side would make, and as a theory of truth: true ideas are those we can assimilate, validate and verify, truths are made in the course of experience, and they remain answerable to existing beliefs and to facts. Critics, Russell among them, objected that this identified truth with usefulness.\n\nIn his last works James defended radical empiricism, on which relations between experiences are themselves experienced, and the view that mind and matter are built from a neutral pure experience, an idea Russell later adapted. A Pluralistic Universe (1909) attacked Hegelian absolutism in favor of a world still in the making. James influenced Husserl, Russell, Wittgenstein and later pragmatists such as Rorty and Putnam, and was active in the Anti-Imperialist League against American rule in the Philippines.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/james/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "William_James_b1842c.jpg", alt: "Photograph of William James", cap: "Photograph of William James. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["nietzsche", "Friedrich Nietzsche", "Nietzsche", "1844–1900", "German philologist and critic of morality, truth and Christianity.", ["The Gay Science (1882)", "Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883–85)", "Beyond Good and Evil (1886)", "On the Genealogy of Morality (1887)"], ["schopenhauer"], { x: "Friedrich Nietzsche was born in 1844 in Röcken, in Saxony, the son of a Lutheran pastor who died when he was four. He studied classical philology at Bonn and Leipzig and was so brilliant that in 1869, at twenty-four and before finishing his doctorate, he was appointed professor at Basel. He became a close friend of Richard Wagner, a friendship that ended bitterly in the late 1870s. Chronic illness forced him to resign in 1879, and for a decade he lived in boarding houses in Switzerland, Italy and the south of France, writing a book a year. In January 1889 he collapsed in Turin and never recovered his sanity; he spent his last years in the care of his mother and his sister Elisabeth, and died in 1900.\n\nThe Birth of Tragedy (1872) explained Greek tragedy as the union of two artistic drives, the Apollonian, of form, measure and dream, and the Dionysian, of intoxication and the dissolution of the individual. Human, All Too Human (1878) and Daybreak (1881) turned to psychological analysis of moral feelings, in aphoristic form.\n\nIn The Gay Science (1882) a madman announces that God is dead and that we have killed him: belief in the Christian God has become unbelievable, and with it the foundation of European morality is collapsing, though its consequences have not yet been grasped. Nietzsche's later work asks what values can survive this event. In Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883-85) the prophet Zarathustra teaches the overman (Übermensch), a human being who creates his own values, and the eternal recurrence, the thought of living one's life again, identically, infinitely many times, as a test of whether one can affirm it.\n\nBeyond Good and Evil (1886) and On the Genealogy of Morality (1887) offered a genealogy of morality. The noble types of antiquity called themselves good and the weak bad. The slave revolt in morality, born of ressentiment, the hatred of the powerless, inverted this, calling the powerful evil and praising humility, pity and self-denial as good. The Genealogy traces guilt and bad conscience to debt and to instincts turned inward, and criticizes the ascetic ideal, which gives suffering meaning by turning life against itself. Nietzsche held that all knowledge is perspectival and that life is will to power, and he valued affirmation, amor fati, the love of one's fate, truthfulness, creativity and individuality.\n\nHis last works, Twilight of the Idols, The Antichrist and Ecce Homo, were written in 1888. After his collapse Elisabeth, a nationalist and antisemite whom Nietzsche had opposed, controlled his papers and compiled The Will to Power from his notes, a misleading edition that helped the Nazis claim him. Postwar scholarship freed his work from that appropriation, and he has deeply influenced existentialism, psychoanalysis, post-structuralism and modern literature.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nietzsche/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Nietzsche187a.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Friedrich Nietzsche", cap: "Nietzsche about 1875. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["frege", "Gottlob Frege", "Frege", "1848–1925", "German logician who founded modern logic and analytic philosophy of language.", ["Begriffsschrift (1879)", "The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884)", "“On Sense and Reference” (1892)"], ["leibniz", "kant"], { x: "Gottlob Frege was born in Wismar in 1848, studied at Jena and Göttingen, where he received a doctorate in mathematics in 1873, and taught mathematics at Jena for the rest of his career. His work received little recognition in his lifetime. He died in 1925.\n\nHis Begriffsschrift (1879), or concept-script, created the first system of modern logic: a formal language with quantifiers and variables able to express generality and the logical form of mathematical reasoning, together with a precise notion of proof. In place of the old analysis of sentences into subject and predicate, Frege analyzed them into function and argument, so that a concept is a function whose value is a truth value.\n\nThe Foundations of Arithmetic (1884) defended logicism, the thesis that arithmetic can be derived from logic. It criticized empiricist and psychologistic accounts of number, argued that numbers are objects, defined number through what is now called Hume's principle (two concepts have the same number when their instances can be paired off one to one), and stated the context principle: never ask for the meaning of a word in isolation, only in the context of a sentence.\n\nIn “On Sense and Reference” (1892) he distinguished an expression's reference, what it stands for, from its sense, the mode in which that thing is presented. “The morning star” and “the evening star” have the same reference, the planet Venus, but different senses, which is why “the morning star is the evening star” can be informative. Sentences in attitude reports, such as “she believes that...”, refer to their customary senses, which explains why substituting co-referring terms there can change a sentence's truth.\n\nHis Basic Laws of Arithmetic (1893, 1903) tried to carry out the logicist program in full. In 1902, as the second volume was in press, Russell wrote to tell him that one of its axioms, Basic Law V, led to a contradiction, Russell's paradox, which undermined the project. Frege's logic and philosophy of language deeply influenced Russell, Carnap and Wittgenstein, and he is widely regarded as a founder of analytic philosophy; neo-logicists have revived his derivation of arithmetic from Hume's principle.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frege/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Young_frege.jpg", alt: "Photograph of the young Gottlob Frege", cap: "Frege as a young man. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["saussure", "Ferdinand de Saussure", "Saussure", "1857–1913", "Swiss linguist whose theory of the sign founded structuralism.", ["Course in General Linguistics (1916, compiled from students' notes)"], [], { x: "Ferdinand de Saussure was born in Geneva in 1857 and studied at Leipzig, then a center of comparative Indo-European linguistics, where he received his doctorate in 1880. While still a student he published a study of the vowel system of Proto-Indo-European that proposed sounds no attested language preserved; this laryngeal theory was confirmed decades later, after the decipherment of Hittite. He taught for eleven years at the École pratique des hautes études in Paris, then at the University of Geneva, where he taught Sanskrit, Indo-European and, from 1907 to 1911, general linguistics. He died in 1913.\n\nSaussure published little. The Course in General Linguistics (1916), the book that made him famous, was compiled after his death by his colleagues Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye from students' notes of his lectures, so some of its formulations are hard to trace to Saussure himself.\n\nThe Course distinguishes langue, the shared system of a language, from parole, individual acts of speaking, and makes langue the object of linguistics. A linguistic sign unites a signifier, a sound-image, with a signified, a concept, and the bond between them is arbitrary, fixed by convention and not by any natural resemblance, with marginal exceptions such as onomatopoeia.\n\nThe elements of a language get their value from their relations and differences with other elements in the same system. In language, the Course says, there are only differences without positive terms. Saussure also distinguished synchronic study, of a language as a system at one moment, from diachronic study, of its change over time, and gave priority to the synchronic, in contrast with the historical linguistics of his day. He distinguished syntagmatic relations, between elements in sequence, from associative relations between elements that could replace one another.\n\nHe proposed a general science of signs in social life, semiology. The Course founded structural linguistics and shaped the Prague School's phonology, and from the 1950s its model of language as a system of differences was carried into anthropology by Lévi-Strauss, into literary and cultural theory by Barthes, and into psychoanalysis by Lacan, becoming the starting point of structuralism and of the post-structuralist critiques that followed.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_de_Saussure", srcName: "Wikipedia", img: { file: "Ferdinand_de_Saussure_by_Jullien.png", alt: "Photograph of Ferdinand de Saussure", cap: "F. Jullien, photograph of Saussure. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["husserl", "Edmund Husserl", "Husserl", "1859–1938", "Founder of phenomenology.", ["Logical Investigations (1900–01)", "Ideas I (1913)", "Cartesian Meditations (1931)", "The Crisis of European Sciences (1936)"], ["descartes", "kant", "brentano"], { x: "Edmund Husserl was born in 1859 into a Jewish family in Prossnitz, Moravia. He trained as a mathematician, then studied with Franz Brentano in Vienna, who turned him to philosophy. He taught at Halle and Göttingen, and at Freiburg from 1916 until he retired in 1928. Under the Nazi regime he was barred from teaching and publishing because of his Jewish descent. He died in 1938, and his tens of thousands of pages of manuscripts were smuggled out of Germany to Leuven, where the Husserl Archives were founded.\n\nHis Logical Investigations (1900-01) opened with a critique of psychologism: the laws of logic are ideal and cannot be reduced to the empirical laws of how people happen to think. The rest of the work analyzed meaning and intentionality, the directedness of consciousness toward objects, distinguishing acts that merely intend an object emptily from acts in which it is intuitively given and the intention fulfilled.\n\nIn Ideas I (1913) Husserl introduced the epoché, the suspension of the natural attitude in which we take the world's existence for granted, and the phenomenological reduction, which turns attention to the world purely as it appears to consciousness. He presented this as a turn toward the world's givenness, and analyzed experiences into their act side (noesis) and their object-as-intended side (noema). His transcendental phenomenology studies how objects are constituted for consciousness, while holding that the world is not reducible to consciousness.\n\nHe gave detailed analyses of time-consciousness, in which every present moment includes a retention of what has just passed and a protention of what is about to come, allowing us to perceive duration and melody. He studied the lived body, and argued that objectivity rests on intersubjectivity and that empathy gives access to other minds, though never in the way we experience ourselves; the Cartesian Meditations (1931) set out this theory.\n\nIn The Crisis of European Sciences (1936) he argued that the natural sciences rest on the lifeworld, the pre-scientific world of everyday experience, which is historically and culturally shaped, and that scientism forgets this foundation. His work founded the phenomenological movement and shaped Heidegger, Edith Stein, Merleau-Ponty, Sartre and Levinas, and it continues to influence philosophy of mind and cognitive science.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/husserl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Edmund_Husserl_1900.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Edmund Husserl", cap: "Husserl about 1900. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["dewey", "John Dewey", "Dewey", "1859–1952", "American pragmatist of inquiry, democracy and education.", ["Democracy and Education (1916)", "Experience and Nature (1925)", "Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938)"], ["hegel", "peirce", "james"], { x: "John Dewey was born in Burlington, Vermont, in 1859 and studied at Johns Hopkins, where he heard Peirce and absorbed Hegelian idealism. He taught at Michigan and Minnesota, then led the philosophy department at the University of Chicago, where he founded the Laboratory School to test his ideas about education. In 1904 he moved to Columbia University. For half a century he was one of America's leading public intellectuals, active in causes such as academic freedom and civil liberties. He died in New York in 1952.\n\nWith Peirce and James he founded American pragmatism. His essay on the reflex arc concept (1896) argued that stimulus and response are phases of a continuous, coordinated activity, and he treated perception, habit, emotion and thought as transactions between an organism and its environment. In Experience and Nature (1925) he sought a naturalistic account of experience that would overcome the dualisms of mind and body, subject and object.\n\nDewey called his theory of knowledge instrumentalism. Ideas and theories are tools for resolving problems; inquiry begins in an indeterminate, troubled situation and aims to transform it into a settled one, as he set out in Logic: The Theory of Inquiry (1938). He rejected what he called the spectator theory of knowledge, on which knowing is passive contemplation of a fixed reality, and tied truth to the reliable results of ongoing inquiry.\n\nIn education, Democracy and Education (1916) argued that learning should grow out of students' own experience and problems and that schools should be small democratic communities; he called philosophy the general theory of education. In ethics he favored experimental, context-sensitive inquiry into values over fixed ultimate ends. The Public and Its Problems (1927) treated democracy as a way of life grounded in communication and shared problem-solving, and he criticized forms of individualism that undermined public welfare.\n\nArt as Experience (1934) treated art as continuous with the heightened experiences of everyday life, and A Common Faith (1934) recast religious feeling as devotion to ideals without supernatural belief. Dewey's influence runs through education, psychology, political theory, aesthetics and public policy, and through later pragmatists such as Rorty and Putnam.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dewey/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "John_Dewey_cph.3a51565.jpg", alt: "Photograph of John Dewey", cap: "Photograph of Dewey (Library of Congress). Public domain, via" } }],
  ["weber", "Max Weber", "Weber", "1864–1920", "German sociologist of rationalization, bureaucracy and the Protestant ethic.", ["The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1905)", "Economy and Society (1922)"], ["marx", "nietzsche"], { x: "Max Weber was born in Erfurt in 1864 into a prosperous and politically connected family. He trained in law, studied at Heidelberg and Berlin, and became a professor of economics, first at Freiburg and then at Heidelberg. After his father's death in 1897 he suffered a breakdown and withdrew from regular teaching for years, though he continued to write. After the First World War he took part in the debates over the new Weimar constitution and returned to teaching in Munich, where he died of pneumonia in 1920.\n\nThe Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1904-05) argued that the disciplined, methodical conduct encouraged by Calvinist ideas of a calling helped produce the spirit of modern capitalism, an account of its origins that gave a central role to religious ideas. Weber saw modern Western history as a process of rationalization, the spread of calculation, bureaucracy and formal rules into every sphere of life, and of disenchantment, the loss of a world understood through magic and meaning.\n\nHe feared that people would be trapped in an iron cage of bureaucratic routine, and that modern society would split into rival value spheres, science, politics, art, religion, with no shared ultimate ground for choosing among them. His lectures Science as a Vocation and Politics as a Vocation (both 1919) asked what scholarship and politics demand of those who devote themselves to them, and distinguished an ethic of conviction, loyalty to one's ultimate values, from an ethic of responsibility for the foreseeable consequences of action; the mature politician, he held, must combine both.\n\nWeber's method combined causal explanation with interpretive understanding (Verstehen) of the meaning actions have for those who perform them. He used ideal types, deliberately one-sided conceptual models such as “bureaucracy” or “the Protestant ethic,” as tools for comparing historical cases, and insisted on value freedom: researchers should keep their empirical findings separate from their own value judgments.\n\nHe distinguished three pure types of legitimate rule: traditional, resting on the sanctity of custom; charismatic, resting on devotion to an extraordinary leader; and legal-rational, resting on impersonal rules and offices, whose characteristic form is bureaucracy. His work, much of it collected after his death in Economy and Society (1922), is foundational to sociology and political science and influenced thinkers from Lukács and the Frankfurt School to Schmitt and Foucault.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/weber/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Max_Weber,_1918.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Max Weber", cap: "Max Weber in 1918. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["dubois", "W. E. B. Du Bois", "Du Bois", "1868–1963", "American sociologist, historian and civil-rights leader.", ["The Souls of Black Folk (1903)", "Black Reconstruction in America (1935)"], ["james"], { x: "W. E. B. Du Bois was born in Great Barrington, Massachusetts, in 1868. He studied at Fisk University and at Harvard, where in 1895 he became the first African American to earn a Harvard doctorate, and spent two years at the University of Berlin. He taught at Wilberforce and Atlanta University, helped found the American Negro Academy and the NAACP, and edited its magazine The Crisis for a quarter century. He organized Pan-African congresses, moved toward socialism in later life, and in 1961 settled in Ghana, where he died in 1963.\n\nThe Philadelphia Negro (1899) was one of the first empirical sociological studies of a Black community in the United States. Du Bois treated the “Negro problem” as a cluster of social problems, failures of a group to realize its ideals, which he traced to racial prejudice and to the historical deprivations it imposed. He saw sociology as the science of human action, combining social regularities with the role of chance and choice.\n\n“The Conservation of Races” (1897) argued that races are distinguished by shared history and ideals more than by physical traits, and called on Black Americans to develop their distinctive contribution to humanity. Kwame Anthony Appiah argued that this account still relies on biology, and the debate over Du Bois's concept of race remains central to the philosophy of race. In Dusk of Dawn (1940) he treated race as a social and historical fact.\n\nThe Souls of Black Folk (1903) declared that the problem of the twentieth century is the problem of the color line. It described double consciousness, the sense of always looking at oneself through the eyes of a world that regards one with contempt, and the veil that separates Black and white America. It also criticized Booker T. Washington's emphasis on vocational training and accommodation, and argued for leadership by a college-educated “talented tenth.”\n\nBlack Reconstruction in America (1935) rewrote the history of Reconstruction from the standpoint of the freed people, argued that white workers were compensated for low wages by a public and psychological wage of racial status, and held that accurate history yields moral knowledge for reform. In “Criteria of Negro Art” (1926) he argued that all art is propaganda. Much of the contemporary philosophy of race takes its starting points from his work.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dubois/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "W.E.B._Du_Bois_by_James_E._Purdy,_1907.jpg", alt: "Photograph of W. E. B. Du Bois", cap: "James E. Purdy, photograph of Du Bois, 1907. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["mauss", "Marcel Mauss", "Mauss", "1872–1950", "French anthropologist of the gift, exchange and techniques of the body.", ["The Gift (1925)", "“Techniques of the Body” (1934)"], [], { x: "Marcel Mauss was born into a Jewish family in Épinal in 1872, the nephew of the sociologist Émile Durkheim, under whom he studied philosophy at Bordeaux. He helped Durkheim found the journal L'Année sociologique, responsible for its sociology of religion, and in 1901 took a chair in the history of religions of “non-civilized peoples” at the École pratique des hautes études. He served as an army interpreter in the First World War, co-founded the Institut d'Ethnologie in 1926 and in 1931 became the first professor of sociology at the Collège de France. Under the German occupation antisemitic laws drove him from his post. He died in Paris in 1950.\n\nWith Henri Hubert he wrote studies of sacrifice (1899) and of magic (1902), and with Durkheim an essay on primitive forms of classification (1902), which traced the categories of thought to the organization of society.\n\nHis most influential work, The Gift (1925), asked what force in a thing given compels its recipient to give something back. Drawing on ethnographic material such as the potlatch of the Pacific Northwest, Mauss argued that gift exchange is governed by three obligations: to give, to receive and to reciprocate. Refusing a gift rejects a social bond, and failing to return one costs honor and status. Because the gift is never wholly separate from the giver, it creates a debt, and exchange builds lasting ties of solidarity.\n\nMauss treated the gift as a “total social fact,” at once legal, economic, religious, moral and aesthetic, and used his findings to criticize the reduction of social life to market exchange. Critics such as Alain Testart have argued that he overstated the obligation to reciprocate.\n\nIn “Techniques of the Body” (1934) he showed that walking, swimming, sleeping and other ordinary movements are learned differently in different societies, so that the body must be studied biologically, psychologically and sociologically at once; here he used the term habitus. Lévi-Strauss built on Mauss in founding structural anthropology, Bataille drew on his account of the potlatch in The Accursed Share, and Bourdieu developed his concept of habitus.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcel_Mauss", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["russell", "Bertrand Russell", "Russell", "1872–1970", "British logician and a founder of analytic philosophy.", ["“On Denoting” (1905)", "Principia Mathematica (1910–13, with Whitehead)", "The Problems of Philosophy (1912)"], ["frege"], { x: "Bertrand Russell was born in 1872 into an aristocratic family; his grandfather, Lord John Russell, had twice been prime minister. He studied mathematics and philosophy at Cambridge, where, with G. E. Moore, he broke with the idealism then dominant in British philosophy, and he is regarded as a founder, with Moore, of analytic philosophy. He lived until 1970, writing on almost every subject and campaigning on many causes.\n\nIn logic and mathematics he defended logicism, the view that mathematics can be reduced to logic, and refined Frege's logic. In 1901 he discovered the paradox that bears his name, concerning the set of all sets that are not members of themselves, which showed that Frege's system was inconsistent. His theory of types was designed to avoid such paradoxes, and with Alfred North Whitehead he wrote Principia Mathematica (1910-13), an attempt to derive mathematics from logical axioms.\n\nHis theory of descriptions, in “On Denoting” (1905), analyzed phrases such as “the author of Waverley” or “the present King of France” as disguised quantified claims about existence and uniqueness, so that they need not refer to anything to be meaningful; it became a model of philosophical analysis. In epistemology he distinguished knowledge by acquaintance, direct awareness of sense-data, universals and perhaps the self, from knowledge by description. He developed logical atomism, on which the world consists of facts mirrored in a logically perfect language, and later defended neutral monism, on which mind and matter are constructed from a single kind of stuff that is neither.\n\nRussell was an outspoken atheist and critic of religion. He opposed the First World War and was imprisoned in 1918 for his anti-war writings, and was imprisoned again in 1961, aged eighty-nine, for protest against nuclear weapons. He co-founded the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament and was involved in the Russell-Einstein Manifesto (1955). His appointment at the City College of New York was revoked in 1940 after a campaign against his views on sex and marriage.\n\nHe received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1950. His popular books, above all The Problems of Philosophy (1912) and A History of Western Philosophy (1945), introduced generations of readers to the subject.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/russell/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "Bertrand_Russell_transparent_bg.png", alt: "Photograph of Bertrand Russell", cap: "Photograph of Bertrand Russell. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["moore", "G. E. Moore", "Moore", "1873–1958", "British philosopher of common sense and of the indefinability of good.", ["Principia Ethica (1903)", "“A Defence of Common Sense” (1925)"], [], { x: "G. E. Moore was born in 1873 and grew up in south London. He studied classics and then philosophy at Trinity College, Cambridge, held a prize fellowship from 1898, returned to Cambridge as a lecturer in 1911, and was professor of philosophy from 1925 until he retired in 1939, when Wittgenstein succeeded him. He edited the journal Mind from 1921 to 1944. Among his friends were members of the Bloomsbury Group, including Keynes, Lytton Strachey and Leonard and Virginia Woolf, who took his ethics as a guide. He died in 1958.\n\nWith Russell, Moore led the break with the idealism of Bradley and the British Hegelians. “The Refutation of Idealism” (1903) argued that in a sensation of blue the blue is an object distinct from the act of awareness, so that what is known is not a mere content of the mind. Together with Russell and Wittgenstein, he made Cambridge the center of the new analytic philosophy.\n\nPrincipia Ethica (1903) argued that goodness is a simple, indefinable property. Any attempt to define it in natural terms, as pleasure or what we desire, commits what Moore called the naturalistic fallacy; his open question argument holds that for any such definition it remains an open, meaningful question whether the thing so defined is good. He held that the greatest intrinsic goods are personal affection and the appreciation of beauty, and that right action is what produces the most good.\n\nIn “A Defense of Common Sense” (1925) he claimed to know with certainty many truisms, that he has a body, that the earth has existed for many years, and held that philosophy should analyze such propositions and leave their truth in no doubt. In “Proof of an External World” (1939) he held up his hands and said “here is one hand” and “here is another,” concluding that at least two external objects exist. In late notes he conceded that he could not know for certain that he was not dreaming. He also noticed the absurdity of saying something like “it is raining, but I do not believe that it is,” now called Moore's paradox.\n\nMoore made analysis, the careful clarification of concepts and propositions, the central method of philosophy, and developed a theory of perception in terms of sense-data. His argument that goodness cannot be defined in natural terms shaped the twentieth-century metaethics of Ayer and Stevenson, though both rejected his non-natural property in favor of non-cognitivism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moore/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "G._E._Moore_c._1903_(cropped).jpg", alt: "Photograph of G. E. Moore as a young man", cap: "G. E. Moore around 1903, the year of Principia Ethica. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["kelsen", "Hans Kelsen", "Kelsen", "1881–1973", "Austrian legal theorist of the pure theory of law.", ["Pure Theory of Law (1934)"], ["kant"], { x: "Hans Kelsen was born in Prague in 1881, grew up in Vienna and studied law at its university. He was the principal architect of the Austrian constitution of 1920, which still forms the basis of Austrian constitutional law, and of its separate constitutional court, a model of judicial review later adopted across Europe. He taught in Vienna and from 1930 in Cologne. When the Nazis came to power in 1933 he was dismissed and moved to Geneva and then Prague. In 1940 he emigrated to the United States, where he taught at Berkeley until his death in 1973.\n\nHis Pure Theory of Law (1934, much expanded in a second edition of 1960) set out to describe law purely as a system of norms, free of moral evaluation, political ideology and sociological or psychological explanation. Drawing on Kant's distinction between what is and what ought to be, Kelsen held that a norm's validity can be derived only from another norm, never from a fact.\n\nLegal norms therefore form a hierarchy, each lower norm, such as a judicial decision or an administrative order, valid because it was created in the way a higher norm, a statute or the constitution, authorizes. At the top of the chain stands the basic norm (Grundnorm), a presupposed norm that the historically first constitution ought to be obeyed. It is the condition under which a legal order can be understood as valid at all.\n\nKelsen was a legal positivist who rejected natural law theory and separated the science of law from legal politics. He held that the state and the legal order are identical and rejected theories that treat the state as a reality prior to law. In a famous debate of the early 1930s he argued, against Carl Schmitt, that a constitutional court, not the head of state, should be the guardian of the constitution.\n\nHe also wrote widely on democracy, which he defended on the basis of relativism about values, and on sovereignty and international law, which he regarded as forming a single legal order with national law; his work informed the prosecution of war crimes after the Second World War. His influence has been greatest in continental Europe and Latin America, and H. L. A. Hart and Joseph Raz developed their own positivism in dialogue with his.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Kelsen", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["neurath", "Otto Neurath", "Neurath", "1882–1945", "Austrian social scientist and logical empiricist of the Vienna Circle.", ["“Protocol Sentences” (1932)"], [], { x: "Otto Neurath was born in Vienna in 1882 and earned a doctorate in economic history in Berlin in 1906. In 1919 he briefly headed the central planning office of the short-lived Bavarian Soviet Republic, and when it fell he was imprisoned before being allowed to return to Austria. In socialist “Red Vienna,” from 1921 to 1934, he worked in housing and adult education, founded the Social and Economic Museum, and developed with his collaborators the Isotype system of picture statistics, an ancestor of modern infographics. He was a leading organizer of the Vienna Circle and co-wrote its manifesto of 1929.\n\nWhen Austria turned authoritarian in 1934 Neurath moved to the Netherlands, and after the German invasion in 1940 he escaped to England, where he died in 1945. Throughout he promoted the unity of science, editing the International Encyclopedia of Unified Science.\n\nNeurath's physicalism proposed that science should be expressed in a common language of public, observable things and events in space and time. He treated it as a methodological rule for the sciences, and it challenged the authority that philosophers gave to private experience.\n\nIn the protocol-sentence debate of the early 1930s he rejected Carnap's idea that science rests on basic statements recording immediate experience with certainty. Observation reports are themselves revisable statements, accepted or rejected according to how they fit with the rest of our beliefs. His image for this anti-foundationalism became famous: we are like sailors who must rebuild their ship on the open sea, never able to put it into dry dock and reconstruct it from the best materials.\n\nHe opposed hierarchical, reductive systems of science in favor of a looser encyclopedia of connected and changing knowledge. In economics he proposed a planned “economy in kind,” calculated in physical quantities without money, which provoked the socialist calculation debate with Ludwig von Mises; he held that economic decisions involve several incommensurable criteria and cannot be settled by a single measure. His boat image was taken up by Quine as an emblem of naturalized epistemology.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/neurath/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["lukacs", "Georg Lukács", "Lukács", "1885–1971", "Hungarian Marxist philosopher and literary critic.", ["The Theory of the Novel (1916)", "History and Class Consciousness (1923)"], ["hegel", "marx", "weber"], { x: "Georg Lukács was born in Budapest in 1885 and died there in 1971. Before the First World War he worked in Budapest, Berlin, Florence and Heidelberg, where he was close to Max and Marianne Weber, and wrote the essays of Soul and Form (1910) and The Theory of the Novel (1916), which read the novel as the form of a world abandoned by meaning.\n\nIn 1918 he joined the Hungarian Communist Party, served as a commissar for education in Béla Kun's Soviet Republic of 1919, and after its fall lived in exile, first in Vienna and from 1930 mostly in Moscow. He returned to Budapest at the end of the Second World War as a university professor. In 1956 he served as a minister in Imre Nagy's government; after the Soviet invasion he was deported for a time and expelled from the party, which readmitted him in 1969.\n\nHistory and Class Consciousness (1923) argued for a Marxism attentive to its Hegelian roots and to the category of totality. Its central essay, “Reification and the Consciousness of the Proletariat,” extended Marx's analysis of commodity fetishism: in capitalist society the commodity form becomes the universal form of objectivity, so that social relations appear as relations between things and human activity confronts people as an alien, law-governed object. The proletariat, as both the object and the potential subject of this process, can grasp society as a whole and become the identical subject-object of history. Lukács distinguished the empirical consciousness of workers from imputed class consciousness, the consciousness appropriate to their objective position.\n\nThe book was condemned by Communist orthodoxy, and Lukács publicly retracted its views. In a preface of 1967 he criticized it for confusing objectification with alienation. His later work reinterpreted Hegel's dialectic as rooted in political economy (The Young Hegel, 1948), attacked post-Hegelian German thought as irrationalism in The Destruction of Reason (1954), a widely criticized book, and defended realism in literature, favoring Balzac, Tolstoy and Thomas Mann over modernism, which drew objections from Brecht and Adorno.\n\nHis last projects were an aesthetics and an unfinished Ontology of Social Being that treats labor as the basis of social life. History and Class Consciousness is regarded as a founding text of Western Marxism; its theory of reification shaped the Frankfurt School and later feminist standpoint theory.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lukacs/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "György_Lukács_1917-1.1.jpg", alt: "Photograph of György Lukács", cap: "György Lukács in 1917. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["schmitt", "Carl Schmitt", "Schmitt", "1888–1985", "German jurist of sovereignty, the exception and the friend–enemy distinction; joined the Nazi party in 1933.", ["Political Theology (1922)", "The Concept of the Political (1932)"], ["bodin", "hobbes"], { x: "Carl Schmitt was born in Plettenberg, Westphalia, in 1888 to Catholic parents, and taught constitutional law in the Weimar Republic, at Bonn and later Berlin. He is among the most influential critics of liberalism and parliamentary democracy, and his support for National Socialism makes the value of his work contested.\n\nPolitical Theology (1922) opens with the claim that the sovereign is the one who decides on the exception. Legal orders, Schmitt argued, cannot apply themselves; in an emergency that the norms do not foresee, someone must decide, and that decision reveals where sovereignty lies. He also argued that the central concepts of the modern theory of the state are secularized theological concepts.\n\nThe Concept of the Political (1927, expanded 1932) held that the specifically political distinction is that between friend and enemy, a public enemy whose existence threatens a people's way of life. Liberalism, he argued, tries to dissolve this distinction into economics and ethics and so cannot ground a political community. The Crisis of Parliamentary Democracy (1923) argued that parliamentary government by discussion had lost its basis in mass democracy, which he understood as the identity of rulers and ruled within a homogeneous people. His Constitutional Theory (1928) and Legality and Legitimacy (1932) belong to the same Weimar period.\n\nSchmitt joined the Nazi party on 1 May 1933 and became a leading legal theorist of the regime. He celebrated the burning of books by Jewish authors and in 1936 chaired a conference calling for German law to be purged of the “Jewish spirit.” That year the SS paper Das Schwarze Korps attacked him as an opportunist, and he lost his party offices, though he kept his professorship. He also justified Nazi expansion in terms of international law.\n\nAfter the war he was held in an internment camp by US forces for more than a year, refused denazification and was barred from teaching. He lived in Plettenberg until his death in 1985, publishing The Nomos of the Earth (1950) and remaining influential among conservative and later radical thinkers. Scholars disagree about whether his Weimar work can be separated from his Nazism; his concepts of the exception and the political have been taken up on both right and left, notably by Giorgio Agamben.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schmitt/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Schmitt", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Carl Schmitt”" }] }],
  ["heidegger", "Martin Heidegger", "Heidegger", "1889–1976", "German philosopher of being; his Nazism remains a subject of controversy.", ["Being and Time (1927)", "“The Question Concerning Technology” (1954)"], ["aristotle", "kierkegaard", "nietzsche", "husserl"], { x: "Martin Heidegger was born in Messkirch, in Baden, in 1889, the son of a craftsman who served as sexton of the local Catholic church. He studied theology and then philosophy at Freiburg, became Edmund Husserl's assistant in 1919, taught at Marburg from 1923, and returned to Freiburg in 1928 to succeed Husserl. He died in 1976.\n\nBeing and Time (1927) asks the question of the meaning of being, which Heidegger argued the philosophical tradition had forgotten. It approaches the question through an analysis of the one being for whom being is an issue, the human being, which he called Dasein. Dasein is being-in-the-world: absorbed in practical dealings before it is ever a subject facing objects. Things show up first as ready-to-hand equipment, like a hammer in use, and only when that use breaks down as present-at-hand objects of theoretical observation.\n\nEveryday Dasein lives in the anonymous conformity of “the they” (das Man), doing what one does. Anxiety, which has no definite object, breaks this absorption and reveals Dasein's finitude and its being as care. In being-toward-death, the anticipation of its own death, Dasein can take over its existence as its own, which Heidegger called authenticity, and the structure of care turns out to be grounded in temporality. The book was never completed, and Heidegger later abandoned its project.\n\nAfter a “turn” in the 1930s his thought centered on the history of being, on truth understood as unconcealment (aletheia), and on language and poetry, especially Hölderlin's. In “The Question Concerning Technology” (1954) he described modern technology as a way of revealing that treats everything, nature and people included, as a standing reserve of resources, and he called for meditative thinking and a poetic dwelling on the earth.\n\nIn 1933 he was elected rector of Freiburg and joined the Nazi party. He resigned the rectorship in 1934 but remained a member until 1945, and after the war he was barred from teaching until 1949. His support for National Socialism and his antisemitism, documented further by the publication of his private Black Notebooks from 2014, are well established; he distorted the record afterward and never clearly repudiated his support. Scholars disagree about how far this bears on his philosophy, which deeply shaped Arendt, Sartre, Gadamer, Levinas, Derrida and Foucault.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/heidegger/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["wittgenstein", "Ludwig Wittgenstein", "Wittgenstein", "1889–1951", "Austrian-British philosopher of logic, language and meaning as use.", ["Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921)", "Philosophical Investigations (1953)", "On Certainty (1969)"], ["frege", "russell"], { x: "Ludwig Wittgenstein was born in Vienna in 1889 into one of the wealthiest industrial families of the Habsburg empire. He studied engineering in Berlin and Manchester, became absorbed by the foundations of mathematics, and in 1911 went to Cambridge to work with Bertrand Russell. He served in the Austrian army in the First World War and completed the Tractatus during it. Afterward he gave away his fortune and worked as a schoolteacher, a gardener and an architect in Austria. He returned to Cambridge in 1929, succeeded G. E. Moore as professor in 1939, and died in 1951.\n\nThe Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921), the only philosophical book he published, holds that the world is the totality of facts and that propositions picture possible facts by sharing their logical form. Logical truths are tautologies that say nothing about the world. What a proposition shares with reality, its form, cannot itself be said but only shown, and the same holds for ethics, aesthetics and the meaning of life. The book ends: whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent.\n\nIn his later work, published after his death as Philosophical Investigations (1953), he rejected the Tractatus's picture of language as naming objects and stating facts. The meaning of a word is, in most cases, its use in the language. Language is woven into human activities and forms of life, which Wittgenstein called language-games, and the many things called by one word, such as “game,” share family resemblances, overlapping similarities, without any single common essence.\n\nThe Investigations also discusses rule-following: no interpretation of a rule can fix how it is to be applied, since any interpretation could itself be misread, and following a rule is a practice. The private language argument holds that a language whose words referred only to the speaker's private sensations would lack any criterion of correctness, and so could not be a language. Philosophy, on this view, does not build theories; it dissolves the confusions that arise when language goes on holiday, and shows the fly the way out of the fly-bottle.\n\nOn Certainty, written in his last months, argues that doubt itself presupposes certainties that stand fast, such as that the earth has existed for a long time. Interpreters disagree about how sharply his early and later work differ. His influence runs through logical positivism, ordinary language philosophy and much of later philosophy of mind, language and religion.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wittgenstein/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", img: { file: "36._Portrait_of_Ludwig_Wittgenstein,_1930.jpg", alt: "Photograph of Ludwig Wittgenstein", cap: "Moritz Nähr, photograph of Wittgenstein, 1930. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["carnap", "Rudolf Carnap", "Carnap", "1891–1970", "German-American logical empiricist of the Vienna Circle.", ["The Logical Structure of the World (1928)", "“Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950)"], ["frege", "russell"], { x: "Rudolf Carnap was born in Ronsdorf, in the Rhineland, in 1891 and studied at Jena, where he attended Frege's lectures on logic. After serving in the First World War he worked on the philosophy of space and physics, and in 1926 joined the Vienna Circle, becoming its leading logician. He moved to Prague in 1931 and, as the Nazis consolidated power, to the United States, reaching Chicago in 1936; from 1954 he taught at UCLA. He died in 1970.\n\nCarnap saw philosophy as conceptual engineering, the design of clearer concepts and languages for science. The Logical Construction of the World (Der logische Aufbau der Welt, 1928) attempted a rational reconstruction of the concepts of science from a basis of elementary experiences and a single relation of remembered similarity. Later he described his method as explication, replacing a vague everyday concept with a precise one that serves the same purposes.\n\nIn “The Elimination of Metaphysics through Logical Analysis of Language” (1932) he argued that metaphysical sentences, with Heidegger's “the nothing itself nothings” as his chief example, are pseudo-statements that express an attitude to life without asserting anything. The Logical Syntax of Language (1934) stated the principle of tolerance: in logic there are no morals, and everyone is free to build a language as he wishes, provided he states its rules clearly. Questions of which language to adopt are practical questions of convenience.\n\nIn “Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology” (1950) Carnap distinguished questions internal to a linguistic framework, such as whether there is a prime number greater than a hundred, which the framework's rules answer, from external questions about whether to adopt the framework at all, which are pragmatic choices. From the 1940s much of his work went into semantics (Meaning and Necessity, 1947) and inductive logic, the attempt to define degrees of confirmation of hypotheses by evidence (Logical Foundations of Probability, 1950).\n\nQuine's attack on the analytic-synthetic distinction, on which Carnap's frameworks depended, and the decline of logical empiricism reduced his influence in the 1950s and 1960s. Since the 1980s a reassessment has restored him to a central place in the history of analytic philosophy, and his metaontology and conceptual engineering have found new followers.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/carnap/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["gramsci", "Antonio Gramsci", "Gramsci", "1891–1937", "Italian Marxist and communist leader who wrote his major work in Mussolini's prisons.", ["Prison Notebooks (1929–35)"], ["marx"], { x: "Antonio Gramsci was born in Ales, Sardinia, in 1891, the fourth of seven sons of a family that fell into poverty when his father was imprisoned. A spinal malformation stunted his growth and he suffered poor health all his life. He won a scholarship to the University of Turin in 1911, joined the Italian Socialist Party in 1913, and became a leading journalist and organizer in Turin, where in 1919 he co-founded the weekly L'Ordine Nuovo and supported the factory councils of the city's workers.\n\nIn 1921 he helped found the Communist Party of Italy at Livorno. He spent 1922-23 in Moscow, where he married Julia Schucht, was elected to parliament in 1924, and became the party's general secretary that year. In November 1926 Mussolini's government arrested him despite his parliamentary immunity; at his trial the prosecutor declared that this brain must be stopped from working for twenty years. In prison he filled more than thirty notebooks, some three thousand pages, on history, politics, culture and philosophy. His health collapsed, and he died in a Rome clinic in 1937, at forty-six.\n\nThe Prison Notebooks' central concept is hegemony. A ruling class keeps power through consent as well as force, by making its own values and view of the world seem like common sense. Political society, the state's apparatus of coercion, works together with civil society, the family, schools, churches, unions and press, where consent is organized. A historic bloc is an alliance of social forces held together by such leadership.\n\nIn the advanced capitalist West, where civil society is dense, revolution cannot follow the Russian model of a direct assault on the state, a war of maneuver; it requires a long war of position, building counter-hegemony within civil society. Every rising social group produces its own organic intellectuals, who articulate its outlook, as distinct from traditional intellectuals who see themselves as independent of any class. Passive revolution names the way a ruling class absorbs pressure for change through concessions and reorganization from above, as in Italian unification and fascism. Gramsci also wrote of subaltern groups, excluded from the dominant order, whose histories are fragmentary.\n\nGramsci drew on Machiavelli, Croce and Hegel and is a central figure of Western Marxism. His concepts of hegemony, intellectuals and the subaltern became basic tools of cultural studies, critical theory, political science and postcolonial history, notably for Stuart Hall and the Subaltern Studies historians.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonio_Gramsci", srcName: "Wikipedia", img: { file: "Gramsci.png", alt: "Photograph of Antonio Gramsci", cap: "Photograph of Gramsci. Public domain, via" } }],
  ["benjamin", "Walter Benjamin", "Benjamin", "1892–1940", "German critic of art, history and modernity, close to the Frankfurt School.", ["“The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” (1935–36)", "“On the Concept of History” (1940)", "The Arcades Project (unfinished)"], ["marx", "lukacs"], { x: "Walter Benjamin was born in Berlin in 1892 into a prosperous assimilated Jewish family. He studied philosophy, took part in the youth movement, and formed a lifelong friendship with Gershom Scholem, which shaped his relation to Judaism. His dissertation treated the concept of art criticism in early German Romanticism. When his habilitation thesis, The Origin of German Tragic Drama, was rejected in 1925, an academic career closed to him, and he lived as a critic and essayist. After 1933 he lived in exile in Paris, supported in part by the Institute for Social Research. In 1940, fleeing occupied France, he died at Portbou on the Spanish border.\n\nHis early writings criticized Kant's narrow, Newtonian concept of experience and, drawing on Hamann, treated language as the medium of all experience. His study of the Baroque mourning play (Trauerspiel) contrasted the symbol, with its fleeting moment of transfiguration, with allegory, which presents history as a petrified landscape of ruins, and its prologue set out a theory of ideas as constellations of phenomena. “Critique of Violence” (1921) distinguished law-making from law-preserving violence and examined the state's claim to a monopoly of force.\n\n“The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” (1935-36) argued that mechanical reproduction strips the work of art of its aura, the authority of its unique presence in time and space, and opens new possibilities for collective experience, especially through film. It ends by contrasting fascism, which aestheticizes politics, with communism's answer, the politicization of art. Adorno criticized the essay, and the role of the aura in Benjamin's thought is more ambivalent than this summary suggests.\n\nFor thirteen years he worked on the Arcades Project, an unfinished study of nineteenth-century Paris through its shopping arcades, fashions, and street life, meant as a model for a new materialist history built from dialectical images. His essays on Baudelaire read modernity through the shock experience of the city crowd and through the commodity's transformation of historical time.\n\nHis last text, “On the Concept of History” (1940), attacked the idea of history as progress. Its angel of history faces the past and sees a single catastrophe piling wreckage upon wreckage, while a storm called progress drives him into the future. The historian's task is to seize the past as it flashes up in a moment of danger. Benjamin's posthumous fame came late, through editions of his writings, and he has shaped Adorno and the Frankfurt School, Derrida, Agamben and much of literary and media theory.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/benjamin/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["horkheimer", "Max Horkheimer", "Horkheimer", "1895–1973", "Director of the Institute for Social Research and founder of Frankfurt critical theory.", ["“Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937)", "Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947, with Adorno)", "Eclipse of Reason (1947)"], ["schopenhauer", "hegel", "marx"], { x: "Max Horkheimer was born in Stuttgart in 1895 into a Jewish family of textile manufacturers and worked in the family business before turning to philosophy. In 1930 he became professor of social philosophy at Frankfurt and director of the Institute for Social Research. When the Nazis closed the Institute in 1933, he led it into exile, through Geneva to New York, where it was affiliated with Columbia University, and in 1941 moved to Los Angeles. He returned to Frankfurt in 1949, re-founded the Institute, served as rector of the university, and retired to Switzerland in 1958. He died in 1973.\n\nIn the 1930s, through the Institute's journal, Horkheimer set out a program of interdisciplinary materialism, combining philosophy with empirical social research in economics, psychology and cultural analysis, with the aim of explaining and reducing human suffering. He rejected both speculative metaphysics and narrow positivist science, and traced suffering to the irrationality of capitalist social organization.\n\nHis essay “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937) contrasted traditional theory, modeled on Descartes and the natural sciences, which takes its own social role for granted, with critical theory, which understands itself as part of the society it studies and aims at its transformation. The essay already shows less confidence in the working class as the agent of change.\n\nWith Adorno he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944, revised 1947), which argues that enlightenment reason, reduced to an instrument for calculating means, turns against its own emancipatory aims and issues in the domination of outer and inner nature, as well as in the culture industry and modern antisemitism. His Eclipse of Reason (1947) distinguished subjective reason, concerned with efficient means to whatever ends are given, from objective reason, which once claimed to judge ends themselves.\n\nHorkheimer's late writings are more pessimistic and show the influence of Schopenhauer: critical theory becomes the negative task of opposing suffering without claiming to define the good. He valued in religion the longing for the totally other, a world better than this one, while rejecting its institutions. Critics have called his late views conservative, pointing to his opposition to the radical student movement. His work shaped Adorno, Marcuse and later critical theorists such as Habermas and Honneth.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/horkheimer/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["bataille", "Georges Bataille", "Bataille", "1897–1962", "French writer and theorist of expenditure, eroticism and the sacred.", ["“The Notion of Expenditure” (1933)", "Inner Experience (1943)", "The Accursed Share, vol. 1 (1949)", "Erotism (1957)"], ["hegel", "nietzsche", "mauss", "kojeve"], { x: "Georges Bataille was born in Billom, in the Auvergne, in 1897. He converted to Catholicism in 1914, considered the priesthood, and renounced Christianity in the early 1920s. After training as an archivist at the École des Chartes he worked for most of his life as a librarian at the Bibliothèque nationale in Paris. The Russian philosopher Lev Shestov introduced him to Nietzsche and Dostoevsky. He died in Paris in 1962, relatively little read in his lifetime.\n\nBataille had a stormy relation with Surrealism and quarreled with André Breton. In the 1930s he founded or joined a series of groups and reviews: he co-founded the College of Sociology, devoted to the study of the sacred in modern life, and the secret society Acéphale, whose emblem was a headless man. In 1946 he founded the review Critique, which became one of the leading French intellectual journals.\n\nHis early base materialism rejected mainstream materialism as a disguised idealism, centered on a low, formless matter that disrupts every opposition of high and low. His erotic fiction, beginning with Story of the Eye (1928), published under a pseudonym, was later read as transgressive literature of philosophical seriousness.\n\nIn “The Notion of Expenditure” (1933) and The Accursed Share (1949), which he called the work of thirty years, Bataille set a general economy against the restricted economy of conventional economics. Economics starts from scarcity; a general economy starts from the excess of energy that life and societies produce and must spend. This accursed share can be spent luxuriously and knowingly, in festivals, art, eroticism and monuments, or catastrophically, in war. He drew on Mauss's account of the gift and the potlatch.\n\nInner Experience (1943), part of his wartime Summa Atheologica, explored a mystical experience without God, and Erotism (1957) linked eroticism, death and the sacred through prohibition and transgression. Sovereignty, the moment of existence freed from servitude to utility, recurs throughout his work. After his death Bataille became a major influence on Foucault, Derrida, Baudrillard, Kristeva and Nancy, and his writing on sovereignty was taken up by Agamben.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_Bataille", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["marcuse", "Herbert Marcuse", "Marcuse", "1898–1979", "Frankfurt School philosopher, influential on the New Left.", ["Eros and Civilization (1955)", "One-Dimensional Man (1964)"], ["hegel", "marx", "heidegger"], { x: "Herbert Marcuse was born in Berlin in 1898 into a well-off Jewish family. He studied at Freiburg, worked with Heidegger, and in 1932 published Hegel's Ontology and the Theory of Historicity. That year he joined the Institute for Social Research and followed it into exile, in Geneva, Paris and then New York at Columbia. During the Second World War he worked for the Office of War Information and the Office of Strategic Services, analyzing Nazi Germany. He later taught at Brandeis and the University of California, San Diego. Called the guru of the New Left in the 1960s, a title he rejected, he died in 1979.\n\nReason and Revolution (1941) defended Hegel's and Marx's dialectical, negative thinking, which measures what exists against its unrealized possibilities, against its positivist critics. Eros and Civilization (1955) reinterpreted Freud historically. Every civilization requires some repression of instinct, but advanced industrial society imposes surplus repression, beyond what the struggle with scarcity demands, in the service of domination; its form of the reality principle is the performance principle.\n\nOne-Dimensional Man (1964) described a comfortable, administered society that absorbs opposition by satisfying needs it has itself created, flattening critical thought into a single dimension that accepts what exists as all that could be. Domination works less through personal authority than through technology, administration and internalized desires. Marcuse's hope lay in a Great Refusal by those outside or against the system.\n\nHis essay “Repressive Tolerance” (1965) argued that in an unequal society tolerance can be turned into a means of legitimating oppression, a thesis that provoked fierce debate. An Essay on Liberation (1969) welcomed a “new sensibility” in the student and liberation movements and argued that technology could be redirected toward human ends and the abolition of toil.\n\nThe Aesthetic Dimension (1978) defended the critical and utopian power of autonomous art against orthodox Marxist aesthetics, while holding that art's promise must be realized in political action. Marcuse's work linked the Frankfurt School to the American New Left and remains a reference point for critical theories of consumer society and technology.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/marcuse/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["schutz", "Alfred Schutz", "Schutz", "1899–1959", "Austrian-American phenomenological sociologist.", ["The Phenomenology of the Social World (1932)"], ["weber", "husserl"], { x: "Alfred Schutz was born in Vienna in 1899. He trained in law and social science, took part in the circle of the economist Ludwig von Mises, and worked as a legal adviser to a bank for most of his life, earning the description a banker by day and a philosopher by night. After the annexation of Austria in 1938 he emigrated with his family, reaching New York in 1939. From 1943 he taught at the New School for Social Research. He died in 1959.\n\nHis one book published in his lifetime, The Phenomenology of the Social World (Der sinnhafte Aufbau der sozialen Welt, 1932), joined Max Weber's interpretive sociology to Edmund Husserl's phenomenology. Schutz admired Weber's method of understanding the meaning actions have for their agents but argued that Weber had left the concept of meaning unexamined. He used Husserl's analysis of inner time-consciousness to clarify how actions acquire meaning.\n\nHe distinguished “in-order-to” motives, which point forward to the project an action is meant to realize, from “because” motives, which point back to the experiences and circumstances that led the agent to it. The social world is layered: consociates share time and space face to face in a we-relationship, while contemporaries, predecessors and successors are known in progressively more anonymous and typified ways.\n\nEveryday life takes place in the lifeworld, which people navigate with a stock of knowledge made of typifications, typical expectations of things, people and courses of action, organized by what is relevant to their purposes. Schutz also described multiple realities, provinces of meaning such as dreams, play and science, of which the everyday world of working is the paramount reality.\n\nSocial scientists, he argued, should construct ideal types of the typifications ordinary actors already use, so that their concepts remain answerable to the meanings of the people studied. Schutz's work shaped Harold Garfinkel's ethnomethodology and conversation analysis, and Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann built The Social Construction of Reality on it.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/schutz/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["ryle", "Gilbert Ryle", "Ryle", "1900–1976", "Oxford philosopher of ordinary language and critic of Cartesian dualism.", ["The Concept of Mind (1949)"], ["wittgenstein"], { x: "Gilbert Ryle was born in Brighton in 1900 and spent his career at Oxford, apart from wartime service in intelligence. He was Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy from 1945 and succeeded G. E. Moore as editor of the journal Mind. He was a friend of Wittgenstein, and he did much to make Oxford the center of postwar ordinary language philosophy. He died in 1976.\n\nThe Concept of Mind (1949) attacks what Ryle called the official doctrine, descended from Descartes: that each person has a body and a mind, the mind a private inner theater of states and processes known directly only to its owner. Ryle mocked this as the dogma of the ghost in the machine and argued that its assumptions generate insoluble problems about how mind and body interact, how we know other minds, and how we know ourselves.\n\nHis central diagnostic tool is the category mistake. A visitor shown the colleges, libraries and laboratories of Oxford who then asks where the university is has made one, treating the university as another item of the same kind as the buildings. The official doctrine, Ryle argued, makes the same mistake about the mind, treating intelligent behavior as the outward effect of a hidden inner cause of the same kind.\n\nMental terms, on his account, mostly describe dispositions and the manner in which people act: to be vain or to know French is to be disposed to behave in certain ways under certain conditions. He distinguished knowing how from knowing that and rejected the “intellectualist legend” that every intelligent action requires first considering a proposition. Ryle is often called a behaviorist, though the Stanford Encyclopedia argues that he never proposed translating statements about minds into statements about behavior.\n\nHis method was to map the logical geography of concepts, as a cartographer maps a village, by tracing how ordinary expressions actually work. His later writings included Dilemmas (1954), essays on thinking that introduced the notion of thick description, later taken up by Clifford Geertz, and studies of Plato. His views anticipate themes associated with Wittgenstein, and his influence on functionalism in philosophy of mind is often underrated.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ryle/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["gadamer", "Hans-Georg Gadamer", "Gadamer", "1900–2002", "German philosopher of hermeneutics.", ["Truth and Method (1960)"], ["plato", "hegel", "heidegger"], { x: "Hans-Georg Gadamer was born in Marburg in 1900 and grew up in Breslau. He wrote his doctorate on Plato at Marburg in 1922 and joined the circle around Heidegger, and his training in classical philology stayed with him. He taught at Kiel and Leipzig, where he served as rector after the war, and at Frankfurt, and in 1949 succeeded Karl Jaspers at Heidelberg. Truth and Method (1960) appeared when he was sixty. After retiring in 1968 he lectured widely, especially in North America, and he died in Heidelberg in 2002, aged 102. How far he accommodated the Nazi regime remains debated.\n\nTruth and Method argues that the human sciences have misunderstood themselves by modeling their claim to truth on scientific method. Understanding, Gadamer held, is a way of taking part in an event of meaning, closer to a conversation than to the application of a procedure. The experience of art comes first: the work draws us into its play, as players are taken up into a game, and it makes a claim to truth.\n\nAll understanding is shaped by prejudices, in the literal sense of prejudgments handed down by tradition. Gadamer rehabilitated prejudice and authority against the Enlightenment's prejudice against prejudice, arguing that our prejudgments open us to what we seek to understand as well as limit us, and that some authority rests on recognized knowledge. We are always within an effective history, a historically effected consciousness that we can never fully survey, and understanding happens as a fusion of the interpreter's horizon with that of the text.\n\nApplication belongs to understanding itself: a judge understands a law, and a preacher a text, only in applying it to a present case, which led Gadamer to Aristotle's practical wisdom (phronesis) as a model. Language is the medium in which understanding takes place, and the book's final part holds that being that can be understood is language.\n\nHabermas criticized Gadamer's defense of tradition as leaving too little ground for criticizing ideology, and their debate became a classic; a later encounter with Derrida was less fruitful. Gadamer's hermeneutics has been influential well beyond philosophy, in literary theory, theology, law, education, medicine and political thought.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/gadamer/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["lacan", "Jacques Lacan", "Lacan", "1901–1981", "French psychoanalyst who reread Freud through structural linguistics.", ["Écrits (1966)"], ["saussure", "kojeve", "levistrauss"], { x: "Jacques Lacan was born in Paris in 1901 and trained as a psychiatrist; his doctoral thesis of 1932 concerned paranoid psychosis. In the 1930s he attended Alexandre Kojève's lectures on Hegel. He broke with the Paris Psychoanalytic Society in 1953, helped found the Société française de psychanalyse, and began the seminar he gave for some twenty-seven years. In 1963 the International Psychoanalytical Association made his removal as a training analyst a condition of recognizing his group, chiefly because of his variable-length sessions, and he founded the École freudienne de Paris. His Écrits (1966) became a bestseller. He dissolved the École in 1980 and died in 1981.\n\nLacan called his project a return to Freud, read through the structural linguistics of Saussure and Roman Jakobson and the anthropology of Lévi-Strauss. His best-known formula is that the unconscious is structured like a language: it works through chains of signifiers, by the mechanisms of metaphor and metonymy, which Lacan matched with Freud's condensation and displacement.\n\nHis paper on the mirror stage, first given in 1936 and in its canonical version in 1949, describes infants between about six and eighteen months recognizing themselves in a mirror with jubilation, identifying with a unified image that contrasts with their actual helplessness. This identification is a misrecognition: the ego is formed as an alienated image, an object, and not the autonomous center of the self.\n\nLacan distinguished three registers. The Imaginary is the realm of images, the ego and its rivalries with others. The Symbolic is the order of language, law and culture, which he called the big Other, into which the child enters through the paternal function he named the Name-of-the-Father. The Real is what resists symbolization altogether, and his emphasis shifted toward it from around 1960. Desire is what remains when need is subtracted from demand; it is sustained by the objet petit a, the object-cause of desire that no actual object can satisfy, while jouissance names an enjoyment beyond the pleasure principle.\n\nHis seminar was attended by Jean Hyppolite, Michel Foucault, Luce Irigaray and Julia Kristeva, among many others, and his reading of Freud shaped a generation of French philosophy and, through debates over his account of feminine sexuality, much 1970s feminist thought. Later his work became central to film theory and to the philosophy of Slavoj Žižek.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lacan/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["kojeve", "Alexandre Kojève", "Kojève", "1902–1968", "Russian-French philosopher whose Paris lectures on Hegel (1933–39) shaped French thought.", ["Introduction to the Reading of Hegel (1947)"], ["marx", "hegel", "heidegger"], { x: "Alexandre Kojève was born Aleksandr Kozhevnikov in Moscow in 1902 into a wealthy family; the painter Wassily Kandinsky was his uncle. He left Soviet Russia in 1920, studied in Berlin and Heidelberg, and in 1926 completed a doctorate under Karl Jaspers on the religious philosophy of Vladimir Soloviev. He then settled in Paris, drawn into philosophy by Alexandre Koyré.\n\nFrom 1933 to 1939 he gave a seminar on Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit at the École pratique des hautes études. Its audience became legendary: Georges Bataille, Maurice Merleau-Ponty, Jacques Lacan, Raymond Aron, André Breton, Raymond Queneau, Michel Leiris and Éric Weil were among those who attended. Queneau edited the lectures from notes and transcripts, and they were published in 1947 as Introduction to the Reading of Hegel.\n\nKojève read Hegel through Marx and Heidegger and placed the dialectic of master and slave at the center of the Phenomenology. Human beings are distinguished from animals by desire directed at another's desire: the desire for recognition. In a struggle for recognition one consciousness risks its life and becomes master; the other, fearing death, becomes slave. Yet it is the slave who, through work that transforms the world, comes to self-consciousness and drives history forward.\n\nHistory, on Kojève's reading, ends when universal and equal recognition is achieved in a universal and homogeneous state, a process he dated essentially to the French Revolution and Napoleon. After the end of history violent struggle over principles is no longer needed. His account influenced Lacan's theory of desire and the mirror stage, Bataille's thought, and decades later Francis Fukuyama's thesis about the end of history, though Kojève's own version looked toward a synthesis of capitalism and socialism as much as the triumph of liberal democracy.\n\nAfter the Second World War Kojève became a senior official of the French Ministry of Economic Affairs, among the planners of the European Economic Community and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade. He carried on a lifelong debate with Leo Strauss about whether philosophers should guide politics, a role he defended and Strauss rejected. He died in Brussels in 1968, shortly after speaking to European officials on behalf of the French government.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre_Kojève", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["popper", "Karl Popper", "Popper", "1902–1994", "Austrian-British philosopher of science and critic of historicism.", ["The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1934)", "The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945)", "Conjectures and Refutations (1963)"], ["hume", "kant"], { x: "Karl Popper was born in Vienna in 1902 to parents of Jewish origin. Briefly a Marxist as a teenager, he was struck by the contrast between Einstein's theory, which made risky predictions, and the theories of Marx, Freud and Adler, which seemed able to explain anything. He took a doctorate in psychology in 1928, was in contact with but never a member of the Vienna Circle, and published Logik der Forschung in 1934 (in English, The Logic of Scientific Discovery, 1959). He taught in New Zealand during the Second World War, joined the London School of Economics in 1946, was knighted in 1965, and died in 1994.\n\nPopper's central problem was demarcation: what distinguishes science from non-science. His answer was falsifiability. A theory is scientific if it forbids some possible observations and so could be refuted by experience. There is an asymmetry between verification and falsification: no number of observations can verify a universal law, but a single genuine counter-instance can refute it.\n\nHe accepted Hume's argument that induction cannot be justified and held that science does not proceed by induction at all. It proceeds by conjectures and refutations: bold hypotheses are proposed and subjected to severe tests, and those that survive are corroborated, never proved. He tried to explain scientific progress through the idea of verisimilitude, closeness to the truth, though his formal definitions were shown to be flawed. In later work he proposed three worlds: physical things, mental states, and the objective contents of thought such as theories and arguments.\n\nIn The Poverty of Historicism and The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), written in New Zealand, he attacked historicism, the belief in laws of historical development that allow history to be predicted, and holism, and traced both to Plato, Hegel and Marx as intellectual sources of totalitarianism. He defended an open society, in which governments can be removed without bloodshed and policies are corrected by criticism, and favored piecemeal social engineering aimed at removing concrete suffering over utopian blueprints.\n\nPopper called his philosophy critical rationalism. Lakatos and Putnam objected that theories are rarely abandoned because of single falsifications, since auxiliary hypotheses protect them, and Kuhn's historical account of science displaced Popper's for many philosophers. Adolf Grünbaum disputed his dismissal of psychoanalysis as unscientific, and Popper himself came to concede that falsifiability alone cannot draw the line between science and non-science.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["adorno", "Theodor W. Adorno", "Adorno", "1903–1969", "Frankfurt School philosopher, sociologist and music theorist.", ["Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/1947, with Horkheimer)", "Minima Moralia (1951)", "Negative Dialectics (1966)", "Aesthetic Theory (1970)"], ["hegel", "marx", "lukacs", "benjamin"], { x: "Theodor W. Adorno was born in Frankfurt in 1903. He studied philosophy and music, took composition lessons with Alban Berg in Vienna, and in 1931 completed a habilitation on Kierkegaard's aesthetics. The Nazis stripped him of his right to teach, and he spent the next fifteen years in exile in Oxford, New York and southern California, working with the Institute for Social Research. He returned to Frankfurt in 1949, became director of the Institute in 1958, and was one of the most influential intellectuals in postwar West Germany, with Habermas among his assistants. He died in 1969, after bitter clashes with protesting students.\n\nWith Horkheimer he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944, revised 1947), whose chapter on the culture industry argued that film, radio and popular music had become standardized commodities that train their consumers in conformity while promising them pleasure. He also contributed to The Authoritarian Personality (1950), a study of the psychological roots of susceptibility to fascism, and wrote extensively on antisemitism.\n\nBuilding on Marx and Lukács, Adorno held that late capitalism had integrated society so thoroughly that criticism could no longer stand outside it, and had to proceed by immanent critique, measuring society and its ideas against their own claims. Minima Moralia (1951), a book of aphorisms from exile, declares that wrong life cannot be lived rightly.\n\nNegative Dialectics (1966) criticizes identity thinking, which subsumes particular things under general concepts as though the concept exhausted them, and attends to the nonidentical, what escapes conceptual grasp. It is a critique of Kant and Hegel from within, a dialectic that refuses to culminate in reconciliation. Adorno also proposed a new categorical imperative after Auschwitz: to arrange thought and action so that Auschwitz will not repeat itself, nothing similar will happen.\n\nAesthetic Theory, unfinished and published after his death (1970), treats autonomous modernist art as the social antithesis of society, whose truth content lies in its resistance to easy meaning and to the exchange relations around it. A trained musician, he championed Schoenberg and the Second Viennese School and was a harsh critic of commercial popular music. Poor early translations limited his reception in English, which has grown with newer translations since the 1990s.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/adorno/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["sartre", "Jean-Paul Sartre", "Sartre", "1905–1980", "French existentialist philosopher, novelist and political writer.", ["Being and Nothingness (1943)", "Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946)", "Critique of Dialectical Reason (1960)"], ["hegel", "husserl", "heidegger"], { x: "Jean-Paul Sartre was born in Paris in 1905 and studied at the École normale supérieure, where he met Simone de Beauvoir, his lifelong companion. He discovered phenomenology in the early 1930s and spent a year in Berlin studying Husserl. Novelist, playwright, critic and philosopher, he became after the Second World War the most famous public intellectual in the world, co-founded the review Les Temps modernes in 1945, and remained politically engaged until his death in 1980. He declined the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1964.\n\nHis early phenomenology took up Husserl's idea of intentionality in a realist spirit: consciousness is always consciousness of something outside itself. The Transcendence of the Ego (1936-37) argued that the self is an object met in reflection, with no hidden subject living inside consciousness, and The Imaginary (1940) tied imagination's power to negate the real to human freedom. His novel Nausea (1938) dramatized the contingency of existence.\n\nBeing and Nothingness (1943) distinguishes being-in-itself, the full, inert being of things, from being-for-itself, consciousness, which exists as a perpetual negation and lack. Human beings are condemned to be free: there is no human nature given in advance, existence precedes essence, and each person is wholly responsible for what he or she makes of a situation. Bad faith is the self-deception by which we flee this freedom, illustrated by the café waiter who plays at being a waiter as though it were his fixed nature. Under the look of another person I discover myself as an object, in shame, and relations with others tend toward conflict.\n\nIn place of Freud's unconscious Sartre proposed existential psychoanalysis, which seeks a person's fundamental project, the original free choice that gives a life its unity; he applied it to Baudelaire, Genet and, in the vast The Family Idiot (1971-72), Flaubert. His lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946) popularized his philosophy and drew him into debate with Heidegger.\n\nThe Critique of Dialectical Reason (1960) tried to join existentialism with Marxism. Human praxis is constrained by the practico-inert, the sediment of past action in matter and institutions; under scarcity people live in seriality, anonymous and isolated like a queue at a bus stop, and can break out in the group in fusion, which tends in turn to harden into institutions. Sartre opposed colonialism and racism, wrote on antisemitism and négritude, and prefaced Fanon's The Wretched of the Earth. Interest in his philosophy waned from the 1970s and has revived since the 2000s.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sartre/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["arendt", "Hannah Arendt", "Arendt", "1906–1975", "German-American political theorist of totalitarianism, action and judgment.", ["The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951)", "The Human Condition (1958)", "Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963)"], ["augustine", "kant", "heidegger"], { x: "Hannah Arendt was born in 1906 into a secular Jewish family in Hanover and grew up in Königsberg. She studied with Heidegger at Marburg, heard Husserl at Freiburg, and wrote her doctorate on Augustine's concept of love under Karl Jaspers at Heidelberg (1929). She fled Germany in 1933, worked for Jewish refugee organizations in Paris, and reached New York in 1941. She later taught at the University of Chicago and the New School for Social Research, and died in New York in 1975.\n\nThe Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) traced antisemitism and imperialism as the elements that crystallized into Nazism and Stalinism, and argued that totalitarianism was a new form of government, built on ideology and terror, that broke with all earlier political categories. Its aim was total domination: to make human beings superfluous, as the camps did.\n\nThe Human Condition (1958) distinguishes three basic activities. Labor meets the needs of biological life and leaves nothing lasting behind; work fabricates a durable world of objects; action, together with speech, discloses who people are and begins something new among others. Action rests on plurality, the fact that people, not Man, inhabit the earth, and on natality, the capacity to begin that comes with each birth. Arendt argued that modernity has lost the public world in which action appears, through the rise of the social and the dominance of labor and consumption.\n\nPower, on her account, arises when people act in concert and rests on persuasion and consent; violence can destroy power but never create it. On Revolution (1963) contrasted the American revolution's founding of freedom with the French revolution's absorption in the social question, and she favored councils and participatory citizenship over mere representation. Her report on the trial of Adolf Eichmann, Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963), described the banality of evil: monstrous deeds done by a thoughtless functionary who was neither demonic nor deeply ideological. The book, and its remarks on the Jewish councils, provoked bitter controversy.\n\nIn her last work, The Life of the Mind (1978), she examined thinking and willing; the volume on judging was never written. She linked thinking, the silent dialogue with oneself, to conscience, and drew on Kant's account of aesthetic judgment for a theory of political judgment that thinks from the standpoint of others. She resists classification as liberal, conservative or socialist, and is often placed closest to civic republicanism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/arendt/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["godel", "Kurt Gödel", "Gödel", "1906–1978", "Austrian-American logician of the completeness and incompleteness theorems.", ["“On Formally Undecidable Propositions” (1931)"], [], { x: "Kurt Gödel was born in 1906 in Brünn, in Moravia, now Brno in the Czech Republic. He studied at the University of Vienna, attended meetings of the Vienna Circle without sharing its positivism, and received his doctorate under Hans Hahn in 1929. After Hahn's death, the murder of Moritz Schlick and the cancellation of his university appointment, he emigrated with his wife Adele to the United States in 1940 and joined the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, where he stayed until he retired in 1976. He was a close friend and daily walking companion of Albert Einstein. He died in Princeton in 1978.\n\nHis dissertation proved the completeness of first-order logic: every logically valid formula of first-order logic can be proved from its axioms, so that in this logic validity and provability coincide. With its consequence, the compactness theorem, this became a basic tool of model theory.\n\nThe incompleteness theorems of 1931 changed the foundations of mathematics. The first shows that any consistent formal system strong enough to express elementary arithmetic contains statements that can be neither proved nor refuted within it. The second shows that such a system cannot prove its own consistency. Together they showed that Hilbert's program, which sought a complete axiomatization of mathematics proved consistent by finitary means, could not be carried out as intended.\n\nBetween 1938 and 1940 he proved that the axiom of choice and the continuum hypothesis are consistent with the standard axioms of set theory, using the constructible universe L, a construction that later independence proofs, such as Paul Cohen's, built upon. He also contributed to intuitionistic logic, and in 1949 found solutions to Einstein's field equations describing a rotating universe in which travel into the past is possible.\n\nPhilosophically Gödel was a mathematical Platonist and a rationalist. He held that mathematical objects exist independently of us and that we perceive them through a kind of mathematical intuition, and he hoped to make philosophy an exact science, turning in his later years to Husserl's phenomenology. He also wrote a formal version of the ontological argument for the existence of God, which he showed to Dana Scott in 1970.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goedel/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["goodman", "Nelson Goodman", "Goodman", "1906–1998", "American philosopher of induction, language and art.", ["Fact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955)", "Languages of Art (1968)", "Ways of Worldmaking (1978)"], [], { x: "Nelson Goodman was born in Somerville, Massachusetts, in 1906 and studied at Harvard, where he completed his doctorate only in 1941, having spent years running an art gallery in Boston. He taught at Tufts, the University of Pennsylvania and Brandeis before returning to Harvard in 1968, where he founded Project Zero, a research program on education in the arts. He died in 1998.\n\nGoodman rejected the idea of an indubitable given in experience and any principled distinction between analytic and synthetic truths. He was a nominalist: with Quine he attempted a constructive nominalism that does without abstract entities such as classes, and with Henry Leonard he developed the calculus of individuals, a theory of parts and wholes. The Structure of Appearance (1951), a revision of his dissertation, built a constructional system in the tradition of Carnap's Aufbau, holding that definitions need only preserve structure.\n\nFact, Fiction, and Forecast (1955) argued that the old problem of induction, how to justify it, is a pseudo-problem: rules of inference are justified by being brought into agreement with the particular inferences we accept, and accepted inferences by agreement with the rules, a mutual adjustment later called reflective equilibrium. The real problem is a new riddle. Define “grue” as applying to things examined before a certain time if green and to other things if blue; every emerald examined so far is both green and grue, yet only “all emeralds are green” is a reasonable projection. Goodman argued that what makes a predicate projectible is its entrenchment, its record of successful use in past projections.\n\nLanguages of Art (1968) developed a general theory of symbols covering pictures, music, dance and literature. It distinguished denotation from exemplification, in which a sample refers to properties it possesses, set out the symptoms of the aesthetic, and distinguished autographic arts, such as painting, in which even a perfect copy is a forgery, from allographic arts, such as music, in which any correct performance of the score is a genuine instance.\n\nWays of Worldmaking (1978) defended irrealism: there are many true but conflicting world-versions, which cannot all be reconciled within a single world, and worlds are made by making versions, as we make the Big Dipper by grouping stars. Goodman's work shaped philosophy of science, language and art, and his new riddle remains a central problem in the theory of confirmation.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/goodman/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["levinas", "Emmanuel Levinas", "Levinas", "1906–1995", "Lithuanian-French philosopher of ethics as responsibility to the other.", ["Totality and Infinity (1961)", "Otherwise than Being (1974)"], ["husserl", "heidegger"], { x: "Emmanuel Levinas was born in Kaunas, Lithuania, then part of the Russian empire, into a Jewish family. He studied philosophy in Strasbourg from 1923, where he became a lifelong friend of Maurice Blanchot, and in 1928-29 studied with Husserl in Freiburg and attended Heidegger's seminar. His thesis on Husserl's theory of intuition (1930) was the first book-length introduction to Husserl in French. He became a French citizen in 1939, was captured as a French officer in 1940 and spent the war in a German prisoner-of-war camp; his family in Lithuania was murdered.\n\nAfter the war he directed the École normale israélite orientale in Paris and from 1957 gave Talmudic readings. He taught at Poitiers, at Nanterre and from 1973 at the Sorbonne, and died in Paris in 1995.\n\nHis early works, On Escape (1935), Existence and Existents (1947) and Time and the Other (1947), describe the anonymous, impersonal fact of being, the “there is” (il y a), experienced in insomnia and darkness, and the subject's need to escape it. They point toward the other person, and toward death as the encounter with what cannot be mastered.\n\nTotality and Infinity (1961) argues that ethics is first philosophy. Western thought, from ontology to the state, tends toward totality, a system that absorbs everything other into the same. The face of the other person breaks this: it addresses me with a demand, “you shall not kill,” that interrupts my freedom and self-sufficiency and exceeds every idea I can form of it, as the idea of the infinite does. My responsibility for the other comes before choice, knowledge or reflection.\n\nOtherwise than Being, or Beyond Essence (1974) radicalizes this through substitution, a responsibility for the other so deep that I am held hostage, one-for-the-other, and through the distinction between the Saying, the exposure and address to another that makes speech possible, and the Said, the content of what is spoken. Levinas drew on Heidegger's analysis of being-in-the-world while criticizing his fundamental ontology as forgetting the other. Derrida's critical engagement with his work, from 1964 on, did much to make Levinas a major presence in French and later Anglophone philosophy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/levinas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["senghor", "Léopold Sédar Senghor", "Senghor", "1906–2001", "Senegalese poet and theorist of négritude, first president of Senegal.", ["Anthology of New Black and Malagasy Poetry (ed., 1948)"], [], { x: "Léopold Sédar Senghor was born in 1906 in Joal, Senegal, then part of French West Africa, into a prosperous Serer family. He was educated in Catholic schools and a seminary in Dakar, went to Paris in 1928, studied at the Lycée Louis-le-Grand and the Sorbonne, and in 1935 became one of the first Africans to pass the agrégation. He taught in French lycées until the war. Enlisting in 1939, he was captured in 1940 and spent two years in German prisoner-of-war camps, where he wrote poetry.\n\nIn Paris in the 1930s Senghor, Aimé Césaire and Léon-Gontran Damas created négritude, a literary and intellectual movement that affirmed the value of African and Black cultures, characteristics and aesthetics against French cultural domination and the colonial claim that Africa had no culture comparable to Europe's. The movement reclaimed the slur “nègre” as a term of pride. Senghor's version emphasized the dialogue and eventual mixing of cultures, and he argued that Africa and Europe shared a cultural continuum reaching from ancient Egypt through Greece and Rome.\n\nHis poetry, from Chants d'ombre (1945) and Hosties noires (1948) to Éthiopiques (1956) and Nocturnes (1961), made him one of the major French-language poets of the century. In 1948 he edited the Anthology of New Black and Malagasy Poetry in French, for which Jean-Paul Sartre wrote the famous preface “Black Orpheus.” His essays were collected in the five volumes of Liberté (1964-93).\n\nSenghor was elected to the French National Assembly after the war, co-founded the Senegalese Democratic Bloc in 1948, and developed an African socialism grounded in négritude as an alternative to Marxism. He favored federation over small independent states, and led the short-lived Mali Federation. From 1960 to 1980 he was the first president of independent Senegal. His rule had authoritarian features: in 1962 he had his prime minister Mamadou Dia arrested, Dia spent twelve years in prison, and by 1966 Senegal was a one-party state, before limited multiparty politics returned in 1976.\n\nHe retired voluntarily in 1980, handing power to Abdou Diouf, and in 1983 became the first African elected to the Académie française. He died in France in 2001. Critics of négritude, among them a later generation of African writers and philosophers, objected that Senghor's account of a distinct African sensibility reproduced colonial stereotypes, while defenders read it as a strategic affirmation of cultures that colonialism had denied.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Léopold_Sédar_Senghor", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["tempels", "Placide Tempels", "Tempels", "1906–1977", "Belgian Franciscan missionary in the Belgian Congo.", ["Bantu Philosophy (1945)"], [], { x: "Placide Tempels was born Frans Tempels in Berlaar, Belgium, in 1906, took the name Placide on entering the Franciscan order in 1924, and was ordained a priest in 1930. In 1933 he was sent as a missionary to the Belgian Congo, where he worked for about twenty-nine years. He returned to Belgium in 1962 and died in a Franciscan monastery in Hasselt in 1977.\n\nHis book Bantu Philosophy grew out of his missionary work among the Baluba. Written in Dutch and published first in journal chapters, it appeared as a book in French in 1945, translated with the encouragement of his friend Émile Possoz, a magistrate in the Congo. Tempels revised the translation for the Présence Africaine edition of 1949, the basis of most later translations, including the English edition of 1959.\n\nTempels argued that Bantu thought had a coherent philosophical system and that its primary ontological category is vital force. Western thought treats static being as primary and force as an attribute of beings; for the Bantu, he claimed, being is force. The world forms a hierarchy of forces, from God through the ancestors and the living to animals, plants and things, which constantly influence one another, and the highest value is to increase one's vital force.\n\nThe book was written for missionaries and colonial officials, to help them understand the people they sought to convert and govern, and its framing reflects that purpose. Aimé Césaire mocked it in his Discourse on Colonialism. Yet by claiming that a philosophy existed in African thought at a time when this was widely denied, it had a large influence and started a debate among African philosophers, including Alexis Kagame, who developed a related account of Bantu-Rwandan philosophy.\n\nPaulin Hountondji made Tempels the founding example of what he called ethnophilosophy: the presentation of a people's collective, anonymous worldview as a philosophy, without individual thinkers who argue and criticize. Henry Odera Oruka's sage philosophy was in part a response, an attempt to find critical, individual philosophical reflection within traditional African communities. Tempels's book thus stands at the start of the modern debate over what African philosophy is.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placide_Tempels", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bantu_Philosophy", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Bantu Philosophy”" }, { src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" }] }],
  ["hart", "H. L. A. Hart", "Hart", "1907–1992", "British legal philosopher of the rule of recognition.", ["The Concept of Law (1961)"], ["bentham", "johnaustin", "wittgenstein"], { x: "H. L. A. Hart was born in Harrogate, Yorkshire, in 1907 into a Jewish family; his father was a tailor. He took a first in classics at New College, Oxford, and practiced as a barrister at the Chancery bar from 1932 to 1940. During the Second World War he worked in British intelligence, with MI5 and at Bletchley Park. After the war he turned to philosophy, taught at New College, and in 1952 became Professor of Jurisprudence at Oxford, a chair he held until 1969. He later served as principal of Brasenose College and died in 1992.\n\nThe Concept of Law (1961), his most famous book, revived legal positivism by replacing John Austin's command theory. Austin's model of orders backed by threats, Hart argued, cannot explain rules that confer powers, such as those for making contracts, wills or legislation, nor the continuity of law when a sovereign dies, nor the way law binds lawmakers themselves.\n\nLaw, on Hart's account, is the union of primary rules, which impose duties on conduct, and secondary rules, which are rules about rules: rules of change, which say how primary rules can be made and altered; rules of adjudication, which empower courts; and above all the rule of recognition, the practice of officials that sets the criteria for what counts as valid law in a system. Such rules exist because officials accept them from an internal point of view, as standards for criticism and justification, which distinguishes having an obligation from merely being obliged by force.\n\nHart held that legal language has an open texture, so that in hard cases at the edges of rules judges must exercise discretion, and that a minimum content of natural law follows from basic facts of human vulnerability and scarcity, though the validity of law does not depend on its morality. In the Hart-Fuller debate of 1958 he defended the separation of law and morality against Lon Fuller, and in Law, Liberty, and Morality (1963) he argued against Lord Devlin that the criminal law should not be used to enforce conventional morality, supporting the decriminalization of homosexuality recommended by the Wolfenden report.\n\nHis other works include Causation in the Law (1959, with Tony Honoré), Punishment and Responsibility (1968) and Essays on Bentham (1982). Ronald Dworkin, his successor at Oxford, became his chief critic, and Hart replied in a posthumous postscript to The Concept of Law. His students and interlocutors included Joseph Raz, John Finnis and John Rawls.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H._L._A._Hart", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lawphil-nature/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “The Nature of Law”" }] }],
  ["beauvoir", "Simone de Beauvoir", "Beauvoir", "1908–1986", "French existentialist philosopher and feminist.", ["The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947)", "The Second Sex (1949)"], ["hegel", "husserl", "sartre"], { x: "Simone de Beauvoir was born in Paris in 1908 into a bourgeois Catholic family, lost her faith as a teenager, and studied philosophy at the Sorbonne. In the agrégation of 1929 she placed second, after Jean-Paul Sartre, who became her lifelong companion in an open relationship. She taught in lycées until she was dismissed during the German occupation, co-founded Les Temps modernes in 1945, and won the Prix Goncourt for her novel The Mandarins in 1954. She died in Paris in 1986.\n\nHer philosophical novel She Came to Stay (1943) explored the conflict of consciousnesses, and the essays Pyrrhus and Cinéas (1944) and The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947) set out an existentialist ethics. Human existence is ambiguous, at once free subject and material object, transcendence and facticity. My freedom needs the freedom of others, since my projects require others who take them up, so to will oneself free is also to will others free. Her ethics treats oppression as the denial of another's freedom and acknowledges that violence is sometimes a tragic feature of political life.\n\nThe Second Sex (1949) asks what a woman is. Man has defined himself as the subject, the absolute, and woman as the Other, relative to him, and this asymmetry runs through biology, psychoanalysis and historical materialism as accounts of women's situation, through myths of femininity, and through the lived experience of girls and women from childhood to old age. Its most famous sentence, “one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman,” is widely credited with introducing the distinction between sex and gender.\n\nWomen, on Beauvoir's account, have been confined to immanence, repetition and maintenance of life, and denied the transcendence of projects that open a future, sometimes with their own complicity. Liberation requires economic independence and a transformed situation, and reciprocal recognition between women and men.\n\nHer later works include a travel book on China, a campaign on behalf of Djamila Boupacha, an Algerian woman tortured by French soldiers, A Very Easy Death on her mother's death, Old Age (1970) on society's treatment of the elderly, and several volumes of memoirs. Long read as Sartre's follower, she has been established by feminist scholarship as an original philosopher in her own right, while critics have examined the limits of The Second Sex on race and colonialism.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/beauvoir/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["merleauponty", "Maurice Merleau-Ponty", "Merleau-Ponty", "1908–1961", "French phenomenologist of perception and the lived body.", ["Phenomenology of Perception (1945)", "The Visible and the Invisible (1964)"], ["husserl", "heidegger"], { x: "Maurice Merleau-Ponty was born in Rochefort-sur-Mer in 1908 and studied at the École normale supérieure, where he became friends with Sartre and Beauvoir. In 1945 he co-founded Les Temps modernes with them and was its political editor until he broke with Sartre in the early 1950s over communism. He held the chair of child psychology and pedagogy at the Sorbonne and from 1952 the chair of philosophy at the Collège de France. He died suddenly in Paris in 1961, at fifty-three. He was the leading academic phenomenologist of postwar France.\n\nThe Structure of Behavior (1942) and Phenomenology of Perception (1945) take perception as the starting point for understanding mind and body, world and experience. Merleau-Ponty criticized two traditions, empiricism, which builds perception out of sensations, and intellectualism, which makes it a judgment of the mind, for sharing a common error: both presuppose a ready-made, fully determinate world, and so miss how the world first takes shape in perception.\n\nThe subject of perception is the lived body. Through its body schema, its practical sense of its own posture and powers, the body inhabits space and is directed toward things in a motor intentionality that precedes reflective thought, as when a typist's fingers know the keyboard. Merleau-Ponty drew on psychology and neurology, especially the case of Schneider, a brain-damaged patient who could perform concrete habitual movements but not abstract ones such as pointing on command. Perception is ambiguous and open, and meaning is always embodied.\n\nHis political essays, Humanism and Terror (1947) and Adventures of the Dialectic (1955), moved from a qualified defense of Soviet communism to a critique of it, and of Sartre's politics. In Signs and in “Eye and Mind” he wrote on language, expression and painting, above all Cézanne, as ways in which meaning is born.\n\nThe unfinished The Visible and the Invisible, published in 1964, began a new ontology built around flesh, an element common to the perceiver and the perceived, and the chiasm, the intertwining of seeing and being seen, touching and being touched. Post-structuralists such as Foucault, Deleuze and Derrida distanced themselves from him, but his influence has grown in feminist and critical phenomenology, embodied cognitive science and environmental philosophy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/merleau-ponty/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["levistrauss", "Claude Lévi-Strauss", "Lévi-Strauss", "1908–2009", "French anthropologist who founded structural anthropology.", ["The Elementary Structures of Kinship (1949)", "Structural Anthropology (1958)", "The Savage Mind (1962)"], ["saussure", "mauss"], { x: "Claude Lévi-Strauss was born in Brussels in 1908 to French Jewish parents and grew up in Paris. He studied philosophy and law and passed the agrégation in philosophy in 1931. From 1935 to 1939 he taught at the University of São Paulo and carried out fieldwork in the Brazilian interior, among peoples such as the Nambikwara and the Bororo. Fleeing Vichy France, he spent most of the war in New York, where he met the linguist Roman Jakobson and the circle of Franz Boas. He returned to Paris, held a chair at the Collège de France from 1959 to 1982, was elected to the Académie française in 1973, and died in 2009, at a hundred.\n\nFrom Jakobson he took the methods of structural linguistics, derived from Saussure, and applied them to culture: anthropology should study the relations among elements of a system, such as kinship terms or myths, more than the elements themselves, and should look for the unconscious structures that generate the visible variety of human customs.\n\nThe Elementary Structures of Kinship (1949) argued that kinship rests on alliance, the exchange of women between groups, more than on descent. The incest taboo, found in every society, is the rule that compels this exchange, and so marks the passage from nature to culture. Structural Anthropology (1958) set out the method in essays, and the memoir Tristes Tropiques (1955) made him a public intellectual.\n\nThe Savage Mind (1962) argued that mythical thought is as rigorous as scientific thought, working by a different logic of the concrete. The mythmaker is a bricoleur, who makes new arrangements from materials already at hand, in contrast with the engineer, who designs from a plan. Its final chapter attacked Sartre's philosophy of history as one more myth. The four volumes of Mythologiques (1964-71) analyzed hundreds of myths from the Americas, breaking them into units he called mythemes and tracing how they work through and mediate binary oppositions such as raw and cooked.\n\nLévi-Strauss made structuralism the dominant intellectual movement in France in the 1960s, and his work influenced Lacan, Barthes and Althusser and provoked the post-structuralist critiques of Derrida, whose essay “Structure, Sign, and Play” took him as its main subject. Critics in anthropology questioned the extent of his fieldwork and the testability of his claims about myth.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Lévi-Strauss", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["quine", "W. V. O. Quine", "Quine", "1908–2000", "American logician who attacked the analytic–synthetic distinction and naturalized epistemology.", ["“On What There Is” (1948)", "“Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951)", "Word and Object (1960)"], ["russell", "carnap"], { x: "Willard Van Orman Quine was born in Akron, Ohio, in 1908. He studied mathematics at Oberlin and wrote his Harvard doctoral dissertation on Whitehead and Russell's Principia Mathematica. A traveling fellowship in 1932-33 took him to Vienna, Warsaw and Prague, where he met Carnap, whose friend and chief critic he became. He spent his whole career at Harvard and died in 2000.\n\nHis naturalism holds that there is no first philosophy, no standpoint outside science from which to justify it. Philosophy is continuous with science, and epistemology, in his phrase, falls into place as a chapter of psychology: the study of how creatures like us get from the stimulation of our sensory surfaces to our theories of the world.\n\n“Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951) attacked two assumptions of logical empiricism: the distinction between analytic truths, true by meaning alone, and synthetic truths, true by fact, and the reductionist idea that each statement has its own empirical content. Our statements face the tribunal of experience as a corporate body. Our beliefs form a web, touching experience only at the edges, and no statement, not even of logic, is immune to revision if experience requires adjustments elsewhere.\n\nIn ontology Quine held that a theory is committed to the entities its variables must range over for it to be true, summed up in the slogan to be is to be the value of a variable, from “On What There Is” (1948). Regimenting science in first-order logic, he accepted physical objects and sets and rejected properties, propositions and meanings as entities.\n\nWord and Object (1960) argued for the indeterminacy of translation. A linguist translating a wholly unknown language, who hears “gavagai” when a rabbit runs by, could translate it as “rabbit,” “undetached rabbit part” or “rabbit stage,” and all the evidence of speakers' behavior would fit equally well; there is no fact of the matter about which is right. He also argued that theories are underdetermined by all possible evidence. His attack on logical empiricism reshaped analytic philosophy after the war.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/quine/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["austin", "J. L. Austin", "Austin", "1911–1960", "Oxford philosopher of ordinary language and speech acts.", ["How to Do Things with Words (1962)", "Sense and Sensibilia (1962)"], [], { x: "John Langshaw Austin was born in Lancaster in 1911, studied classics at Shrewsbury and Balliol College, Oxford, and became a fellow of All Souls and then of Magdalen. In the Second World War he served in British intelligence, rising to lieutenant colonel, and was credited with much of the accuracy of the intelligence for D-Day. In 1952 he became White's Professor of Moral Philosophy at Oxford. He gave the William James Lectures at Harvard in 1955 and died in Oxford in 1960, at forty-eight. He published little; How to Do Things with Words (1962) and Sense and Sensibilia (1962) were reconstructed from his lecture notes.\n\nAustin is usually grouped with the ordinary language philosophers, though the label fits him imperfectly. He held that ordinary words embody the distinctions that generations of speakers have found worth drawing, so that careful attention to what we would say when is a good starting point for philosophy, and that philosophers' claims should meet the ordinary standards of accuracy they so often ignore.\n\nHow to Do Things with Words began from a contrast between constative utterances, which describe and are true or false, and performatives, such as “I promise” or “I name this ship,” which do something and are happy or unhappy. Performatives can misfire, when the conventional procedure is not correctly carried out, or be abuses, when, for example, a promise is insincere.\n\nAustin then argued that the distinction breaks down, since stating is itself an act, and replaced it with a general theory of speech acts. In saying anything we perform a locutionary act, uttering words with a meaning; an illocutionary act, the act done in saying them, such as warning, stating or promising, with its force; and often a perlocutionary act, achieved by saying them, such as persuading or alarming someone.\n\nIn Sense and Sensibilia he attacked sense-data theory, especially A. J. Ayer's, and the argument from illusion, arguing that its key terms were confused, and in “A Plea for Excuses” he studied excuses as a route into the theory of action. His speech act theory was developed by John Searle and challenged by Jacques Derrida, and it underlies later work on performativity, including Judith Butler's.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/austin-jl/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["sellars", "Wilfrid Sellars", "Sellars", "1912–1989", "American philosopher who attacked the “myth of the given”.", ["“Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind” (1956)"], ["kant"], { x: "Wilfrid Sellars was born in 1912 in Ann Arbor, Michigan, the son of the philosopher Roy Wood Sellars, and studied at Michigan, Buffalo and, as a Rhodes Scholar, at Oxford. He taught at Iowa, Minnesota and Yale, and from 1963 until his death in 1989 at the University of Pittsburgh. His work spans epistemology, philosophy of mind, language, science and ethics.\n\n“Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind” (1956) attacked what Sellars called the myth of the given: the idea that some cognitive states, such as sense impressions, can both be independent of all other knowledge and serve as its foundation. To know something, even “this is green,” is to place it in the logical space of reasons, of justifying and being able to justify what one says, which requires a whole network of concepts. Knowledge, he wrote, is a self-correcting enterprise that can put any claim in jeopardy, though not all at once.\n\nThe same essay tells a myth of its own, about a genius named Jones who introduces the concept of inner thoughts as theoretical entities to explain his fellows' intelligent behavior, modeled on their overt speech. Psychological concepts, on this account, work like theoretical concepts in science, and our privileged access to our own thoughts is learned. Meaning, for Sellars, is a functional role within a linguistic system, defined by the inferences an expression licenses, and is not a relation between words and objects.\n\n“Philosophy and the Scientific Image of Man” (1962) distinguished the manifest image, the framework of persons and colored, solid things in which we understand ourselves, from the scientific image of the entities postulated by theoretical science. Sellars held that in describing and explaining the world science is the measure of what is and what is not, yet the framework of persons, intentions and norms cannot simply be eliminated; philosophy seeks a stereoscopic vision that joins the two images.\n\nHis followers divide into right-wing Sellarsians, such as Paul and Patricia Churchland, who stress his scientific realism, and left-wing Sellarsians, such as Richard Rorty, John McDowell and Robert Brandom, who stress the irreducibility of the normative space of reasons; the latter group made Pittsburgh the center of a revival of interest in his work.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/sellars/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["camus", "Albert Camus", "Camus", "1913–1960", "French-Algerian writer and philosopher of the absurd.", ["The Myth of Sisyphus (1942)", "The Rebel (1951)"], ["kierkegaard", "nietzsche"], { x: "Albert Camus was born in 1913 in Mondovi, in French Algeria, and grew up poor in Algiers. Tuberculosis, which struck him as a young man, ended his hopes of an academic career and kept him from military service. He studied philosophy at the University of Algiers, writing a thesis on Plotinus and Augustine, joined the Algerian branch of the Communist Party in the mid-1930s and was expelled for refusing to follow its line on colonialism. As a journalist he reported on famine in Kabylie in 1939. During the occupation he joined the Resistance and in 1944 became editor of Combat, its main non-Communist newspaper. He received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957 and died in a car accident in January 1960, at forty-six.\n\nHis essay The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), written alongside the novel The Stranger (1942), begins with what Camus called the one truly serious philosophical problem: whether life is worth living, and so whether to commit suicide. The absurd arises from the confrontation between the human demand for meaning and clarity and a world that remains silent.\n\nCamus rejected both physical suicide and what he called philosophical suicide, the leap into hope or transcendence by which Kierkegaard, Shestov, Jaspers and even Husserl evaded the absurd after recognizing it. The right response is to live in full awareness of the absurd, with revolt, freedom and passion. Sisyphus, condemned to push his rock forever, becomes the absurd hero, and we must imagine him happy.\n\nThe Rebel (1951) turned from the individual to history. Rebellion begins with a no that also affirms a limit, a value shared with others, but revolutions that claim to fulfill history have repeatedly turned rebellion into murder. Camus argued that Marxism and Communism justified killing in the name of a historical future, and that genuine rebellion must respect limits; he opposed the death penalty and defended freedom of speech as tests of any revolution. The book's attack on revolutionary violence led to a bitter public break with Sartre.\n\nHis other works include the novels The Plague (1947) and The Fall (1956) and plays such as Caligula. Camus refused the label existentialist, which he associated with the thinkers he criticized for abandoning the absurd. His position on the Algerian War, opposing both colonial injustice and the violence of independence, isolated him in his last years.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/camus/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["ricoeur", "Paul Ricoeur", "Ricoeur", "1913–2005", "French philosopher of hermeneutics, narrative and memory.", ["Freud and Philosophy (1965)", "Time and Narrative (1983–85)"], ["husserl", "heidegger", "gadamer"], { x: "Paul Ricoeur was born in Valence in 1913. His mother died soon after his birth and his father was killed in the First World War, and he was raised by relatives in Rennes in a Protestant household. Captured in 1940, he spent the war in German prisoner-of-war camps, where he read Jaspers and translated Husserl's Ideas I into French. He taught at Strasbourg, the Sorbonne and Nanterre, where he served as dean in the turmoil after 1968, and at the University of Chicago from 1970 to 1992. He died in 2005.\n\nHis philosophy is an anthropology of the capable human being: a person who can speak, act, narrate and take responsibility, and who is also vulnerable and fallible. His first major project, Freedom and Nature (1950), used phenomenology to describe the voluntary and involuntary sides of human existence. Fallible Man and The Symbolism of Evil (1960) treated evil as a possibility rooted in human disproportion, the gap between our finitude and our aspirations, and studied the symbols and myths, of stain, sin and guilt, through which people confess it. The symbol, he wrote, gives rise to thought.\n\nThis led Ricoeur to conclude that self-understanding comes through the long detour of interpreting signs, symbols and texts, never through direct introspection. In Freud and Philosophy (1965) he distinguished a hermeneutics that seeks to recover meaning from a hermeneutics of suspicion, practiced by the three “masters of suspicion,” Marx, Nietzsche and Freud, which unmasks the illusions of consciousness; interpretation must hold the two together. He used structuralism as a method of explanation while rejecting it as a complete account of meaning.\n\nThe Rule of Metaphor (1975) argued that living metaphor creates new meaning and redescribes reality, and the three volumes of Time and Narrative (1983-85) argued that narrative, by organizing events into a plot, makes human time intelligible. Oneself as Another (1990) developed the idea of narrative identity, distinguishing identity as sameness (idem) from selfhood as constancy through change (ipse), exemplified in keeping a promise.\n\nIts “little ethics” defines the ethical aim as aiming at the good life, with and for others, in just institutions. Ricoeur's late works, Memory, History, Forgetting (2000) and The Course of Recognition (2004), examined the uses and abuses of memory, the limits of forgiveness and the forms of mutual recognition. His work spans phenomenology, hermeneutics, analytic philosophy of action and theology.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ricoeur/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["cesaire", "Aimé Césaire", "Césaire", "1913–2008", "Martinican poet and politician, a founder of négritude.", ["Notebook of a Return to the Native Land (1939)", "Discourse on Colonialism (1950)"], [], { x: "Aimé Césaire was born in 1913 in Basse-Pointe, Martinique. A scholarship took him to the Lycée Louis-le-Grand in Paris, where he met Léopold Sédar Senghor and Léon-Gontran Damas, and in 1935 he entered the École normale supérieure. That year the three launched the review L'Étudiant noir, whose manifestos began the movement of négritude; Césaire coined the word.\n\nHis long poem Notebook of a Return to the Native Land, written in the 1930s and first published in 1939, is the founding text of négritude: a descent into the misery of colonial Martinique that turns into an affirmation of Black identity and history. In 1939 he returned to Martinique to teach at the Lycée Schoelcher, where Frantz Fanon was among his students, and in 1941 founded the review Tropiques with his wife, Suzanne Césaire. André Breton, who met him in Martinique during the war, championed his poetry as a high point of surrealism.\n\nDiscourse on Colonialism (1950) argued that colonization dehumanizes the colonizer as well as the colonized, and reduces the colonized to things, a process Césaire called thingification. Its most famous argument is that Nazism was colonialism turned back on Europe: Europe tolerated, even absolved, the methods of colonial violence so long as they were applied to non-European peoples, and Hitler's crime in European eyes was to apply them to Europeans. The essay also attacked the scholars and missionaries, Placide Tempels among them, who served the colonial project.\n\nCésaire was elected mayor of Fort-de-France in 1945 and held the office for more than fifty years, and he sat in the French National Assembly for decades. He championed the 1946 law that made Martinique an overseas department of France, a choice that later critics of assimilation questioned. In 1956, after the Soviet suppression of the Hungarian revolution, he left the French Communist Party with a public letter to its leader, Maurice Thorez, and in 1958 founded the Martinican Progressive Party.\n\nHis plays include The Tragedy of King Christophe (1963) and A Tempest (1969), a rewriting of Shakespeare from the standpoint of Caliban. Fanon called him a mentor, and his work is foundational for Caribbean, African and postcolonial thought. He died in Fort-de-France in 2008, and the island's airport now bears his name.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aimé_Césaire", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["barthes", "Roland Barthes", "Barthes", "1915–1980", "French semiotician and literary theorist.", ["Mythologies (1957)", "“The Death of the Author” (1967)", "S/Z (1970)"], ["saussure"], { x: "Roland Barthes was born in Cherbourg in 1915. His father, a naval officer, was killed in the First World War before Barthes was a year old, and he was raised by his mother, aunt and grandmother in Bayonne, then in Paris. Tuberculosis interrupted his studies for years and kept him out of the war. He studied classics at the Sorbonne, held posts in Romania and Egypt, worked for the CNRS, taught at the École pratique des hautes études, and in 1977 took the chair of literary semiology at the Collège de France. In February 1980 he was struck by a laundry van while walking home and died a month later.\n\nWriting Degree Zero (1953) argued that “writing,” the style a writer adopts, is a historically situated choice shaped by conventions. Mythologies (1957) gathered short essays on French popular culture, wrestling, wine, a new Citroën, detergent advertisements, and ended with an essay on myth as a second-order system of signification: a myth takes a sign, with its literal meaning, and makes it the signifier of a further, ideological meaning, so that what is historical and bourgeois comes to look natural.\n\nIn the 1960s Barthes was a leading structuralist, applying Saussure's linguistics to culture in Elements of Semiology and The Fashion System, and distinguishing denotation from the connotations layered on top of it. His essay “The Death of the Author” (1967) argued that a text's meaning is not fixed by its author's intentions: the author gives way to a scriptor who recombines existing writing, and the birth of the reader must be at the cost of the death of the author.\n\nS/Z (1970), a line-by-line reading of Balzac's story Sarrasine, distinguished readerly texts, which offer fixed meanings for passive consumption, from writerly texts, which make the reader a producer of meaning. The Pleasure of the Text (1973) contrasted plaisir, the comfortable pleasure of reading, with jouissance, a bliss that unsettles the reader's cultural assumptions and sense of self. Empire of Signs (1970) reflected on Japan, and A Lover's Discourse (1977) arranged the language of love in fragments.\n\nCamera Lucida (1980), written after his mother's death, distinguished the studium of a photograph, its general, culturally coded interest, from its punctum, the detail that pierces a particular viewer. Barthes moved from structuralism to post-structuralism and founded no school, but his work shaped literary theory, semiotics, media studies and the theory of photography.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roland_Barthes", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["althusser", "Louis Althusser", "Althusser", "1918–1990", "French structural Marxist.", ["For Marx (1965)", "Reading Capital (1965, with others)", "“Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses” (1970)"], ["spinoza", "marx", "gramsci", "lacan"], { x: "Louis Althusser was born in Algeria in 1918 into a French family and raised as a Catholic. He spent the Second World War in a German prisoner-of-war camp, then studied at the École normale supérieure, where he passed the agrégation in 1948 and taught for more than three decades; his students included many of the leading French philosophers of the next generation. He joined the French Communist Party in 1948. Throughout his life he suffered from severe depression and spent long periods in psychiatric care.\n\nFor Marx and Reading Capital (both 1965, the second with his students) proposed a new reading of Marx. Althusser argued that Marx's mature philosophy is largely implicit in Capital and must be drawn out by a symptomatic reading, attentive to the gaps, silences and contradictions of the text. He held that an epistemological break separates the humanist, Hegelian writings of the young Marx from the mature work, in which Marx founded a science of history.\n\nThat science, on his reading, is theoretically anti-humanist: it explains history through structures and relations of production, and not through a human essence or the consciousness of individuals. Society is a complex whole of economic, political and ideological practices, each with relative autonomy; events are overdetermined, produced by the combination of many contradictions, and the economy is determinant only in the last instance, through a structural causality in which the whole acts in its effects.\n\nHis essay “Ideology and Ideological State Apparatuses” (1970) asked how a society reproduces the conditions of its own production. Alongside the repressive state apparatus, police, courts and army, ideological state apparatuses, such as schools, churches, families and the media, reproduce the relations of production by forming subjects. Ideology interpellates individuals as subjects, hailing them as a police officer hails a passerby who turns around, so that they recognize themselves in the place assigned to them and come to act freely within it.\n\nIn 1980, during a psychotic episode, Althusser strangled his wife, Hélène Rytmann. He was found not legally responsible under French law and was confined to psychiatric hospitals, and he wrote a memoir about the act and his life. In the 1980s he sketched an aleatory materialism, or materialism of the encounter, in which social orders arise from contingent conjunctions. He died in 1990. His theory of ideology remains widely used across the humanities and social sciences.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/althusser/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["anscombe", "G. E. M. Anscombe", "Anscombe", "1919–2001", "British philosopher of intention and action, translator of Wittgenstein.", ["Intention (1957)", "“Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958)"], ["aristotle", "aquinas", "wittgenstein"], { x: "Elizabeth Anscombe was born in Limerick, Ireland, in 1919. She converted to Roman Catholicism in her teens and studied at St Hugh's College, Oxford. At Cambridge she became a student and close friend of Wittgenstein, who made her one of his literary executors; she became his chief English translator, including of the Philosophical Investigations (1953). She married the philosopher Peter Geach, with whom she had seven children. She held Wittgenstein's former chair at Cambridge from 1970 until 1986 and died in 2001.\n\nIn 1956 she publicly opposed Oxford's award of an honorary degree to Harry Truman, arguing that ordering the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki made him a murderer. Intentionally killing the innocent as a means to an end is always murder, she held, and the distinction between what one intends and what one merely foresees has moral weight, a theme connected with the doctrine of double effect.\n\nIntention (1957) is a founding work of modern philosophy of action. Anscombe identified intentional actions as those to which a certain sense of the question “Why?” applies, the sense that asks for the agent's reason. The same action can be intentional under one description and unintentional under another: a man moving his arm may intentionally be pumping water and unintentionally be poisoning the household. An agent knows what she is doing without observation, by a practical knowledge that depends on practical reasoning.\n\n“Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958) argued that the concepts of moral obligation and the moral “ought” are survivals of a law conception of ethics that no longer makes sense without belief in a divine lawgiver, and should be set aside; that ethics needs an adequate philosophy of psychology before it can proceed; and that English moral philosophers since Sidgwick did not differ much among themselves, a position she named “consequentialism,” coining the term. The essay helped launch the modern revival of virtue ethics.\n\nIn “Causality and Determination” (1971) she argued that causes need not necessitate their effects, against the Humean tradition, and in “The First Person” (1975) that “I” is not a referring expression. She also wrote on perception, distinguishing material from intentional objects, and on testimony, which she held to be a basic source of knowledge. She is widely regarded as one of the most important philosophers of the twentieth century.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/anscombe/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["rawls", "John Rawls", "Rawls", "1921–2002", "American political philosopher of justice as fairness.", ["A Theory of Justice (1971)", "Political Liberalism (1993)"], ["locke", "rousseau", "kant"], { x: "John Rawls was born in Baltimore in 1921, studied at Princeton, and served as an infantryman in the Second World War, an experience that ended his Christian faith. He taught at Cornell and MIT before joining Harvard in 1962, where he spent the rest of his career. He died in 2002. A Theory of Justice (1971) is widely credited with reviving political philosophy in the English-speaking world.\n\nRawls asked which principles of justice free and equal persons would choose to govern the basic structure of their society. He modeled the choice as an original position, in which the parties choose behind a veil of ignorance: they do not know their class, talents, sex, race or conception of the good life, so that the principles they choose cannot be tailored to their own advantage. Justice is thus fairness in the conditions of agreement.\n\nHe argued that they would choose two principles. First, each person has an equal right to the most extensive scheme of basic liberties compatible with the same liberties for all, and these liberties have priority. Second, social and economic inequalities must be attached to positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity, and must be to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged, the difference principle. Rawls presented this as an alternative to utilitarianism, which, he argued, does not take seriously the distinctness of persons. His method was reflective equilibrium, adjusting principles and considered judgments until they cohere. He favored a property-owning democracy or liberal socialism over welfare-state capitalism.\n\nPolitical Liberalism (1993) asked how a society can be stable and its coercive power legitimate when citizens hold irreconcilable religious and philosophical doctrines, a condition he called reasonable pluralism. His answer was a political conception of justice that citizens can endorse from within their different doctrines, an overlapping consensus, and an ideal of public reason, under which basic political questions are argued in terms all citizens could reasonably accept.\n\nThe Law of Peoples (1999) extended the theory to international relations, distinguishing liberal and decent peoples from outlaw states and burdened societies and defending a duty of assistance, and Justice as Fairness: A Restatement (2001) summarized his mature view. Robert Nozick, communitarians such as Michael Sandel, and critics of his account of race, gender and global justice made Rawls the central point of reference for later political philosophy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/rawls/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["williams", "Raymond Williams", "Raymond Williams", "1921–1988", "Welsh cultural critic and theorist of cultural materialism.", ["Culture and Society (1958)", "Keywords (1976)", "Marxism and Literature (1977)"], ["marx", "gramsci"], { x: "Raymond Williams was born in 1921 in Pandy, a Welsh border village, the son of a railway worker. He read English at Trinity College, Cambridge, from 1939, joined the Communist Party there, and in the Second World War served as an anti-tank officer in the Guards Armoured Division in Normandy and Germany. After the war he spent fifteen years as an adult education tutor, much of it in Workers' Educational Association classes. He returned to Cambridge in 1961 and became its first Professor of Drama in 1974. He died in 1988.\n\nCulture and Society (1958) traced how the idea of culture took shape in English writing from the Industrial Revolution onward, alongside the words industry, democracy, class and art, as a response to social change. In his essay “Culture Is Ordinary” (1958) he insisted that culture means a whole way of life as well as the arts and learning, and that every society and every mind makes its own. The Long Revolution (1961) described the democratic, industrial and cultural revolutions of modern Britain as one continuing process.\n\nWilliams introduced the concept of a structure of feeling: the lived quality of experience in a particular period and generation, felt in its art and conventions before it is articulated as ideas. Keywords (1976) traced the shifting meanings of the words, such as culture, nature, society and ideology, through which social life is argued over.\n\nIn Marxism and Literature (1977) he set out cultural materialism, a Marxism that treats culture as material production in its own right. He rejected a mechanical model of base and superstructure, redefining determination as the setting of limits and the exertion of pressures, and distinguished dominant, residual and emergent elements in any culture. The Country and the City (1973) and Television: Technology and Cultural Form (1974) applied this approach to literature and to media.\n\nWilliams was a novelist as well, beginning with Border Country (1960), and a political writer of the New Left who later argued for many forms of socialism and for links between the labor and ecological movements. His work laid much of the foundation of British cultural studies.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_Williams", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["kuhn", "Thomas Kuhn", "Kuhn", "1922–1996", "American historian and philosopher of science.", ["The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962)"], [], { x: "Thomas Kuhn was born in 1922 and trained as a physicist, taking his doctorate at Harvard. Teaching in a Harvard course on the history of science for non-scientists, he was struck by how unlike modern physics Aristotle's physics was, and how coherent it appeared once read on its own terms. He turned to the history and then the philosophy of science, taught at Berkeley, Princeton and MIT, and died in 1996. His first book, The Copernican Revolution (1957), studied the transition from Ptolemaic to Copernican astronomy.\n\nThe Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), among the most cited academic books ever written, argued that science does not grow by the steady accumulation of knowledge. For long periods of normal science, a community works within a paradigm, built around shared exemplars, model solutions to problems, which define what counts as a legitimate problem and an acceptable solution. Normal science is puzzle-solving.\n\nPersistent anomalies, results that resist the paradigm, can provoke a crisis, and a crisis can end in a scientific revolution, in which one paradigm is replaced by another, as when Newtonian mechanics gave way to relativity. Scientists who move between paradigms, Kuhn wrote, see the world differently, as in a gestalt switch.\n\nSuccessive paradigms are incommensurable: they lack a common measure, since their concepts, problems and standards differ, so that choosing between them cannot be settled by a neutral algorithm. Shared values such as accuracy, consistency, scope, simplicity and fruitfulness guide theory choice without determining it. In a postscript to the 1970 second edition Kuhn distinguished the paradigm as a whole disciplinary matrix from the paradigm as exemplar.\n\nPhilosophers often read Kuhn as a relativist who made science irrational, readings he rejected. His later work recast incommensurability in terms of taxonomies and the lexicons of scientific communities, and compared scientific progress to biological evolution, as a growth in puzzle-solving power that moves away from earlier states without approaching a fixed goal. His work helped end the dominance of logical positivism and shaped the history, sociology and philosophy of science.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/thomas-kuhn/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["guha", "Ranajit Guha", "Guha", "1923–2023", "Indian historian who founded Subaltern Studies.", ["Elementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency in Colonial India (1983)"], ["gramsci"], { x: "Ranajit Guha was born in 1923 in Backergunge district, Bengal, now in Bangladesh, the son of a lawyer who became a judge. He studied at Presidency College and the University of Calcutta, joined the Communist Party of India in the 1940s and traveled widely for the party's youth organizations, and distanced himself from it after the Soviet invasion of Hungary in 1956. He taught in India, moved to Britain in 1959, became a reader in history at the University of Sussex, and in 1981 moved to the Australian National University. He spent his last decades in Purkersdorf, Austria, and died there in 2023, at ninety-nine.\n\nHis first book, A Rule of Property for Bengal (1963), traced the European ideas behind the Permanent Settlement of 1793, the British land revenue system in Bengal. In 1982 he founded and edited the first volume of Subaltern Studies, which became one of the most influential projects in modern historiography.\n\nIts opening statement, “On Some Aspects of the Historiography of Colonial India,” charged that the history of Indian nationalism had been written from an elitist standpoint, by both colonial and nationalist historians, who saw the making of the nation as the achievement of British administrators or of Indian elites. It ignored the politics of the people, the subaltern classes, who acted autonomously from the elite in their own domain. Guha took the term subaltern from Antonio Gramsci, to name the subordinate groups outside the elite sectors of society.\n\nElementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency in Colonial India (1983) reconstructed the consciousness of rebellious peasants from the colonial records that described them as criminals and fanatics, reading those sources against the grain. Dominance without Hegemony argued that British rule in India rested on dominance without achieving the consent that marks hegemony, and that Indian elites likewise failed to win it.\n\nThe Subaltern Studies collective included Partha Chatterjee and Dipesh Chakrabarty, and Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak's critical engagement with it, above all “Can the Subaltern Speak?”, carried its questions into postcolonial theory. Guha's later work, including History at the Limit of World-History (2002), criticized Hegel's philosophy of history for excluding most of humanity from history.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranajit_Guha", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["lyotard", "Jean-François Lyotard", "Lyotard", "1924–1998", "French philosopher of the postmodern condition and the sublime.", ["Libidinal Economy (1974)", "The Postmodern Condition (1979)", "The Differend (1983)"], ["kant", "marx"], { x: "Jean-François Lyotard was born in 1924. After studying philosophy he taught for several years at a lycée in Constantine, in French Algeria, and for over a decade was active in the radical Marxist group Socialisme ou Barbarie and its offshoot Pouvoir Ouvrier, writing especially on the Algerian war. He later taught at the University of Paris at Nanterre and Vincennes and helped found the Collège international de philosophie. He died of leukemia in Paris in 1998.\n\nHis first major book, Discourse, Figure (1971), defended the figural, the sensuous, visual dimension of experience and art, against the structuralist reduction of meaning to linguistic systems. Libidinal Economy (1974), which he later repudiated, treated political and social structures as fields of desire and intensities.\n\nThe Postmodern Condition: A Report on Knowledge (1979), commissioned by the government of Quebec, made “postmodern” a philosophical term. Lyotard defined the postmodern as incredulity toward metanarratives, the grand stories, such as the emancipation of humanity or the progress of knowledge, that modern societies used to legitimate science and politics. In their place knowledge is increasingly legitimated by performativity, its efficiency in increasing a system's output. He set against this a postmodern science of instabilities, and a plurality of incommensurable language games.\n\nThe Differend (1983), which he considered his most philosophical book, defines a differend as a conflict between parties that cannot be equitably resolved because no rule of judgment applies to both. Judging one side's claim in the other's idiom wrongs it, silencing a party that cannot express its injury in the language of the tribunal. His central example is Auschwitz and the denial of its gas chambers, where the victims cannot testify in the terms demanded of them. In Just Gaming (1979) he argued that we judge without criteria, case by case.\n\nHis later writings turned to Kant's analytic of the sublime, which he read as the feeling of what cannot be presented, and to avant-garde art, especially the paintings of Barnett Newman, as witnessing to it. The Inhuman (1988) contrasted the inhumanity of a technoscientific system that seeks total efficiency with the inhuman in us that remains open to the unforeseeable event.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lyotard/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["deleuze", "Gilles Deleuze", "Deleuze", "1925–1995", "French philosopher who wrote studies of Hume, Nietzsche, Kant, Bergson, Spinoza and Leibniz, and four books with Félix Guattari.", ["Nietzsche and Philosophy (1962)", "Difference and Repetition (1968)", "The Logic of Sense (1969)", "Anti-Oedipus (1972, with Guattari)", "A Thousand Plateaus (1980, with Guattari)", "Cinema 1 and Cinema 2 (1983, 1985)", "What Is Philosophy? (1991, with Guattari)"], ["hume", "nietzsche", "kant", "spinoza", "leibniz", "foucault"], { x: "Gilles Deleuze was born in Paris on 18 January 1925. During the German Occupation he spent a year at school in Normandy, where he was taught by Pierre Halbwachs at Deauville; his older brother was arrested by the Nazis for resistance activity and died on the train to Auschwitz. After the Liberation he completed the khâgne, the preparatory year for the grandes écoles, at the Lycée Henri IV, then studied the history of philosophy at the Sorbonne under Jean Hyppolite, Ferdinand Alquié, Georges Canguilhem and Maurice de Gandillac. He passed the agrégation in philosophy in 1948.\n\nHe taught at lycées in Amiens, Orléans and Paris (Louis-le-Grand) until 1957, then took a position at the University of Paris. From 1960 to 1964 he was a research fellow at the CNRS, and from 1964 to 1969 a professor at the University of Lyon. He later called the stretch from 1953 to 1962, when he published little, “a hole in my life”. In those years he developed the respiratory illness that stayed with him; tuberculosis in 1968 cost him a lung.\n\nHis first books were studies of other philosophers: Empiricism and Subjectivity (1953), on Hume; Nietzsche and Philosophy (1962); Kant's Critical Philosophy (1963); Bergsonism (1966); and a study of Sacher-Masoch (1967), published in English as Masochism: Coldness and Cruelty. Proust and Signs (1964) turned to literature. He married Fanny (Denise Paul) Grandjouan, a translator of D. H. Lawrence, and they had two children.\n\nIn 1968 he received the doctorat d'État with Difference and Repetition as his main thesis and Expressionism in Philosophy: Spinoza as his secondary thesis, both published that year. The Logic of Sense followed in 1969, and a shorter book, Spinoza: Practical Philosophy, appeared in its second, expanded French edition in 1981.\n\nIn 1969 he took a permanent post at the experimental University of Paris VIII at Vincennes, later moved to Saint-Denis, where he gave weekly seminars until he retired in 1987. That same year he met Félix Guattari, a psychoanalyst and political activist, and they began a collaboration that lasted until Guattari's death. Together they wrote the two volumes of Capitalism and Schizophrenia, Anti-Oedipus (1972) and A Thousand Plateaus (1980), as well as Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975) and What Is Philosophy? (1991).\n\nHis later books include Dialogues (1977, with Claire Parnet), Francis Bacon: The Logic of Sensation (1981), Cinema 1: The Movement-Image (1983), Cinema 2: The Time-Image (1985), Foucault (1986), The Fold: Leibniz and the Baroque (1988), the collection of interviews Negotiations (1990) and Essays Critical and Clinical (1993). He traveled to the United States in 1975 and otherwise rarely went abroad. His last essay, “Immanence: A Life”, appeared in 1995.\n\nHis final years were marked by severe respiratory illness that made even writing laborious. He died by suicide in Paris on 4 November 1995, at the age of seventy. Collections of his shorter writings appeared afterward, among them Desert Islands and Other Texts, 1953–1974 (2002).", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilles_Deleuze", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Gilles Deleuze”" }] }],
  ["fanon", "Frantz Fanon", "Fanon", "1925–1961", "Martinican psychiatrist and theorist of colonial racism and decolonization.", ["Black Skin, White Masks (1952)", "The Wretched of the Earth (1961)"], ["hegel", "sartre", "merleauponty", "cesaire"], { x: "Frantz Fanon was born in 1925 in Martinique, then a French colony. At the Lycée Schoelcher he was taught by Aimé Césaire. In the Second World War he joined the Free French forces and was wounded in France. He studied medicine and psychiatry in Lyon and trained at the psychiatric hospital of Saint-Alban under François Tosquelles. In 1953 he became head of a ward at the Blida-Joinville psychiatric hospital in Algeria. He joined the Algerian National Liberation Front, served as ambassador to Ghana for the provisional Algerian government, and died of leukemia in Bethesda, Maryland, in December 1961, at thirty-six.\n\nBlack Skin, White Masks (1952) examines how anti-Black racism shapes the inner lives of both Black and white people. Its central chapter on the lived experience of the Black man begins with a child's cry, “Look, a Negro!”, in which Fanon finds himself fixed as an object by the white gaze, his body broken apart and reassembled by racial myths. Colonialism, he argued, is a total project that reaches into language, desire and identity, producing an internalized inferiority complex; such suffering must be understood socially and historically as well as in individual psychology, a perspective he called sociogeny.\n\nThe book engaged Hegel, Sartre and négritude. Fanon criticized Sartre's preface “Black Orpheus” for treating négritude as a passing moment in a dialectic, and rejected the idea that the Black person should be defined by the past, closing with a prayer for a body that always questions. He contrasted antisemitism, which fears its targets' supposed power, with anti-Black racism, which despises their supposed weakness.\n\nHis essays on the Algerian revolution, collected in A Dying Colonialism (1959), studied how the struggle transformed the veil, the family and the radio. The Wretched of the Earth (1961), with a preface by Sartre, argued that decolonization is always a violent phenomenon and that anti-colonial violence dismantles colonial structures while forming new identities in the colonized. It warned of the pitfalls of national consciousness, the danger that a national bourgeoisie would simply take the colonizers' place, gave a revolutionary role to the peasantry and the lumpenproletariat, and ended with a call to work out new concepts and create a new humanity.\n\nThe book also includes case studies of mental disorders produced by the colonial war, in both the colonized and torturers. Fanon became a central reference for anti-colonial movements, the Black Power movement and the Black Panther Party, and for postcolonial theory, critical race theory and Africana philosophy.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/frantz-fanon/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["foucault", "Michel Foucault", "Foucault", "1926–1984", "French historian of systems of thought, power and the subject.", ["History of Madness (1961)", "The Order of Things (1966)", "Discipline and Punish (1975)", "The History of Sexuality, vol. 1 (1976)"], ["kant", "nietzsche", "heidegger"], { x: "Michel Foucault was born in Poitiers in 1926 and studied philosophy and psychology at the École normale supérieure. In the 1950s and 1960s he held posts in Sweden, Poland and Germany, then at French universities including Clermont-Ferrand and Vincennes. In 1969 he was elected to the Collège de France, where he held a chair in the history of systems of thought. From the early 1970s he was politically active, co-founding the Prison Information Group with others, and he lectured widely in the United States. He died in Paris in 1984, one of the first prominent victims of AIDS.\n\nHis early histories asked how the sciences of the human came to be. History of Madness (1961) traced how the age of reason separated madness from reason and confined the mad, and The Birth of the Clinic (1963) examined the origin of modern medicine's gaze. The Order of Things (1966) described the epistemes, the unspoken orders of knowledge, of the Renaissance, the classical age and modernity, and argued that “man,” as at once the subject and object of knowledge, is a recent invention that may be erased like a face drawn in sand. The Archaeology of Knowledge (1969) set out the method: the study of discursive formations, the rules that govern what can be said in a period.\n\nDiscipline and Punish (1975) turned to genealogy and power. It contrasts the spectacular public execution of the old regime with the modern prison and describes disciplinary power, which works through hierarchical observation, normalizing judgment and the examination to produce docile, useful bodies. Bentham's Panopticon, in which inmates never know whether they are watched and so watch themselves, becomes the emblem of a disciplinary society whose techniques spread to schools, factories, barracks and hospitals. Power, Foucault argued, is bound up with knowledge, and it produces as well as represses.\n\nThe first volume of The History of Sexuality (1976) attacked the repressive hypothesis, the belief that modern societies silenced sex, and showed instead an explosion of discourses about sexuality, through which power took hold of bodies and populations. It introduced biopower, the power to foster and administer life, as opposed to the sovereign's power to put to death. His lectures of the late 1970s developed the concept of governmentality, the arts of governing populations, including an analysis of neoliberalism.\n\nThe last two volumes of The History of Sexuality (1984) turned to ancient ethics, the care of the self and the practices by which individuals shaped themselves into subjects of their own conduct. Foucault's concepts of discourse, discipline, biopower and governmentality have become basic tools across history, sociology, political theory, gender studies and many other fields.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/foucault/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["dorothysmith", "Dorothy E. Smith", "Dorothy Smith", "1926–2022", "Canadian sociologist who founded institutional ethnography and a feminist standpoint sociology.", ["The Everyday World as Problematic (1987)"], ["marx", "schutz"], { x: "Dorothy E. Smith was born in 1926 in Northallerton, Yorkshire, and studied sociology and social anthropology at the London School of Economics. She took her doctorate at Berkeley in 1963, then, after her marriage ended, raised her two children alone. She taught at Essex and from 1968 at the University of British Columbia, where she helped establish women's studies, and from 1977 at the Ontario Institute for Studies in Education in Toronto. She was appointed to the Order of Canada in 2019 and died in Vancouver in 2022.\n\nSmith's sociology began from her own experience as a woman moving between the work of caring for children at home and the abstract world of the university. She described this as a bifurcation of consciousness: women's lives are lived in the local, bodily world of particular people and tasks, while the institutions that govern them, and the sociology that describes them, are organized from an extra-local standpoint that had been built by and for men.\n\nHer feminist sociology, set out in The Everyday World as Problematic (1987), proposed to start inquiry from the standpoint of women, from people's actual everyday activities and experiences, and to make the everyday world itself the problem to be investigated. Her work is a major source of feminist standpoint theory, alongside that of Sandra Harding and Nancy Hartsock.\n\nThe central concept of The Conceptual Practices of Power (1990) and Texts, Facts, and Femininity (1990) is the ruling relations: the complex of bureaucracy, management, professional knowledge, media and administration that coordinates people's activities across many sites. Texts, forms and records play a key role, since the same document read in many places organizes what people do and turns their lives into institutional facts.\n\nInstitutional ethnography, the method she developed and described in Institutional Ethnography: A Sociology for People (2005), traces how local experiences are organized by these translocal relations, starting from what people actually do and following the texts and work processes that connect them. Smith drew on Marx, Alfred Schutz, George Herbert Mead, Merleau-Ponty and Harold Garfinkel, and her approach is used across sociology, education, health research and social work.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_E._Smith", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["gettier", "Edmund Gettier", "Gettier", "1927–2021", "American epistemologist, famous for one three-page paper.", ["“Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?” (1963)"], [], { x: "Edmund Gettier was born in Baltimore in 1927. He studied at Johns Hopkins and took his doctorate at Cornell in 1961, with a dissertation on Russell's theories of belief supervised by Norman Malcolm. He taught at Wayne State University from 1957 to 1967 and at the University of Massachusetts Amherst from 1967 until his retirement in 2001. He died in 2021.\n\nHe is famous for one paper. “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?”, three pages long, appeared in the journal Analysis in 1963. He is reported to have written it to help secure tenure. He never published anything else in epistemology, and he declined to attend a conference held in 2013 to mark the paper's fiftieth anniversary.\n\nThe paper targeted the traditional analysis of knowledge, traced back to Plato's Theaetetus and defended in Gettier's time by philosophers such as Roderick Chisholm and A. J. Ayer: that a person knows something when he believes it, it is true, and he is justified in believing it. Gettier gave two counterexamples in which someone has a justified true belief that is true only by luck.\n\nIn the first, Smith has strong evidence that Jones will get a job and that Jones has ten coins in his pocket, and so justifiably believes that the man who will get the job has ten coins in his pocket. Unknown to Smith, he himself gets the job, and he too happens to have ten coins in his pocket. His belief is justified and true, yet he does not know it. In the second, Smith justifiably believes that Jones owns a Ford and infers that either Jones owns a Ford or Brown is in Barcelona; Jones owns no Ford, but by chance Brown is in Barcelona.\n\nThe paper started the “Gettier problem,” the search for what must be added to or substituted for justified true belief. Proposals include a condition that the belief not rest on any falsehood, causal and reliabilist accounts, Robert Nozick's requirement that belief track the truth, virtue-based accounts, and the view, defended by Timothy Williamson among others, that knowledge cannot be analyzed into simpler parts.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Gettier", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Epistemology”" }] }],
  ["luckmann", "Thomas Luckmann", "Luckmann", "1927–2016", "German-American sociologist of knowledge and religion.", ["The Social Construction of Reality (1966, with Berger)"], ["schutz"], { x: "Thomas Luckmann was born in 1927 in Jesenice, then in Yugoslavia and now in Slovenia, and grew up speaking Slovene and German. Displaced by the war, he served in the German air force as a teenager and became a prisoner of war. He studied philosophy and linguistics in Vienna and Innsbruck, emigrated to the United States in 1950, and studied at the New School for Social Research, where Alfred Schutz became his teacher. He taught at Hobart College and the New School, became a professor in Frankfurt in 1965, and taught at the University of Konstanz from 1970 until he retired. He died in Austria in 2016.\n\nThe Social Construction of Reality (1966), written with Peter Berger, recast the sociology of knowledge as the study of everything that passes for knowledge in everyday life. People who interact over time develop habits and typify one another's actions; when these patterns become reciprocal roles that anyone could take up, they are institutionalized, and later generations meet them as an objective, given reality.\n\nInstitutions are then made plausible by legitimations, from proverbs and myths to religious and theoretical systems, which culminate in symbolic universes that give an entire social order its meaning. Individuals internalize this reality through primary socialization in childhood, which is emotionally charged and hard to undo, and secondary socialization into specialized roles. Everyday conversation keeps subjective reality in place. The book introduced the phrase “social construction” into general use, and the International Sociological Association later ranked it among the most important sociology books of the twentieth century.\n\nThe Invisible Religion (1967) argued that religion in modern societies has become privatized, a matter of individuals assembling meaning from many sources outside the churches.\n\nLuckmann completed Schutz's unfinished The Structures of the Life-World from his teacher's notes, published in two volumes, and later developed the study of communicative genres, such as gossip, proverbs and jokes, as social forms of knowledge. He drew on Schutz, Weber, Marx, Mead and Max Scheler, and his work is a foundation of social constructionism across the social sciences.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Luckmann", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Social_Construction_of_Reality", srcName: "Wikipedia, “The Social Construction of Reality”" }] }],
  ["berger", "Peter L. Berger", "Berger", "1929–2017", "Austrian-American sociologist of knowledge and religion.", ["The Social Construction of Reality (1966, with Luckmann)", "The Sacred Canopy (1967)"], ["weber", "schutz"], { x: "Peter L. Berger was born in Vienna in 1929 and emigrated to the United States in 1946. He studied at Wagner College and took his doctorate at the New School for Social Research, where he was shaped by Alfred Schutz's phenomenological sociology. He taught at Hartford Seminary, the New School and Rutgers, and from 1981 at Boston University as University Professor of Sociology and Theology, where in 1985 he founded what became the Institute on Culture, Religion and World Affairs. A Lutheran, he wrote as a theologian as well as a sociologist. He died in 2017.\n\nInvitation to Sociology (1963) presented sociology as a humanistic discipline, a way of seeing through the facades of social structures while recognizing how they make us. The Social Construction of Reality (1966), written with Thomas Luckmann, became one of the most influential books in the discipline and helped found social constructionism.\n\nBerger and Luckmann described a dialectic in which society is a human product, society is an objective reality, and human beings are social products. Through externalization people make institutions, through objectivation those institutions come to face them as fixed realities, and through internalization they enter consciousness as the way things are.\n\nThe Sacred Canopy (1967) applied this framework to religion. Every society builds a nomos, a meaningful order, which needs legitimation and plausibility structures, the social supports that make a view of the world credible. Religion has historically provided the sacred canopy that legitimates the social order by grounding it in a cosmic one. Modern pluralism, by multiplying worldviews, weakens every canopy and leaves individuals to choose, a condition Berger and his co-authors later called the homeless mind. A Rumor of Angels (1969) explored signals of transcendence within ordinary experience.\n\nLike most sociologists of his generation, Berger first predicted the steady secularization of the world. He later concluded that he had been wrong: in The Desecularization of the World (1999) he argued that the world was as furiously religious as ever, with Western Europe and Western academic elites as the main exceptions. He also wrote on modernization, capitalism and the importance of mediating structures such as family, church and neighborhood.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_L._Berger", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["frankfurt", "Harry Frankfurt", "Frankfurt", "1929–2023", "American moral philosopher of free will, caring and bullshit.", ["“Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971)", "On Bullshit (2005)"], [], { x: "Harry Frankfurt was born in 1929 in Langhorne, Pennsylvania. He took his doctorate at Johns Hopkins and taught at Ohio State, Rockefeller University, Yale and Princeton, where he became professor emeritus. He died in 2023. His early work included a well-known interpretation of Descartes's rationalism, Demons, Dreamers, and Madmen (1970).\n\n“Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility” (1969) attacked the principle that a person is morally responsible for an action only if he could have done otherwise. In what are now called Frankfurt cases, someone stands ready to intervene and force an agent to act in a certain way if the agent shows signs of choosing otherwise, but never needs to intervene because the agent chooses that way on his own. The agent could not have done otherwise, yet seems fully responsible. The argument reshaped the free will debate.\n\n“Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person” (1971) distinguished first-order desires, desires to do or have something, from second-order desires about which first-order desires to have, and second-order volitions, wanting a particular desire to be the one that moves one to act. A person is a being with second-order volitions; a creature that acts on whatever desire is strongest, without caring which, is a wanton. The unwilling addict, who is moved by a desire he repudiates, lacks freedom of the will, while a willing addict who endorses his craving acts freely, on this compatibilist account.\n\nFrankfurt's later work turned to caring and love. In The Importance of What We Care About (1988) and The Reasons of Love (2004) he argued that caring about something is what makes it important to the person who cares, and that love, a disinterested concern for the flourishing of what is loved, gives our lives their final ends. He also argued that the moral ideal in economic matters is sufficiency, that everyone should have enough, more than equality.\n\nHis essay “On Bullshit,” written in 1986 and published as a small book in 2005, became a bestseller. The liar, Frankfurt argued, cares about the truth enough to hide it; the bullshitter is indifferent to whether what he says is true at all, caring only about the impression he makes, which makes bullshit a greater enemy of truth than lies. On Truth (2006) followed.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_Frankfurt", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["habermas", "Jürgen Habermas", "Habermas", "1929–2026", "German philosopher of communicative action and deliberative democracy.", ["The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962)", "The Theory of Communicative Action (1981)", "Between Facts and Norms (1992)"], ["kant", "weber", "horkheimer", "adorno"], { x: "Jürgen Habermas was born in Düsseldorf in 1929 and grew up in Gummersbach. Born with a cleft palate, he needed repeated operations as a child, an experience he later linked to his interest in communication. He was fifteen when Nazi Germany was defeated, and he said that confronting its crimes set him on his path. He wrote his dissertation on Schelling, became Adorno's assistant at the Institute for Social Research, and later held the chair of philosophy and sociology at Frankfurt, with a decade directing a Max Planck Institute at Starnberg. He was for decades a leading public intellectual in Germany, intervening in the Historians' Dispute of the 1980s and in debates on European unification. He died in Starnberg in March 2026, at ninety-six.\n\nThe Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere (1962) traced the rise of a bourgeois public sphere in the coffeehouses, salons and press of the eighteenth century, where private persons debated public matters by reason, and its decline as mass media, interest groups and public relations displaced rational-critical debate. Knowledge and Human Interests (1968) argued that knowledge is guided by interests, technical, practical and emancipatory, which positivism conceals.\n\nThe Theory of Communicative Action (1981) distinguished action oriented to reaching understanding from strategic action oriented to success, and treated the first as basic. In speaking, people raise validity claims to truth, normative rightness and sincerity that can be challenged and defended with reasons. Modern societies are integrated through the lifeworld, the shared background of meanings reproduced by communication, and through systems, the economy and the administrative state, steered by money and power. The pathologies of modernity arise when systemic imperatives colonize the lifeworld.\n\nDiscourse ethics holds that valid moral norms are those that all affected could accept as participants in a rational discourse, a principle of universalization worked out with Karl-Otto Apel. Between Facts and Norms (1992) applied the theory to law and democracy: private autonomy, protected by individual rights, and public autonomy, exercised in popular sovereignty, are co-original, and democratic legitimacy flows from an informal public sphere into formal deliberation in parliaments and courts.\n\nHis later work addressed constitutional patriotism, cosmopolitan law, the European Union and a postsecular society in which religious citizens contribute to public debate. Usually counted as the leading figure of the Frankfurt School's second generation, though the label fits imperfectly, he shaped critical theory, democratic theory and social philosophy worldwide.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/habermas/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/influential-german-philosopher-jurgen-habermas-dies-at-96", srcName: "PBS NewsHour, obituary (2026)" }] }],
  ["macintyre", "Alasdair MacIntyre", "MacIntyre", "1929–2025", "Scottish-American moral philosopher who revived virtue ethics.", ["After Virtue (1981)", "Whose Justice? Which Rationality? (1988)"], ["aristotle", "aquinas", "marx"], { x: "Alasdair MacIntyre was born in Glasgow in 1929. He studied classics at Queen Mary College, London, and philosophy at Manchester, and taught at several British universities while active in the Marxist New Left, working toward a Marxist ethics that could condemn Stalinism. In 1970 he moved to the United States, where he taught at Brandeis, Boston University, Vanderbilt, Notre Dame and Duke, returning to Notre Dame until his retirement in 2010. After periods as a fideist and an atheist, he identified as a Thomist from about 1984. He died in 2025.\n\nAfter Virtue (1981), his most influential book, opens with a disquieting suggestion: that the language of morality is in a state of grave disorder, like the fragments of a science that survived a catastrophe, used by people who no longer understand the context that gave them meaning. Modern moral argument is interminable because rival positions rest on incommensurable premises, and moral language is used in a culture of emotivism, as an instrument for expressing preferences and manipulating others.\n\nThe cause, he argued, is the failure of the Enlightenment project of justifying morality without any account of the human good. Once Aristotle's idea of human beings as having a telos, a characteristic end, was abandoned, the moral rules inherited from that tradition lost their rationale. The choice, as the book frames it, is between Nietzsche and Aristotle.\n\nMacIntyre reconstructed the virtues in three stages. Virtues are qualities that enable us to achieve the goods internal to practices, cooperative activities such as chess, farming or scholarship, whose goods can be had only by excelling at them. They also sustain the narrative unity of a single human life, understood as a quest, and they live within traditions, socially embodied arguments extended over time about what the good is.\n\nWhose Justice? Which Rationality? (1988) and Three Rival Versions of Moral Enquiry (1990) argued that rationality itself is shaped by traditions, and that a tradition proves its superiority by resolving crises that its rivals cannot. Dependent Rational Animals (1999) stressed human vulnerability and the virtues of acknowledged dependence. A sharp critic of liberal individualism and of capitalism, MacIntyre rejected the communitarian label often applied to him, but his work drove the revival of virtue ethics.", src: "https://iep.utm.edu/mac-over/", srcName: "Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["guattari", "Félix Guattari", "Guattari", "1930–1992", "French psychoanalyst and political activist who worked at the La Borde clinic and wrote four books with Gilles Deleuze.", ["Anti-Oedipus (1972, with Deleuze)", "Molecular Revolution (1977)", "A Thousand Plateaus (1980, with Deleuze)", "The Three Ecologies (1989)", "Chaosmosis (1992)"], ["lacan"], { x: "Pierre-Félix Guattari was born on 30 March 1930 in Villeneuve-les-Sablons, Oise, in a working-class district northwest of Paris. As a teenager he took part in Trotskyist activism. Fernand Oury, his high school teacher, became an early influence and introduced him to his brother, the psychiatrist Jean Oury.\n\nIn the early 1950s Guattari was an apprentice and analysand of Jacques Lacan. He first met Jean Oury at a private psychiatric clinic in Saumery, in the Loire Valley, and in 1955 followed him to the experimental clinic of La Borde at Cour-Cheverny, founded two years earlier. He worked there for the rest of his life and developed forms of group therapy at the clinic.\n\nHe was politically active throughout. He belonged to the French Communist Party and later to Trotskyist groups, and from 1955 to 1965 edited and wrote for the newspaper La Voie communiste. He supported anticolonial struggles and later the Italian Autonomists, took part in the working groups that led to the Association of Institutional Psychotherapy in 1965, co-founded the FGERI and its review Recherches, and helped found OSARLA, a solidarity organization for Latin American revolutionary movements, in 1967.\n\nDuring May 1968 he was involved from the start, with the Movement of 22 March. In 1970 he created CERFI, a center for research on institutions, which carried on the work of Recherches; in 1973 he was tried and fined for an “outrage to public decency” over an issue of the journal devoted to homosexuality. He met Gilles Deleuze in the aftermath of 1968, and together they wrote Anti-Oedipus (1972), Kafka: Toward a Minor Literature (1975), A Thousand Plateaus (1980) and What Is Philosophy? (1991).\n\nHis own books include Psychanalyse et transversalité (1972), Molecular Revolution: Psychiatry and Politics (1977), The Machinic Unconscious (1979), Schizoanalytic Cartographies (1989), The Three Ecologies (1989) and Chaosmosis: An Ethico-Aesthetic Paradigm (1992). In the 1980s he turned toward the environmental movement under the name of ecosophy.\n\nHe also wrote with others: Communists Like Us (1985) with Antonio Negri, Pratique de l'institutionnel et politique (1985) with Jean Oury and François Tosquelles, and Molecular Revolution in Brazil (1986) with Suely Rolnik. Les années d'hiver, 1980–1985 (1986) collects his writings from those years.\n\nGuattari died of a heart attack at La Borde on 29 August 1992, aged sixty-two. Several collections appeared after his death, including Chaosophy (1995), Soft Subversions (1996) and The Anti-Oedipus Papers (2006), his working notes for the first book with Deleuze.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A9lix_Guattari", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Félix Guattari”", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/deleuze/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Gilles Deleuze”" }] }],
  ["bourdieu", "Pierre Bourdieu", "Bourdieu", "1930–2002", "French sociologist of habitus, field and cultural capital.", ["Outline of a Theory of Practice (1972)", "Distinction (1979)"], ["marx", "weber", "mauss", "merleauponty"], { x: "Pierre Bourdieu was born in 1930 in Denguin, a village in the Béarn in southwestern France, the son of a postal worker. He studied philosophy at the École normale supérieure, a path that left him acutely aware of the distance between his origins and the French intellectual elite. Military service in Algeria during the war of independence led to fieldwork there and to his turn from philosophy to anthropology and sociology. He taught at the École des hautes études en sciences sociales and from 1981 held the chair of sociology at the Collège de France. In the 1990s he became a prominent public critic of neoliberalism. He died in 2002.\n\nHabitus, his central concept, names the system of durable dispositions, ways of perceiving, judging and acting, that people acquire through their social conditions and carry largely without awareness, so that their practices tend to reproduce the structures that formed them. He set it out in Outline of a Theory of Practice (1972) and The Logic of Practice (1980), against both structuralism, which ignored agents, and theories of free rational choice.\n\nSocial life is divided into fields, relatively autonomous arenas such as art, science, education, religion or politics, in which agents compete for the stakes specific to each. They compete with different forms of capital: economic capital; cultural capital, such as education, knowledge and taste; social capital, the resources of networks and connections; and symbolic capital, prestige and recognition, which works because it is not recognized as capital.\n\nDistinction (1979), based on large surveys of French tastes, argued that taste in food, music, art and sport classifies the classifier: judgments of taste express and reinforce social position, and the dominant classes' taste presents itself as natural refinement. With Jean-Claude Passeron, Reproduction (1970) showed how schools reproduce privilege by rewarding the cultural capital of the already advantaged as though it were individual merit. Bourdieu called this symbolic violence: domination exercised through categories that the dominated themselves accept, and doxa the unquestioned beliefs that make a social order seem self-evident.\n\nHe insisted on reflexivity, applying sociology to sociologists themselves, as in Homo Academicus (1984) on the French university, and The Rules of Art (1992) traced the formation of the literary field. Late works included The Weight of the World (1993), a collective study of social suffering, and On Television (1996). His concepts are among the most widely used in sociology, anthropology, education and cultural studies.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Bourdieu", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["derrida", "Jacques Derrida", "Derrida", "1930–2004", "French philosopher, founder of deconstruction.", ["Of Grammatology (1967)", "Writing and Difference (1967)", "Margins of Philosophy (1972)"], ["nietzsche", "saussure", "husserl", "heidegger"], { x: "Jacques Derrida was born in 1930 in El Biar, near Algiers, into a Sephardic Jewish family. In 1942, under Vichy's antisemitic laws, he was expelled from his lycée. He studied at the École normale supérieure in Paris, where he later taught, and from 1983 directed studies at the École des hautes études en sciences sociales; he also taught regularly at American universities, including Johns Hopkins, Yale and the University of California, Irvine. In 1992 Cambridge awarded him an honorary degree over the public objections of a group of analytic philosophers. He died of pancreatic cancer in 2004.\n\nIn 1967 he published three books, Of Grammatology, Writing and Difference and Voice and Phenomenon, in which he named his project deconstruction. Its target is what he called the metaphysics of presence: the tradition from Plato onward that grounds meaning in something fully present, such as the speaking voice, the living now, or the self-presence of consciousness, and that privileges speech over writing (phonocentrism) and logos over its others (logocentrism).\n\nDeconstruction works in two phases. It first reverses the hierarchical oppositions on which a text depends, such as speech and writing, nature and culture, presence and absence, and then shows that the devalued term was already at work in the privileged one, displacing the opposition itself. Writing, for example, turns out to name the general condition of all signification, speech included, since every sign must be repeatable in the absence of its speaker. No concept is pure: opposites contaminate each other.\n\nDifférance, spelled with an “a” that is silent in French, names at once the differing of signs from one another, from which Saussure said meaning arises, and the deferral of any final presence of meaning. Every element carries the trace of others. In Of Grammatology Derrida's reading of Rousseau's logic of the supplement, something added that both completes and replaces, led to the much-quoted claim that there is no outside-text: no meaning available apart from the play of signification.\n\nFrom the 1980s his writing turned increasingly ethical and political. “Force of Law” argued that justice exceeds law and is undeconstructible, so that a just decision must pass through the ordeal of undecidability. Specters of Marx (1993) defended a democracy to come, and later work treated hospitality, forgiveness, the gift, sovereignty, the death penalty and animals. His dense, playful style won him both a vast following in literary studies and fierce hostility among analytic philosophers.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/derrida/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["bell", "Derrick Bell", "Derrick Bell", "1930–2011", "American legal scholar, a founder of critical race theory.", ["Race, Racism, and American Law (1973)", "Faces at the Bottom of the Well (1992)"], [], { x: "Derrick Bell was born in Pittsburgh in 1930, served in the Air Force, and took his law degree at the University of Pittsburgh. He worked in the Civil Rights Division of the Justice Department, resigning when told to give up his NAACP membership, and then for the NAACP Legal Defense Fund, where he supervised more than three hundred school desegregation cases and helped James Meredith enter the University of Mississippi. He died in New York in 2011.\n\nIn 1971 he became the first tenured Black professor at Harvard Law School. He was later dean of the University of Oregon School of Law, and from 1991 taught at New York University. He took unpaid leaves from Harvard and finally gave up his position there to protest the absence of women of color on its law faculty, a protest that drew both support and criticism.\n\nHis casebook Race, Racism, and American Law (1973) is regarded as a founding text of critical race theory. Bell argued that racism is a permanent, structural feature of American life that reappears in new forms after each wave of reform, a position he called racial realism. His 1980 article on Brown v. Board of Education stated the principle of interest convergence: the interests of Black Americans in racial equality are accommodated only when they converge with the interests of whites, as desegregation served the United States' image abroad during the Cold War.\n\nHe also argued that civil rights lawyers had sometimes served the goals of their organizations at the expense of the communities they represented, pressing for integration where many Black parents wanted better schools. These arguments made him a central figure for the scholars who, in the 1980s, built critical race theory out of and against critical legal studies.\n\nBell developed a distinctive method of legal storytelling. And We Are Not Saved (1987) and Faces at the Bottom of the Well (1992) present arguments through fables and dialogues with a fictional interlocutor, Geneva Crenshaw. In the best-known, “The Space Traders,” aliens offer the United States unimaginable wealth in exchange for all its Black citizens, and the nation votes to accept.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derrick_Bell", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_race_theory", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Critical race theory”" }] }],
  ["dworkin", "Ronald Dworkin", "Dworkin", "1931–2013", "American legal and political philosopher of law as integrity.", ["Taking Rights Seriously (1977)", "Law's Empire (1986)"], [], { x: "Ronald Dworkin was born in Worcester, Massachusetts, in 1931, studied at Harvard and, as a Rhodes Scholar, at Oxford, and clerked for the judge Learned Hand. After practicing law in New York he taught at Yale Law School, and in 1969 succeeded H. L. A. Hart as Professor of Jurisprudence at Oxford. He later taught at New York University and University College London. A prolific essayist in The New York Review of Books, he received the Holberg Prize in 2007 and died in London in 2013.\n\nTaking Rights Seriously (1977) attacked Hart's legal positivism. Law, Dworkin argued, contains principles as well as rules: rules apply in an all-or-nothing way, while principles, such as that no one should profit from his own wrong, have weight and must be balanced. Since judges are bound by principles that no rule of recognition identifies, the positivist picture, on which judges exercise discretion when the rules run out, is mistaken. Even in hard cases there is usually one right answer. Individual rights are trumps that override collective goals.\n\nLaw's Empire (1986) developed law as integrity. Legal practice is interpretive: to state what the law is, a judge must offer the interpretation of past legal decisions that both fits them and shows them in their best moral light. Dworkin compared judges to authors of a chain novel, each writing a new chapter that must continue the story already written. His idealized judge, Hercules, has the time and knowledge to construct the theory that best fits and justifies the whole of the law. He also defended a moral reading of the United States Constitution.\n\nIn political philosophy, Sovereign Virtue (2000) argued for equality of resources: people should bear the consequences of their choices but not of their unchosen circumstances, such as natural talents and handicaps, which he modeled through a hypothetical auction and insurance market. He argued, against Isaiah Berlin, that liberty and equality, properly understood, need not conflict.\n\nLife's Dominion (1993) treated abortion and euthanasia as disputes about the sanctity of life, and his last major work, Justice for Hedgehogs (2011), defended the unity of value: ethical and moral values form a single interdependent whole, so that, like Archilochus's hedgehog, one may know one big thing. He was among the most cited legal scholars of the twentieth century.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Dworkin", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["taylor", "Charles Taylor", "Charles Taylor", "b. 1931", "Canadian philosopher of the self, recognition and secularity.", ["Sources of the Self (1989)", "“The Politics of Recognition” (1992)", "A Secular Age (2007)"], ["hegel"], { x: "Charles Taylor was born in Montreal in 1931 to an English-speaking father and a French-speaking mother. He studied history at McGill and, as a Rhodes Scholar, philosophy at Balliol College, Oxford, where he wrote his doctorate under Isaiah Berlin. He taught for most of his career at McGill, held the Chichele chair of social and political theory at Oxford, and later taught at Northwestern University. He ran several times for Parliament for the New Democratic Party in the 1960s, and in 2007 co-chaired with Gérard Bouchard Quebec's commission on the reasonable accommodation of cultural differences. His honors include the Templeton Prize (2007), the Kyoto Prize (2008) and the Berggruen Prize (2016).\n\nThe Explanation of Behavior (1964) criticized behaviorism and the naturalistic attempt to explain human action on the model of the natural sciences. Taylor defended an engaged hermeneutics: human beings are self-interpreting animals, whose interpretations of themselves partly make them what they are. His Hegel (1975) did much to revive interest in Hegel in English-speaking philosophy.\n\nSources of the Self (1989) traced the making of the modern identity. Selfhood is bound up with orientation to the good: we are who we are through strong evaluations, distinctions of worth between higher and lower ways of living that are not mere preferences. Modern identity draws on several sources, the inwardness descended from Augustine and Descartes, the affirmation of ordinary life of work and family, and a Romantic expressivism that sees nature as an inner source. The Malaize of Modernity (1991), published in the United States as The Ethics of Authenticity, defended the ideal of authenticity against its degraded forms.\n\n“The Politics of Recognition” (1992) argued that identity is formed dialogically, in relation to others, so that misrecognition, a demeaning picture reflected back by society, can be a real harm. He distinguished a politics of equal dignity from a politics of difference, and defended a liberalism that can accommodate collective goals such as the survival of French culture in Quebec. Taylor is often grouped with the communitarian critics of liberal individualism, a label he accepts only with reservations.\n\nA Secular Age (2007) asked how Western societies moved from a condition in which belief in God was virtually unchallengeable to one in which it is one option among others. Taylor rejected the subtraction story, on which religion simply fell away as science advanced, and described instead the construction of new social imaginaries and an immanent frame in which belief and unbelief are both contestable. The Language Animal (2016) argued that language constitutes as well as designates the world.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Taylor_(philosopher)", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["wiredu", "Kwasi Wiredu", "Wiredu", "1931–2022", "Ghanaian philosopher who called for conceptual decolonization.", ["Philosophy and an African Culture (1980)", "Cultural Universals and Particulars (1996)"], [], { x: "Kwasi Wiredu was born in Kumasi, in the Gold Coast, now Ghana, in 1931. He studied at the University of Ghana and then at Oxford, where Gilbert Ryle supervised his work. He taught at the University of Ghana for twenty-three years, later became a professor at the University of South Florida, and died in 2022, at ninety. He is often called the most important African philosopher of his generation.\n\nPhilosophy and an African Culture (1980), which won the Ghana National Book Award, set out the program for which he is best known: the conceptual decolonization of African philosophy. Many African thinkers, Wiredu argued, had absorbed philosophical concepts and problems through colonial languages, such as English or French, and through Christian and colonial education. They should test those concepts against their own languages, such as Akan, asking whether a problem stated in English can even be formulated in them, and keep or discard concepts on their merits.\n\nHe was equally critical of ethnophilosophy, the presentation of a people's communal beliefs as their philosophy. Folk thought, Wiredu insisted, must be distinguished from philosophy, which requires individual critical reflection and argument, and traditional thought should be examined critically, as any tradition should. He found such critical thinking already practiced in Akan communities, in long disputations among elders aimed at consensus.\n\nHe also wrote on truth, which in some writings he described, provocatively, as nothing but opinion, a perspectival account drawing on his reflection on the Akan language and on pragmatism. His account of the Akan concept of a person distinguished an ontological dimension from a normative one: on the normative side, personhood is something one achieves through moral conduct and responsibility to the community.\n\nCultural Universals and Particulars (1996) argued for universals grounded in shared human biology and reasoning alongside culturally particular concepts. Drawing on traditional Akan political practice, Wiredu defended a non-party democracy by consensus as an alternative to majoritarian, multiparty politics, which he thought deepened ethnic division in African states. He drew on Hume, Kant and Dewey as well as Akan thought.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwasi_Wiredu", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" }] }],
  ["hall", "Stuart Hall", "Stuart Hall", "1932–2014", "Jamaican-British cultural theorist of the Birmingham school.", ["“Encoding/Decoding” (1973)", "Policing the Crisis (1978, with others)"], ["gramsci", "althusser"], { x: "Stuart Hall was born in Kingston, Jamaica, in 1932 and went to Merton College, Oxford, as a Rhodes Scholar in 1951. He was a leading figure of the British New Left and the founding editor of New Left Review in 1960. In 1964 he joined Richard Hoggart's new Center for Contemporary Cultural Studies at the University of Birmingham and directed it from 1968 to 1979, then became professor of sociology at the Open University until 1997, reaching a wide public through its broadcasts. He died in London in 2014. With Hoggart and Raymond Williams he is regarded as a founder of British cultural studies.\n\nUnder Hall, the Birmingham Center turned cultural studies toward the analysis of youth subcultures, media, race and gender, drawing on Gramsci's concept of hegemony, Althusser's theory of ideology, semiotics and Foucault. Culture, for Hall, is a site where relations of power are established and contested, and his method of conjunctural analysis studied a historical moment as a meeting of economic, political and cultural forces.\n\nHis paper “Encoding and Decoding in the Television Discourse” (1973) argued that media messages are encoded with preferred meanings by their producers, but that audiences decode them from their own social positions. A reader may take up the dominant reading, a negotiated reading that accepts some elements and resists others, or an oppositional reading that understands the message and rejects it. The model replaced the idea of passive audiences.\n\nPolicing the Crisis (1978), written with colleagues, analyzed the moral panic over “mugging” in 1970s Britain, showing how crime statistics, police and media constructed a racialized threat that helped legitimate a turn to authoritarian law and order. In “The Great Moving Right Show” (1979) he coined the term “Thatcherism” and analyzed it as authoritarian populism, a hegemonic project that won popular consent for the free market and a strong state.\n\nIn “Cultural Identity and Diaspora” (1990) and essays on new ethnicities, Hall argued that cultural identities are positionings within histories, always in process, and not fixed essences, and that Caribbean and Black British identities are hybrid and diasporic. Representation (1997), an Open University textbook, set out a constructionist account of how meaning is produced in signs. He was widely called a godfather of multiculturalism.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_Hall_(cultural_theorist)", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["said", "Edward Said", "Said", "1935–2003", "Palestinian-American literary critic and a founder of postcolonial studies.", ["Orientalism (1978)", "Culture and Imperialism (1993)"], ["gramsci", "foucault"], { x: "Edward Said was born in Jerusalem in 1935, under the British Mandate, into a Palestinian Christian family. After the 1948 war the family settled in Cairo, and he was sent to school in the United States. He studied at Princeton and took his doctorate in English literature at Harvard, and he taught at Columbia University from 1963 until his death from leukemia in New York in 2003. He was also a gifted pianist and music critic, and in 1999 co-founded the West-Eastern Divan Orchestra of Israeli and Arab musicians with Daniel Barenboim.\n\nHis early books, Joseph Conrad and the Fiction of Autobiography (1966) and Beginnings: Intention and Method (1974), were works of literary theory. Orientalism (1978), his most influential book, argued that Western scholarship, literature and political writing about the Middle East and Asia formed a discourse, in Foucault's sense, that produced “the Orient” as Europe's exotic, backward and unchanging Other, and that this body of knowledge was bound up with colonial and imperial power. Drawing also on Gramsci's concept of hegemony, Said showed how scholars, novelists and administrators shared a system of representations that justified rule over those they described.\n\nOrientalism is widely credited with founding postcolonial studies. Culture and Imperialism (1993) extended the argument to the Western novel, from Jane Austen to Kipling and Conrad, reading canonical works contrapuntally, together with the imperial histories they presuppose or exclude, and with the cultures of resistance that answered them.\n\nSaid was the most prominent advocate of the Palestinian cause in the United States. The Question of Palestine (1979) and Covering Islam set out his political and media criticism. He served on the Palestinian National Council from 1977 to 1991, broke with Yasser Arafat over the Oslo Accords, which he regarded as a capitulation, and in his last years argued for a single binational state for Israelis and Palestinians.\n\nIn Representations of the Intellectual (1994) he described the intellectual's role as speaking truth to power, from the standpoint of an exile and amateur, never as a servant of a state or party. His memoir Out of Place (1999) told the story of his displaced childhood. Critics such as Bernard Lewis attacked Orientalism as polemical and unfair to scholarship, while supporters treat it as one of the founding books of modern cultural criticism.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Said", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Colonialism”" }] }],
  ["harding", "Sandra Harding", "Harding", "1935–2025", "American philosopher of science and of feminist standpoint epistemology.", ["The Science Question in Feminism (1986)", "Whose Science? Whose Knowledge? (1991)"], [], { x: "Sandra Harding was born in 1935 and earned her doctorate in philosophy in 1973. She taught at the State University of New York at Albany and the University of Delaware, and later at the University of California, Los Angeles, where she was Distinguished Professor of Education and Gender Studies; she also held an affiliate professorship in philosophy at Michigan State University. She died in 2025.\n\nThe Science Question in Feminism (1986) moved feminist criticism of science from the woman question in science, why so few women were scientists, to the science question in feminism: whether the sciences themselves, their problems, methods and claims to objectivity, are shaped by gender and other forms of social power. Harding distinguished three feminist responses: feminist empiricism, which holds that sexist bias is bad science to be corrected by better application of existing methods; feminist standpoint theory; and feminist postmodernism, which doubts any privileged vantage point.\n\nShe became the leading philosophical advocate of standpoint theory, developed with and alongside Nancy Hartsock and Dorothy Smith. Knowledge is socially situated, and starting research from the lives of marginalized groups, from questions their experience raises about dominant institutions, can produce less partial and distorted knowledge than starting from the lives of the powerful.\n\nWhose Science? Whose Knowledge? (1991) proposed strong objectivity. Conventional objectivity, Harding argued, is weak because it scrutinizes only the methods of individual researchers and ignores the social values shared by the whole scientific community that chooses what to study and how. Strong objectivity requires a strong reflexivity that examines those background assumptions too, which is best achieved by starting from marginalized standpoints.\n\nIs Science Multicultural? (1998), Science and Social Inequality (2006) and Objectivity and Diversity (2015) extended her work into postcolonial science studies, examining the knowledge traditions of non-Western cultures and the role of science in colonialism and development. Her work was influential across the social sciences and gender studies, and it was among the main targets of critics during the Science Wars of the 1990s.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandra_Harding", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["hacking", "Ian Hacking", "Hacking", "1936–2023", "Canadian philosopher of science, probability and kinds of people.", ["Representing and Intervening (1983)", "The Social Construction of What? (1999)"], ["foucault"], { x: "Ian Hacking was born in Vancouver in 1936. He studied at the University of British Columbia and at Cambridge, where he completed his doctorate in 1962. He taught at Stanford and from 1982 at the University of Toronto, where he became University Professor, and from 2000 to 2006 held the chair of philosophy and history of scientific concepts at the Collège de France. He received the Holberg Prize in 2009 and the Balzan Prize in 2014, and died in Toronto in 2023.\n\nThe Emergence of Probability (1975) argued that the modern concept of probability, with its two faces, the degree of belief warranted by evidence and the stable frequencies of chance processes, emerged suddenly around 1660, and asked what conditions made it thinkable. The Taming of Chance (1990) traced how nineteenth-century statistics, through an avalanche of printed numbers about crime, suicide and disease, made chance into law-like regularity and created the concept of the normal.\n\nRepresenting and Intervening (1983) shifted the debate over scientific realism from theories to experiments. Hacking defended entity realism: we have good reason to believe in entities such as electrons when we can use them to intervene in the world and investigate other phenomena, whatever we think of the theories that describe them. Of electrons he wrote that if you can spray them, then they are real.\n\nFrom around 1990 he turned to the human sciences, influenced by Foucault. Kinds of people, such as the multiple personality, the child abuser or the autistic, are made up as well as discovered: classifying people changes how they understand themselves and behave, which in turn changes the classification, a looping effect that distinguishes human kinds from natural kinds. He called this view dynamic nominalism and studied cases in Rewriting the Soul (1995) on multiple personality and Mad Travelers (1998) on a nineteenth-century epidemic of compulsive traveling.\n\nThe Social Construction of What? (1999) dissected the many claims that something is socially constructed, distinguishing what exactly is said to be constructed and in what sense. Historical Ontology (2002) gathered his essays on how objects, concepts and ways of being come into existence historically, and he also wrote on styles of scientific reasoning, such as the laboratory, statistical and taxonomic styles.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ian_Hacking", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["nagel", "Thomas Nagel", "Nagel", "b. 1937", "American philosopher of mind and ethics.", ["“What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974)", "The View from Nowhere (1986)"], [], { x: "Thomas Nagel was born in Belgrade in 1937 to German Jewish refugees and grew up in New York. He studied at Cornell and at Corpus Christi College, Oxford, and took his doctorate at Harvard under John Rawls. He taught at Berkeley and Princeton and then for most of his career at New York University, where he is University Professor Emeritus.\n\nThe Possibility of Altruism (1970) defended a rationalist, Kantian account of practical reason: the recognition that others are as real as oneself gives reasons to act for their sake, with no need for a prior desire. His later work distinguished agent-relative reasons, which hold for a particular person, from agent-neutral reasons, which hold for anyone.\n\n“What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974) argued that an organism has conscious mental states only if there is something it is like to be that organism, for the organism itself. A bat perceives through echolocation, and however much we learned of its neurophysiology, we would not know what its experience is like from its point of view. Physicalist theories, which describe the world from an objective standpoint, leave this subjective character of experience out. The paper became one of the most discussed in the philosophy of mind.\n\nMortal Questions (1979) collected essays including “Moral Luck,” which showed how much of what we are judged for depends on factors beyond our control, such as the consequences of a reckless act, and “The Absurd,” which located the sense of life's absurdity in the clash between the seriousness with which we live and the detached perspective from which everything seems arbitrary. The View from Nowhere (1986) generalized the theme: the drive toward an objective conception of the world, a view from nowhere, is essential to knowledge and ethics, yet the most objective view is not always the most complete, since it cannot absorb the subjective standpoint.\n\nLater books include the introduction What Does It All Mean? (1987), Equality and Partiality (1991), The Last Word (1997), a defense of reason against relativism, and The Myth of Ownership (2002, with Liam Murphy) on taxation and justice. Mind and Cosmos (2012) argued that the materialist neo-Darwinian picture of nature cannot account for consciousness, reason and value, and that teleological principles may be needed; an atheist, Nagel drew sharp criticism for the book from many scientists and philosophers.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Nagel", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["nozick", "Robert Nozick", "Nozick", "1938–2002", "American libertarian political philosopher.", ["Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974)"], ["locke", "kant"], { x: "Robert Nozick was born in Brooklyn in 1938, studied at Columbia and took his doctorate at Princeton. He joined the Harvard philosophy department in 1969, where he and John Rawls were colleagues for three decades, and taught there until his death in 2002. His work ranged across political philosophy, epistemology, decision theory and the meaning of life.\n\nAnarchy, State, and Utopia (1974) begins: individuals have rights, and there are things no person or group may do to them without violating their rights. Rights act as side constraints on action, limits on what may be done to people even in pursuit of good social goals, because persons are separate and inviolable: sacrificing one person for others uses him, ignoring that his life is the only one he has. From this Nozick argued that only a minimal state, limited to protecting people against force, theft and fraud and enforcing contracts, can be justified; any more extensive state violates rights.\n\nPart I answers the anarchist. Starting from a Lockean state of nature, Nozick traced how private protective associations would arise, how one would become dominant in a territory, and how, by prohibiting risky private enforcement and compensating those it prohibits, it would become a minimal state without violating anyone's rights.\n\nPart II sets out an entitlement theory of justice in holdings. A distribution is just if everyone is entitled to what they hold: if their holdings arose by just acquisition of unowned things, subject to a Lockean proviso that others are not made worse off, by just transfer, or by rectification of past injustice. Justice is historical, depending on how a distribution came about. Against patterned principles such as Rawls's difference principle, Nozick offered the Wilt Chamberlain argument: if a million fans freely pay to watch a great player, the resulting inequality arises from just steps, and preserving any pattern would require continual interference with free exchanges. He argued that taxation of earnings is on a par with forced labor.\n\nPart III proposes a framework for utopia, in which people can found and leave diverse communities under the minimal state. The book also introduced the experience machine, a thought experiment against hedonism: most of us would not plug into a machine that gave us any experiences we wanted, which suggests we care about more than how life feels. In Philosophical Explanations (1981) Nozick proposed a tracking account of knowledge, and in The Examined Life (1989) he seemed to moderate some of his libertarian views, though he later downplayed any break.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nozick-political/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy" }],
  ["harman", "Gilbert Harman", "Harman", "1938–2021", "American philosopher of reasoning and ethics.", ["“The Inference to the Best Explanation” (1965)"], ["quine"], { x: "Gilbert Harman was born in 1938, studied at Swarthmore and took his doctorate at Harvard under W. V. O. Quine. He taught at Princeton from 1963 until he retired in 2017, and co-directed its Cognitive Science Laboratory with the psychologist George Miller. He died in 2021. His work ranged over epistemology, philosophy of mind and language, ethics and statistical learning theory.\n\nHis paper “The Inference to the Best Explanation” (1965) gave a name to a form of reasoning by which we infer, from the fact that a hypothesis would best explain the evidence, that the hypothesis is true. Harman argued that much inductive reasoning, and much of our knowledge, rests on such inference. In responding to Gettier's counterexamples he proposed that knowledge requires reasoning that does not essentially depend on any false lemma.\n\nChange in View (1986) argued that logic is not a theory of reasoning. Logic concerns implication and consistency, relations among propositions; reasoning is a change in what one believes or intends, guided by considerations such as conservatism, keeping beliefs one already has unless there is reason to give them up, and coherence. That a set of beliefs implies a conclusion does not by itself mean one ought to believe it, since one may instead have reason to abandon one of the beliefs.\n\nIn ethics, The Nature of Morality (1977) posed a problem for moral realism. We explain observations in science by appeal to physical facts, but moral judgments, such as that it is wrong for children to set a cat on fire, seem fully explained by our upbringing and psychology without appeal to moral facts. Harman also defended a version of moral relativism, on which moral judgments make sense only relative to agreements or frameworks shared by those concerned.\n\nIn philosophy of mind he defended the transparency of experience: when we attend to our perceptual experience, we find only the features of what is experienced, its colors and shapes, and no intrinsic qualities of the experience itself, which supports an account of experience in terms of its intentional content. He also drew on social psychology to argue that there is little evidence for the stable character traits presupposed by virtue ethics, a position known as situationism.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_Harman", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["vogel", "Lise Vogel", "Lise Vogel", "b. 1938", "American Marxist-feminist sociologist and art historian.", ["Marxism and the Oppression of Women (1983)"], ["marx"], { x: "Lise Vogel was born in New York City in 1938, the daughter of a doctor who had volunteered for the Spanish Republic. She graduated from Radcliffe in 1960 and took a doctorate in art history at Harvard in 1968. In 1964-65 she worked with the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee in Mississippi, and in the late 1960s she joined Bread and Roses, a socialist-feminist group in Boston. After teaching art history at Brown and writing on feminist art history, she earned a second doctorate, in sociology, at Brandeis in 1981, and taught sociology at Rider University until she retired in 2003.\n\nHer major work, Marxism and the Oppression of Women: Toward a Unitary Theory (1983), asked how Marxism could explain women's oppression. Socialist feminists of the 1970s had often adopted dual-systems theories, which treated capitalism and patriarchy as two separate structures to be combined. Vogel rejected this approach. Marxist theory, she argued, does not need to be supplemented by a separate theory of patriarchy; it needs to be developed on its own ground.\n\nHer starting point was a question Marx raised but did not answer: how labor power, the capacity to work that capitalists buy, is itself produced and renewed. It is reproduced daily, through food, rest and care, and across generations, through childbearing and child rearing. Because women bear children, and because this work is mostly done in households outside the market, women's oppression in capitalist societies is rooted in their place in the social reproduction of labor power.\n\nIn the domestic labor debate of the period Vogel treated domestic labor as part of the necessary labor that reproduces workers. It produces use-values, not surplus value, and so is not productive labor in Marx's technical sense, yet capital depends on it. Her account thus explained women's oppression from within the logic of capital accumulation. Later books include Mothers on the Job (1993), on maternity policy in the workplace, and Woman Questions (1995).\n\nThe book had limited impact in the 1980s, as feminist theory moved away from Marxism. Its reissue in 2013, in the Historical Materialism book series, made it the founding text of a revived social reproduction theory; Vogel wrote the preface to Tithi Bhattacharya's collection Social Reproduction Theory (2017). Critics such as Himani Bannerji have questioned its treatment of race.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lise_Vogel", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["gyekye", "Kwame Gyekye", "Gyekye", "1939–2019", "Ghanaian philosopher of moderate communitarianism.", ["An Essay on African Philosophical Thought (1987)", "Tradition and Modernity (1997)"], [], { x: "Kwame Gyekye was born in 1939 in Ahanta, in the Gold Coast, now Ghana. He studied at the University of Ghana, at Oxford and at Harvard, where he took his doctorate with a thesis on Graeco-Arabic philosophy, and his first book was a study of Arabic logic. He taught at the University of Ghana for more than forty years, chaired its philosophy department, and was a visiting professor at Temple University. He died in 2019.\n\nAn Essay on African Philosophical Thought: The Akan Conceptual Scheme (1987) argued that the thought of the Akan peoples of Ghana contains genuine philosophy, rigorous enough to belong in the world's philosophical canon. Against critics of ethnophilosophy such as Paulin Hountondji, Gyekye held that traditional African thought includes the reflective work of individual thinkers, even when their names have been lost, and can be studied philosophically. His position put him in debate with Kwasi Wiredu, Hountondji and Henry Odera Oruka, with whom he kept warm relations.\n\nHis account of the Akan concept of a person holds that persons have a divine origin and intrinsic worth. Every human being has equal personhood from birth. Against Wiredu, who treated personhood as partly a normative status one achieves, Gyekye held that moral achievement concerns how good a person one is, not whether one is a person at all.\n\nGyekye is best known for moderate communitarianism. Ifeanyi Menkiti had argued that in African thought the community defines the person, who becomes a person only through incorporation into it. Gyekye called this radical communitarianism and objected that it subordinates individual self-realization, rights and dignity to social obligations. Persons are by nature social and depend on community, but they also have rational and moral capacities of their own, including the capacity to question and revise communal values. A good community recognizes both duties to others and individual rights.\n\nTradition and Modernity: Philosophical Reflections on the African Experience (1997) applied this view to the problems of postcolonial African states, from ethnicity and nationhood to political legitimacy, corruption and the relation between inherited traditions and modern science and technology. Gyekye argued that African societies should critically select from their traditions what serves human well-being.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwame_Gyekye", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["menkiti", "Ifeanyi Menkiti", "Menkiti", "1940–2019", "Nigerian philosopher of personhood and community.", ["“Person and Community in African Traditional Thought” (1984)"], [], { x: "Ifeanyi Menkiti was born in Onitsha, Nigeria, in 1940 and came to the United States in 1961. He graduated from Pomona College, studied at Columbia and New York University, and took his doctorate in philosophy at Harvard in 1974 with a dissertation on collective responsibility. From 1974 he taught philosophy at Wellesley College. He was also a poet, author of collections including Affirmations and The Jubilation of Falling Bodies, and for decades owned the Grolier Poetry Book Shop in Cambridge, Massachusetts. He died in 2019.\n\nHis essay “Person and Community in African Traditional Thought” (1984) became one of the most discussed texts in African philosophy. It contrasted the African view of the person with the dominant Western view, on which a person is defined by some isolated static quality, such as rationality, will or memory, that each individual possesses on his own.\n\nIn the African view, Menkiti argued, it is the community that defines the person. The reality of the communal world takes priority over the reality of individual life histories, and the individual comes to know himself through the community. He took as his guide John Mbiti's dictum: I am because we are, and since we are, therefore I am.\n\nPersonhood, on this account, is something acquired, by degrees, through a long process of social and ritual incorporation and through taking up one's moral responsibilities to others. One is not born a person in the full sense. Elders, who have lived through that process, are more fully persons than the young, and the ancestors who are remembered continue in a sense to be persons. Menkiti noted, as a linguistic sign of the same intuition, the English habit of calling a newborn child “it.”\n\nKwame Gyekye called this view radical communitarianism and replied with a moderate communitarianism that gives more weight to individual rights and to the person's capacity to question communal values. The exchange set the terms of a continuing debate about personhood, community and rights in African philosophy.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ifeanyi_Menkiti", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwame_Gyekye", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Kwame Gyekye”" }] }],
  ["robinson", "Cedric J. Robinson", "Robinson", "1940–2016", "American political theorist of the Black radical tradition.", ["Black Marxism (1983)"], ["marx", "dubois"], { x: "Cedric J. Robinson was born in 1940 and raised in Oakland, California. He studied social anthropology at the University of California, Berkeley, where he was active in Black radical student politics, and took his doctorate in political theory at Stanford in 1974. After teaching at Michigan and at Binghamton, he joined the University of California, Santa Barbara, in 1978, where he chaired the departments of Black Studies and Political Science and directed the Center for Black Studies Research. He also co-founded a long-running radio and later television program, Third World News Review. He died in 2016.\n\nHis first book, The Terms of Order (1980), was a critique of Western political theory's assumption that social order requires leadership and authority. Robinson argued that this assumption was a myth, and drew on African traditions of social organization that did without centralized power.\n\nBlack Marxism: The Making of the Black Radical Tradition (1983), his major work, argued that capitalism developed from the start as racial capitalism. European feudal society was already organized through racial distinctions among Europeans, such as those applied to the Irish, Slavs and Jews, and capitalism extended these into the systems of slavery, colonialism and imperialism. Race was therefore not a distortion of an otherwise colorblind capitalism; racialism shaped capitalist development from its origins.\n\nMarxism, Robinson argued, shared the Eurocentrism of the civilization it criticized and could not fully grasp the experience of enslaved and colonized peoples. Out of that experience came a distinct Black radical tradition, rooted in African cultures and in resistance to slavery, from maroon communities and slave revolts to the Haitian Revolution. In the book's final part he traced how three thinkers, W. E. B. Du Bois, C. L. R. James and Richard Wright, came to Marxism and moved beyond it toward that tradition.\n\nHis later works include Black Movements in America (1997), An Anthropology of Marxism (2001) and Forgeries of Memory and Meaning (2007), on race in American theater and early film. Little noticed at first, Black Marxism gained wide influence in the 2000s and 2010s, and racial capitalism became a central concept in Black studies, history and political theory.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Robinson", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["kripke", "Saul Kripke", "Kripke", "1940–2022", "American logician and philosopher of naming, necessity and rule-following.", ["Naming and Necessity (1980)", "Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982)"], [], { x: "Saul Kripke was born in Bay Shore, New York, in 1940 and grew up in Omaha, Nebraska. While still in high school he proved a completeness theorem for modal logic, published in 1959. He studied at Harvard and taught at Rockefeller University, Princeton and, from 2003, the City University of New York Graduate Center. He died in 2022.\n\nKripke semantics gave modal logic, the logic of necessity and possibility, a rigorous model theory. A statement is necessary at a world if it is true at every world accessible from it, and possible if it is true at some accessible world; varying the accessibility relation yields the different systems of modal logic. The framework became a standard tool in logic, philosophy, linguistics and computer science.\n\nNaming and Necessity, lectures given at Princeton in 1970 and published as a book in 1980, overturned the descriptivist theory of names associated with Frege and Russell, on which a name means some description that picks out its bearer. Kripke argued that names are rigid designators: “Aristotle” refers to the same person in every possible world in which he exists, even worlds in which he did none of the things we use to describe him. Reference is fixed by an initial baptism and passed along a causal-historical chain of speakers.\n\nKripke separated the metaphysical notion of necessity from the epistemic notion of the a priori. Some necessary truths can be known only by experience, such as “Hesperus is Phosphorus” or “water is H2O”; and some contingent truths can be known a priori, such as that the standard meter stick is one meter long, by the person who fixes the meter's reference by it. The lectures revived essentialism and metaphysics in analytic philosophy, and closed with a modal argument against the identity of pain with any brain state.\n\nWittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982) presented a skeptical paradox drawn from Wittgenstein: nothing in a person's past behavior or mind seems to fix whether by “plus” she meant addition or “quus,” a function that agrees with addition for all the numbers she has used so far. Kripke offered a “skeptical solution” that grounds meaning in the practices of a community. The reading, often called Kripkenstein, became one of the most debated topics in philosophy of language. His “Outline of a Theory of Truth” (1975) showed how a language can contain its own truth predicate.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saul_Kripke", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["pateman", "Carole Pateman", "Pateman", "b. 1940", "British feminist political theorist.", ["The Sexual Contract (1988)"], ["rousseau"], { x: "Carole Pateman was born in Maresfield, Sussex, in 1940. She left school at sixteen, studied at Ruskin College, Oxford, a college for working adults, and then at Lady Margaret Hall, Oxford, where she took her doctorate. She taught political theory at the University of Sydney from 1972 and at the University of California, Los Angeles, from 1990, where she is Distinguished Professor Emeritus. She was the first woman president of the International Political Science Association (1991-94) and later president of the American Political Science Association.\n\nParticipation and Democratic Theory (1970) challenged the elitist theories of democracy then dominant in political science, which saw low participation as a sign of stability. Drawing on Rousseau, John Stuart Mill and G. D. H. Cole, Pateman argued that participation, especially in the workplace, educates citizens in democracy, and that a democratic society requires democratic institutions beyond elections. The Problem of Political Obligation (1979) criticized liberal theories of why citizens should obey the state.\n\nThe Sexual Contract (1988), her best-known book, rereads the social contract tradition of Hobbes, Locke and Rousseau. The story of the original contract, Pateman argued, is half a story. The social contract by which free and equal individuals create civil society presupposes a prior sexual contract that establishes men's political right over women, especially their access to women's bodies. Modern patriarchy is fraternal: power passes from the father to the brotherhood of men.\n\nContract, on her account, is a means of creating relations of subordination as well as freedom. She traced the sexual contract in the marriage contract, the prostitution contract and the surrogacy contract, and compared them to the employment contract, in which a worker sells the use of his own person, and to the slave contract. In Contract and Domination (2007) she and Charles Mills joined her analysis of the sexual contract with his of the racial contract.\n\nThe Disorder of Women (1989) collected her essays on democracy, feminism and political theory. Pateman has also argued for an unconditional basic income as a way of increasing freedom and democratizing social life, by freeing people, women especially, from dependence on employment and marriage.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carole_Pateman", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["kristeva", "Julia Kristeva", "Kristeva", "b. 1941", "Bulgarian-French semiotician and psychoanalyst.", ["Revolution in Poetic Language (1974)", "Powers of Horror (1980)"], ["lacan"], { x: "Julia Kristeva was born in Sliven, Bulgaria, in 1941 and moved to Paris on a research fellowship in December 1965. She studied with Lucien Goldmann and Roland Barthes, joined the avant-garde circle of the review Tel Quel, led by the novelist Philippe Sollers, whom she married, and later trained as a psychoanalyst. She taught for many years at the University of Paris, as well as at Columbia, and received the Holberg Prize in 2004. In 2018 Bulgaria's commission on state security files said she had been registered as an informant in the communist era; she called the claim false.\n\nIn her early semiotic work, Séméiôtiké (1969), Kristeva introduced the concept of intertextuality, drawing on Mikhail Bakhtin: every text is built as a mosaic of quotations, the absorption and transformation of other texts.\n\nRevolution in Poetic Language (1974) distinguished two dimensions of signification. The symbolic is the order of language as structure, grammar, law and meaning. The semiotic is the pre-linguistic dimension of drives, rhythms and tones, rooted in the body and in the infant's relation to the mother, which Kristeva linked to Plato's term chora, a receptacle. All signifying combines the two, and poetic language, as in Mallarmé or Lautréamont, lets the semiotic disrupt the symbolic. The speaking subject is therefore a subject in process, never fixed.\n\nPowers of Horror (1980) developed the concept of abjection: the reaction of horror to what threatens the boundaries of the self, such as bodily fluids, waste and the corpse, which the subject must expel to establish itself. Abjection begins with the infant's separation from the maternal body, and Kristeva traced how cultures and religions organize the abject through rituals of purity and defilement. The concept has been widely used in literary, film and feminist criticism.\n\nLater works include Tales of Love (1983), Black Sun (1987) on depression and melancholia, Strangers to Ourselves (1988) on the foreigner, the Female Genius trilogy on Arendt, Melanie Klein and Colette, and several novels. She is counted among the major French feminist theorists, though her relation to the feminist movement has been ambivalent and she has criticized collective identity politics. Critics have faulted her writing on China and Islam, and Alan Sokal and Jean Bricmont criticized her use of mathematics.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Kristeva", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["spivak", "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak", "Spivak", "b. 1942", "Indian literary theorist and translator of Derrida's Of Grammatology.", ["“Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988)", "A Critique of Postcolonial Reason (1999)"], ["marx", "gramsci", "derrida"], { x: "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak was born in Calcutta in 1942 and graduated from Presidency College in 1959. She went to Cornell, where she wrote her doctorate under Paul de Man, and taught at Iowa, Texas, Emory and Pittsburgh before joining Columbia University in 1991, where she became University Professor in 2007. Since 1986 she has also taught in schools for landless and illiterate Dalit children in western West Bengal, which she supported with her Kyoto Prize award of 2012.\n\nHer translation of Jacques Derrida's Of Grammatology (1976) and its long translator's preface introduced deconstruction to many English-speaking readers. In Other Worlds (1987) brought deconstruction, Marxism and feminism together in readings of literature, culture and the international division of labor.\n\n“Can the Subaltern Speak?” (1988), her most famous essay, began by criticizing a conversation between Foucault and Deleuze in which, she argued, Western intellectuals who claimed to let the oppressed speak for themselves concealed their own role in representing them. Spivak called the colonial constitution of the colonized as Other epistemic violence. Her central case was the British abolition of sati, widow-burning, in India, which she described in the sentence “white men are saving brown women from brown men,” set against the nativist claim that the women wanted to die. Between these two discourses, the subaltern woman herself has no position from which to be heard. The essay's answer to its title was that the subaltern cannot speak, a claim she later qualified.\n\nSpivak engaged critically with the Subaltern Studies historians, whose recovery of subaltern consciousness she both supported and questioned. She proposed strategic essentialism, the temporary adoption of a shared identity by diverse groups for political action, though she later distanced herself from the phrase.\n\nA Critique of Postcolonial Reason (1999) traced the figure of the native informant through Kant, Hegel and Marx, and introduced the notion of sanctioned ignorance, a learned dismissal of the context that would challenge one's view. Death of a Discipline (2003) called for a renewed comparative literature, and An Aesthetic Education in the Era of Globalization (2012) collected her essays on the humanities. Her prose has been criticized as obscure, and defended as a refusal of easy transparency.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gayatri_Chakravorty_Spivak", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/colonialism/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Colonialism”" }] }],
  ["federici", "Silvia Federici", "Federici", "b. 1942", "Italian-American Marxist feminist and Wages for Housework activist.", ["Caliban and the Witch (2004)", "Revolution at Point Zero (2012)"], ["marx"], { x: "Silvia Federici was born in Parma, Italy, in 1942 and came to the United States in 1967 on a Fulbright scholarship to study philosophy at the University at Buffalo. In 1972 she co-founded the International Feminist Collective with Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James, which launched the international Wages for Housework campaign. She taught at the University of Port Harcourt in Nigeria in the 1980s and then at Hofstra University, where she is professor emerita, and in 1990 co-founded the Committee for Academic Freedom in Africa.\n\nHer pamphlet Wages Against Housework (1975) argued that the unwaged work women do in the home, cooking, cleaning, caring and raising children, is work, and that it produces the most essential commodity of capitalism: the worker's capacity to labor. By demanding wages for housework, women would make that work visible as labor, refuse the ideology that it is a natural expression of femininity, and gain the power to refuse it. The point of the demand was to subvert the role of housewife.\n\nCaliban and the Witch: Women, the Body and Primitive Accumulation (2004), her best-known book, reinterpreted the transition from feudalism to capitalism. Marx's primitive accumulation, the violent expropriation that created a class of propertyless workers, had a gendered side that Marx ignored. The great European witch hunts of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, Federici argued, served to destroy women's control over their reproduction and their traditional knowledge, to discipline the female body as a machine for producing new workers, and to confine women to unpaid reproductive labor.\n\nShe linked this to the enclosure of the commons and to colonial expropriation in the Americas, where witch hunts were also used against Indigenous people. Primitive accumulation, on her account, is a continuing feature of capitalism, and institutions such as the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank pursue new enclosures of land and resources in the global South. Capitalism was a counter-revolution against the communal struggles of the late Middle Ages.\n\nLater books include Revolution at Point Zero (2012), Witches, Witch-Hunting, and Women (2018) and Re-enchanting the World: Feminism and the Politics of the Commons (2018). Caliban and the Witch has been translated into many languages and is widely read in feminist and anticapitalist movements, while some historians have questioned its use of evidence on the witch hunts.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silvia_Federici", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["hountondji", "Paulin Hountondji", "Hountondji", "1942–2024", "Beninese philosopher and critic of ethnophilosophy.", ["African Philosophy: Myth and Reality (1976)"], ["husserl", "althusser"], { x: "Paulin Hountondji was born in Abidjan in 1942 into a Beninese family. He studied at the École normale supérieure in Paris, where his teachers included Louis Althusser and Jacques Derrida, and completed a doctorate on Husserl in 1970. After teaching in France and Zaire he joined the National University of Benin in Cotonou, where he taught philosophy for decades. After Benin's return to democracy he served as minister of education and then of culture and communication, resigning in 1994 to return to the university. He later directed the African Center for Advanced Studies in Porto-Novo, and died in Cotonou in 2024.\n\nHis book Sur la “philosophie africaine” (1976), published in English as African Philosophy: Myth and Reality (1983), is one of the founding works of modern African philosophy. Its target was what Hountondji named ethnophilosophy: works such as Placide Tempels's Bantu Philosophy and Alexis Kagame's study of Rwandan thought, which presented the collective, implicit worldview of a whole people as their philosophy.\n\nEthnophilosophy, he argued, rests on a myth of unanimism, the assumption that all members of a society share one system of beliefs to which they unanimously subscribe. It is a hybrid discipline, mixing ethnography and philosophy without the standards of either, and it arose as a reply to European views of Africans, addressed to a European audience. Philosophy, on Hountondji's account, is a body of explicit texts, produced by individuals who argue, criticize and disagree with one another. African philosophy is therefore the literature produced by Africans working in philosophy, a tradition still to be built through debate.\n\nCritics charged that this definition was elitist and Eurocentric, since it required writing and seemed to exclude oral and traditional thought; Henry Odera Oruka's sage philosophy was in part a reply. Hountondji held to his rejection of ethnophilosophy while increasingly seeking to bring traditional African knowledge under rigorous examination.\n\nHis later work turned to the politics of knowledge. He criticized the extraversion of science in Africa, its dependence on research agendas, institutions and markets located in the North, and called for the critical study and development of endogenous knowledge, the local knowledge systems of African societies. The Struggle for Meaning (1997; English 2002) is his intellectual autobiography.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paulin_J._Hountondji", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" }] }],
  ["agamben", "Giorgio Agamben", "Agamben", "b. 1942", "Italian philosopher of sovereignty, bare life and the state of exception.", ["Homo Sacer (1995)", "State of Exception (2003)"], ["heidegger", "benjamin", "schmitt", "foucault"], { x: "Giorgio Agamben was born in Rome in 1942 and took his degree in law in 1965 with a thesis on the political thought of Simone Weil. He attended Heidegger's seminars at Le Thor in 1966 and 1968, played the apostle Philip in Pasolini's film The Gospel According to St Matthew, and edited the Italian edition of Walter Benjamin's works. He has taught at Macerata, Verona and the IUAV in Venice, and at the European Graduate School. In 2004 he cancelled a teaching visit to the United States in protest against its biometric fingerprinting of visitors.\n\nHis early books, Stanzas (1977), Infancy and History (1978) and Language and Death (1982), studied language, poetry and the experience of being a speaking being. The Coming Community (1990) imagined a community of whatever singularities, belonging together without any shared identity or condition of membership.\n\nHomo Sacer: Sovereign Power and Bare Life (1995) opened a series of nine volumes completed with The Use of Bodies (2014). Agamben took up Foucault's concept of biopolitics and Schmitt's definition of the sovereign as the one who decides on the exception. The Greeks distinguished zoē, the bare fact of living, from bios, a qualified form of life; Western politics, he argued, has always been founded on the inclusion of bare life by way of its exclusion. The figure of the homo sacer in archaic Roman law, a man who could be killed with impunity but not sacrificed, embodies this bare life, exposed to sovereign power.\n\nThe concentration camp, on this account, is the space that opens when the state of exception becomes the rule, and it is the hidden paradigm of modern political space. State of Exception (2003) traced the growing use of emergency powers in modern democracies, and Remnants of Auschwitz (1998) reflected on testimony and the figure of the Muselmann in the camps. Later volumes, including The Kingdom and the Glory (2007), traced the theological roots of modern government, and Agamben developed the concepts of inoperativity, the deactivation of the works and functions assigned to life, and form-of-life, a life inseparable from its form.\n\nDuring the COVID-19 pandemic Agamben attacked lockdowns, mask mandates and vaccine passes as a further expansion of the state of exception. Many philosophers and public health experts rejected his interventions, which divided even his admirers. His concepts of bare life, the exception and the camp have been widely used in political theory, legal studies and refugee studies.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giorgio_Agamben", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["hartsock", "Nancy Hartsock", "Hartsock", "1943–2015", "American political theorist of the feminist standpoint.", ["Money, Sex, and Power (1983)"], ["marx"], { x: "Nancy Hartsock was born in Ogden, Utah, in 1943 and took her doctorate in political science at the University of Chicago in 1972. She taught at the University of Michigan and Johns Hopkins, and from 1984 to 2009 at the University of Washington. She was president of the Western Political Science Association and co-founded the Center for Women and Democracy in Seattle. She died in 2015.\n\nHer essay “The Feminist Standpoint: Developing the Ground for a Specifically Feminist Historical Materialism” (1983) is a founding text of feminist standpoint theory. Hartsock started from Marx and Lukács, who argued that the proletariat's position in production gives it a standpoint from which the structure of capitalist society can be seen as it really is, while the ruling class's view is partial and inverted.\n\nShe argued that women's lives, shaped by the sexual division of labor, give rise to a parallel standpoint. Women's work in the household and in caring for bodies and children is concrete, sensuous and tied to the reproduction of life, and it gives a vantage point from which the abstract, dualistic outlook of masculine thought and institutions can be criticized. She drew on Melanie Klein's psychoanalysis and object relations theory to explain how boys and girls develop different relations to others and to the world.\n\nA standpoint, for Hartsock, is achieved through struggle and analysis, as class consciousness was for Marxists, and it carries a liberatory vision of a world organized differently.\n\nMoney, Sex, and Power: Toward a Feminist Historical Materialism (1983) developed the argument through a critique of theories of power, and The Feminist Standpoint Revisited and Other Essays (1998) responded to critics, including postmodern feminists who doubted that any group could claim a privileged standpoint. Her work, with that of Dorothy Smith, Sandra Harding and Patricia Hill Collins, set the terms of debate on standpoint epistemology.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nancy_Hartsock", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["haraway", "Donna Haraway", "Haraway", "b. 1944", "American theorist of science, technology and feminism.", ["“A Cyborg Manifesto” (1985)", "“Situated Knowledges” (1988)", "Simians, Cyborgs, and Women (1991)"], ["marx", "foucault"], { x: "Donna Haraway was born in Denver in 1944. She studied zoology and philosophy at Colorado College and took her doctorate in biology at Yale in 1972. She taught at the University of Hawaii and Johns Hopkins, and from 1980 in the History of Consciousness program at the University of California, Santa Cruz, where she held what was described as the first tenured position in feminist theory in the United States. She is professor emerita there. Her honors include the Ludwik Fleck Prize, the J. D. Bernal Award and, in 2025, the Erasmus Prize.\n\nHer dissertation, published as Crystals, Fabrics, and Fields (1976), studied the metaphors that organized twentieth-century developmental biology. Primate Visions (1989) examined primatology as a site where gender, race and nature were constructed, read through the field's stories, institutions and colonial settings.\n\n“A Cyborg Manifesto” (1985), her best-known essay, proposed the cyborg, a hybrid of organism and machine, as an ironic political myth for socialist feminism at the end of the twentieth century. The boundaries between human and animal, organism and machine, and physical and non-physical had broken down, Haraway argued, and feminism should embrace that breakdown and hold its contradictions together, giving up a natural, unified identity of woman as the ground of politics. The essay ends: I would rather be a cyborg than a goddess.\n\n“Situated Knowledges” (1988) answered the problem of objectivity. The claim to see everything from nowhere, which she called the god trick, is an illusion of disembodied vision. Objectivity is instead a matter of situated knowledges, partial, located and accountable perspectives, joined in webs of connection, a position between relativism and the view from nowhere. Both essays were collected in Simians, Cyborgs, and Women (1991).\n\nIn The Companion Species Manifesto (2003) and When Species Meet (2008) she turned from cyborgs to dogs and other companion species, studying the intertwined lives she called naturecultures. Staying with the Trouble (2016) proposed the Chthulucene, an alternative to the Anthropocene centered on multispecies entanglement, and the slogan make kin, not babies, which drew criticism for its implications about population. Her playful, dense style has been both admired and criticized as obscure.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donna_Haraway", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["oruka", "H. Odera Oruka", "Oruka", "1944–1995", "Kenyan philosopher who developed sage philosophy.", ["Sage Philosophy (1990)"], [], { x: "Henry Odera Oruka was born in Kenya in 1944. He studied in Sweden, at Uppsala, and in the United States, and taught for most of his career at the University of Nairobi, where he helped build the philosophy department. He died in a road accident in Nairobi in 1995.\n\nIn the 1970s and 1980s Oruka distinguished four trends in African philosophy. Ethnophilosophy, associated with Placide Tempels and John Mbiti, presented the communal beliefs of peoples as philosophy. Nationalist-ideological philosophy was developed by political leaders such as Kwame Nkrumah, Léopold Senghor and Julius Nyerere. Professional philosophy was written by academically trained philosophers. Sage philosophy, his own project, sought the critical reflection of individual wise persons in traditional communities.\n\nAgainst critics who held that philosophy requires writing, that traditional African societies discouraged individual critical thought, or that there was no African philosophy before contact with Europe, Oruka set out to show that rigorous reflection on fundamental questions, God, the person, freedom, equality, death, had long been practiced by a few individuals in African communities. From the mid-1970s he recorded dialogues with sages in rural Kenya, questioning them in a manner he compared to Socrates' conversations, and published the results in Sage Philosophy (1990).\n\nHe distinguished folk sages, who know and faithfully narrate the beliefs of their community, from philosophic sages, who subject those beliefs to rational criticism, express dissatisfaction with received opinion, and offer reasoned views of their own. The ideas recorded belong to the sages themselves, and the trained philosopher's role is to elicit them.\n\nCritics such as Peter Bodunrin asked whether the philosophy produced in such dialogues belongs to the sage or to the interviewing philosopher, and whether showing that sages can think philosophically shows that a tradition of systematic reflection exists. Oruka also wrote on ethics and political philosophy, including a right to a human minimum of welfare, collected in Practical Philosophy: In Search of an Ethical Minimum (1996), and on the ethics of humanity's relation to the earth.", src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/african-sage/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “African Sage Philosophy”" }],
  ["winant", "Howard Winant", "Winant", "b. 1946", "American sociologist of race.", ["Racial Formation in the United States (1986, with Omi)"], ["gramsci"], { x: "Howard Winant was born in 1946. He studied at Brandeis and took his doctorate at the University of California, Santa Cruz, in 1980. He has worked in Mexico, Brazil and Argentina, and since 2002 has taught at the University of California, Santa Barbara, where he is Distinguished Professor of Sociology and founded the University of California Center for New Racial Studies.\n\nWith Michael Omi he wrote Racial Formation in the United States (1986; later editions 1994 and 2015), which developed racial formation theory. Race, they argued, is neither a fixed biological essence nor a mere illusion. It is a social and political construction whose meanings are continually created, contested and transformed.\n\nRacial formation is the sociohistorical process by which racial categories are created, inhabited, transformed and destroyed. It proceeds through racial projects, which link interpretations of what race means with efforts to organize and distribute resources along racial lines, at the level of everyday interaction as well as institutions. Drawing on Gramsci's concept of hegemony, Omi and Winant analyzed how racial rule in the United States moved from domination toward hegemony, and how the civil rights reforms absorbed parts of the movements' demands while leaving racial inequality in place.\n\nThe book criticized earlier paradigms that reduced race to ethnicity, to class or to nationhood, and studied the racial state and the right-wing racial reaction that followed the 1960s.\n\nWinant's later books, Racial Conditions (1994), The World Is a Ghetto: Race and Democracy since World War II (2001) and The New Politics of Race (2004), extended the analysis comparatively and globally, treating race as a fundamental dimension of the modern world system and the postwar period as a historical break in its politics.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_Winant", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_formation_theory", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Racial formation theory”" }] }],
  ["collins", "Patricia Hill Collins", "Patricia Hill Collins", "b. 1948", "American sociologist of Black feminist thought.", ["Black Feminist Thought (1990)"], [], { x: "Patricia Hill Collins was born in Philadelphia in 1948. She studied at Brandeis and Harvard, taught and organized in Boston schools, directed the Africana Center at Tufts, and took her doctorate at Brandeis in 1984. She taught at the University of Cincinnati from 1982 to 2005 and then at the University of Maryland, College Park, where she is Distinguished University Professor Emerita. She was the first African American woman to serve as president of the American Sociological Association, and in 2023 she received the Berggruen Prize.\n\nBlack Feminist Thought: Knowledge, Consciousness, and the Politics of Empowerment (1990) reconstructed a Black women's intellectual tradition from sources often excluded from scholarship: fiction, poetry, music, oral history and the everyday knowledge of ordinary women. Black women developed their own worldviews, Collins argued, out of the need to define themselves and to work for justice, and their experiences at the intersection of oppressions illuminate social processes as a whole.\n\nCollins described Black women's position as that of the outsider within, people who, like domestic workers in white households, see dominant institutions from inside while remaining marginal to them. She analyzed the controlling images, the mammy, the matriarch, the welfare mother and the jezebel among them, that justify Black women's subordination, and the importance of self-definition against them.\n\nShe proposed the matrix of domination to describe how intersecting oppressions of race, gender, class and sexuality are organized across structural, disciplinary, hegemonic and interpersonal domains of power. Black feminist epistemology, on her account, uses lived experience as a criterion of meaning, tests knowledge claims in dialogue, and joins an ethic of caring with an ethic of personal accountability.\n\nLater books include Black Sexual Politics (2004), Intersectionality (2016, with Sirma Bilge) and Intersectionality as Critical Social Theory (2019), which argues that intersectionality, the concept named by Kimberlé Crenshaw, should be developed into a full critical social theory. Collins is among the central figures of intersectional feminism and standpoint theory.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patricia_Hill_Collins", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["honneth", "Axel Honneth", "Honneth", "b. 1949", "German critical theorist of recognition.", ["The Struggle for Recognition (1992)", "Reification (2005)"], ["hegel", "habermas"], { x: "Axel Honneth was born in Essen in 1949. He studied philosophy, sociology and German literature at Bonn, Bochum and the Free University of Berlin, where he wrote his doctorate on Foucault and critical theory, published as The Critique of Power. He worked as an assistant to Jürgen Habermas in Frankfurt, taught at Konstanz and the Free University, and in 1996 took Habermas's former chair of social philosophy at Frankfurt. From 2001 to 2018 he directed the Institute for Social Research, and since 2011 he has also taught at Columbia University. He is the leading figure of the Frankfurt School's third generation.\n\nThe Struggle for Recognition (1992) grounds critical theory in a theory of recognition. Drawing on Hegel's early Jena writings, in which the struggle for recognition replaces Hobbes's struggle for self-preservation, and on George Herbert Mead's social psychology, Honneth argued that people form a practical relation to themselves only through being recognized by others.\n\nHe distinguished three forms of recognition. Love, in intimate relationships, gives self-confidence; legal respect, as a bearer of equal rights, gives self-respect; and social esteem, for one's contributions and achievements, gives self-esteem. Each has a corresponding form of disrespect, abuse, the denial of rights, and the denigration of ways of life, and the experience of disrespect drives social struggles. A society can be criticized by asking whether its institutions provide adequate recognition.\n\nIn Redistribution or Recognition? (2003), a debate with Nancy Fraser, Honneth argued that struggles over economic distribution are themselves struggles for recognition, over how contributions are valued; Fraser replied that economic injustice has systemic roots that recognition cannot capture. Reification (2005) reinterpreted Lukács's concept as a forgetting of the prior recognition that underlies all knowledge of others.\n\nFreedom's Right (2011) developed a theory of justice as an account of social freedom, freedom realized in institutions of personal relations, the market and democratic will-formation, following the method of Hegel's Philosophy of Right. Later books include The Idea of Socialism (2015) and The Working Sovereign (2023), on work and democracy.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axel_Honneth", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/recognition/", srcName: "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Recognition”" }] }],
  ["omi", "Michael Omi", "Omi", "b. 1951", "American sociologist of race.", ["Racial Formation in the United States (1986, with Winant)"], ["gramsci"], { x: "Michael Omi was born in 1951 in Berkeley, California, into a Japanese American family, and grew up in San Francisco. He studied sociology at Berkeley and took his master's and doctorate at the University of California, Santa Cruz. He has taught for many years in the Department of Ethnic Studies at Berkeley, where his work spans race theory, Asian American studies and antiracist scholarship, and he has served as an associate director of the Haas Institute for a Fair and Inclusive Society.\n\nWith Howard Winant he wrote Racial Formation in the United States (1986), one of the most influential books in the sociology of race. Its theory of racial formation holds that race is a social construction, a concept that signifies social conflicts and interests by referring to types of human bodies, and that it is nonetheless real in its effects: racial categories shape identities, institutions and the distribution of resources.\n\nRacial meanings are made and remade through racial projects, efforts that interpret race and organize social life accordingly, from everyday expectations to state policy. Race works at both levels, in the racial common sense people learn without explicit teaching and in the institutions and ideologies of the state, media and economy.\n\nDrawing on Gramsci, Omi and Winant analyzed how racial rule in the United States moved from domination toward hegemony, and argued that the reforms of the civil rights era absorbed parts of the movements' demands while leaving institutional racism and racial inequality in place.\n\nOmi's essays have examined the changing meaning of race, antiracist organizing, and the classification of Asian Americans in the census, and the theory of racial formation has become a basic framework in sociology, ethnic studies and critical race theory.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Omi", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_formation_theory", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Racial formation theory”" }] }],
  ["mills", "Charles W. Mills", "Charles Mills", "1951–2021", "Jamaican-American philosopher of race and liberalism.", ["The Racial Contract (1997)", "Black Rights/White Wrongs (2017)"], ["marx", "rawls", "pateman"], { x: "Charles W. Mills was born in London in 1951 and grew up in Kingston, Jamaica. He studied physics at the University of the West Indies and taught physics in Jamaica before turning to philosophy, taking his doctorate at the University of Toronto with a dissertation on the concept of ideology in Marx and Engels. He taught at the University of Oklahoma, the University of Illinois at Chicago and Northwestern University, and from 2016 at the CUNY Graduate Center. He gave the Tanner Lectures in 2020 and died in 2021.\n\nThe Racial Contract (1997), his most influential book, used the social contract tradition against itself. The canonical theorists, Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau and Kant, describe a contract among free and equal persons. Mills argued that the real contract underlying the modern world was a racial contract, an agreement, sometimes explicit and more often tacit, among whites to categorize non-whites as less than full persons and to establish a system of white supremacy, through colonialism, slavery and segregation, from which whites benefit.\n\nThe racial contract also prescribes an epistemology of ignorance: a pattern of structured misperception that leads whites to misunderstand the world they have made, which Mills later developed in the essay “White Ignorance” (2007). With Carole Pateman he connected the racial contract with her sexual contract in Contract and Domination (2007).\n\nMills was a sharp critic of ideal theory in political philosophy, in particular of Rawls, whose idealizing assumptions, he argued, abstract away from the history of racial oppression and so leave liberal philosophy unable to address it. Yet in Black Rights/White Wrongs: The Critique of Racial Liberalism (2017) he argued that liberalism need not be abandoned. Liberalism as it actually existed was racial liberalism, and it could be reconstructed as a Black radical liberalism devoted to corrective justice.\n\nHis other books include Blackness Visible (1998) and From Class to Race (2003), essays on white Marxism and Black radicalism. Mills is regarded as one of the founders of the contemporary philosophy of race.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_W._Mills", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["fukuyama", "Francis Fukuyama", "Fukuyama", "b. 1952", "American political scientist.", ["“The End of History?” (1989)", "The End of History and the Last Man (1992)"], ["hegel", "kojeve"], { x: "Francis Fukuyama was born in Chicago in 1952. He studied classics at Cornell, where he was influenced by Allan Bloom, and took his doctorate in political science at Harvard under Samuel Huntington. He worked at the RAND Corporation and in the US State Department's Policy Planning Staff, then taught at George Mason, Johns Hopkins and Stanford University, where he is a senior fellow and directs a master's program in international policy.\n\nIn the essay “The End of History?” (1989), published as the Cold War ended, and the book The End of History and the Last Man (1992), Fukuyama argued that liberal democracy and market economics may mark the end point of humanity's ideological evolution. History, in the Hegelian sense he took from Alexandre Kojève, is the story of struggle over the principles of political organization, and after the defeat of fascism and communism no serious rival to liberal democracy remained as a universal ideal. Events would continue; the history of ideological alternatives had ended.\n\nThe book drew on Plato's concept of thymos, the part of the soul that demands recognition. Liberal democracy satisfies the desire for equal recognition (isothymia) but must contain the desire to be recognized as superior (megalothymia), and Fukuyama worried, following Nietzsche, that it would produce the last man, comfortable and without great aspirations. Critics read the thesis as triumphalist, often without engaging its qualifications.\n\nFukuyama was associated with neoconservatism and initially supported the Iraq war, but in America at the Crossroads (2006) he broke with the movement. Trust (1995) studied the role of social trust in economic life, and Our Posthuman Future (2002) warned that biotechnology could alter human nature and threaten liberal democracy.\n\nThe Origins of Political Order (2011) and Political Order and Political Decay (2014) argued that stable political orders require three institutions, an effective state, the rule of law and democratic accountability, and that established democracies, the United States included, can decay. Identity (2018) explained identity politics through the demand for dignity, and Liberalism and Its Discontents (2022) defended liberalism against the populist right and the progressive left.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Fukuyama", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre_Kojève", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Alexandre Kojève”" }] }],
  ["haslanger", "Sally Haslanger", "Haslanger", "b. 1955", "American philosopher of social construction, gender and race.", ["Resisting Reality (2012)"], [], { x: "Sally Haslanger was born in 1955. She studied at Reed College and took her doctorate at the University of California, Berkeley, in 1985. She taught at Princeton, Penn and Michigan, and is Ford Professor of Philosophy at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Her early work was in metaphysics, especially on persistence through change, and she has worked extensively on the situation of women and minorities in the profession of philosophy.\n\nHer paper “Gender and Race: (What) Are They? (What) Do We Want Them to Be?” (2000) asked what concepts of gender and race would best serve the struggle against injustice. Haslanger called this approach ameliorative analysis: asking what a concept is for and what it ought to be, alongside how it is actually used, a form of what is now called conceptual engineering.\n\nHer answer defined gender and race as social positions of hierarchy. On her account a woman is someone who is systematically subordinated along some dimension, economic, political, legal or social, and who is marked as a target for this treatment by observed or imagined bodily features presumed to be evidence of a female role in reproduction. Races are defined analogously, through subordination or privilege marked by features presumed to indicate ancestry from a geographical region. The goal of feminist and antiracist politics, on this view, is to eliminate gender and race so defined.\n\nCritics argued that the definition excludes some trans women and wrongly implies that a woman who is not subordinated, such as a queen, is not a woman, and Haslanger has since qualified it. Resisting Reality: Social Construction and Social Critique (2012) collected her papers and distinguished different senses of social construction, including causal construction, in which social factors bring something about, and constitutive construction, in which social relations are part of what it is.\n\nHer later work, including Critical Theory and Practice (2017), develops a theory of ideology and social practices, analyzing how unjust structures are sustained by shared cultural schemas and how they might be changed. She is one of the leading figures of analytic feminism and social philosophy.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sally_Haslanger", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["butler", "Judith Butler", "Butler", "b. 1956", "American philosopher of gender, performativity and vulnerability.", ["Gender Trouble (1990)", "Bodies That Matter (1993)", "The Psychic Life of Power (1997)"], ["hegel", "beauvoir", "austin", "foucault", "derrida"], { x: "Judith Butler was born in Cleveland in 1956. They studied philosophy at Yale, writing a doctoral dissertation on the reception of Hegel in twentieth-century France, published as Subjects of Desire (1987). After teaching at Wesleyan, George Washington University and Johns Hopkins, they joined the University of California, Berkeley, in 1993, where they became Maxine Elliot Professor. They received the Adorno Prize in 2012.\n\nGender Trouble (1990) is a founding text of queer theory. Feminist theory, Butler argued, had assumed a stable subject, women, and a distinction between biological sex and culturally constructed gender. Butler questioned both. Gender is performative: it is constituted through the repeated stylized acts, gestures and speech that are taken to express an inner identity. Drawing on Beauvoir, Foucault, Derrida and J. L. Austin's account of performative utterances, Butler argued that the appearance of a natural sex is itself produced by gender norms organized by what they called the heterosexual matrix. Drag, by exaggerating the imitation, can expose that all gender is imitative.\n\nBodies That Matter (1993) answered the objection that this made bodies unreal by examining how norms materialize bodies, and which bodies come to matter. Excitable Speech (1997) studied hate speech, and The Psychic Life of Power (1997) asked how subjects become attached to the very power that subordinates them. Antigone's Claim (2000) and Undoing Gender (2004) continued the work on kinship and gender.\n\nAfter 2001 Butler turned to violence, mourning and war. Precarious Life (2004) and Frames of War (2009) argued that lives are unequally grievable: some deaths are publicly mourned and others are not even registered as losses, and this distribution of grievability shapes politics and war. Notes Toward a Performative Theory of Assembly (2015) studied bodies gathering in public, and The Force of Nonviolence (2020) defended nonviolence as an active ethical and political practice. Who's Afraid of Gender? (2024) analyzed the international movements against “gender ideology.”\n\nButler's prose drew a “Bad Writing” award in 1998, Martha Nussbaum attacked their work as quietist, and feminists such as Nancy Fraser and Susan Bordo debated their account of gender. Parting Ways (2012), a Jewish critique of Zionism, and their statements on Israel and Palestine have drawn sharp controversy. Their work has been among the most influential in contemporary feminist, queer and political theory.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Butler", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["mbembe", "Achille Mbembe", "Mbembe", "b. 1957", "Cameroonian historian and political theorist.", ["On the Postcolony (2000)", "“Necropolitics” (2003)", "Critique of Black Reason (2013)"], ["bataille", "fanon", "foucault"], { x: "Achille Mbembe was born in 1957 near Otélé, in Cameroon. He took his doctorate in history at the Sorbonne in 1989 and studied political science in Paris. He has taught at Columbia, Pennsylvania, Berkeley, Yale and Duke, worked at the Brookings Institution and directed CODESRIA, the Council for the Development of Social Science Research in Africa, in Dakar. He is a research professor at the Wits Institute for Social and Economic Research at the University of the Witwatersrand in Johannesburg. He received the Holberg Prize in 2024.\n\nOn the Postcolony (French 2000, English 2001) argued that Western discourse on Africa has spoken more about the West's own fantasies and fears than about Africa, which appears in it only as a negation, a place of lack. Mbembe studied power in postcolonial African states through its forms of command, its excess and its everyday spectacle, in which rulers and ruled share a common stylistics of power marked by the grotesque and the obscene.\n\nHis essay “Necropolitics” (2003), later expanded into a book (2016; English 2019), extended Foucault's concept of biopower. Foucault had described modern power as the power to make live and let die. Mbembe argued that this did not account for the ways sovereign power, from the plantation and the colony to contemporary occupations and wars, exercises the power to decide who may live and who must die, creating death-worlds in which populations are subjected to conditions of life that confer on them the status of the living dead. He drew on Fanon, among others, and took examples from slavery, apartheid, Kosovo and Palestine.\n\nCritique of Black Reason (2013; English 2017) traced how the figure of the Black was produced, from the Atlantic slave trade onward, as the emblem of a human being reduced to a commodity, and argued that under neoliberal capitalism the condition once imposed on Africans is being generalized, a becoming-black of the world. Out of the Dark Night (2010; English 2021) reflected on decolonization, and Brutalism (2020) on the violence of contemporary capitalism and technology.\n\nIn 2020 an invitation to give the opening address at Germany's Ruhrtriennale festival led to accusations that he had relativized the Holocaust and supported boycotts of Israel, charges he and many scholars rejected. His work is a central reference in African studies, postcolonial theory and political philosophy.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achille_Mbembe", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["crenshaw", "Kimberlé Crenshaw", "Crenshaw", "b. 1959", "American legal scholar, a founder of critical race theory.", ["“Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex” (1989)", "“Mapping the Margins” (1991)"], ["bell"], { x: "Kimberlé Crenshaw was born in Canton, Ohio, in 1959. She studied at Cornell, took her law degree at Harvard in 1984 and a master's degree in law at Wisconsin. She has taught at the UCLA School of Law since 1986 and at Columbia Law School since 1995, and in 1996 co-founded the African American Policy Forum. In 1991 she worked with the legal team representing Anita Hill during the confirmation hearings of Clarence Thomas. Her memoir, Backtalker, appeared in 2026.\n\nCrenshaw was among the young scholars who in the 1980s built critical race theory, out of and against the critical legal studies movement and in dialogue with Derrick Bell; she helped organize the 1989 workshop at which the name was adopted. Her article “Race, Reform, and Retrenchment” (1988) analyzed how civil rights law both advanced and limited racial justice.\n\nHer essay “Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex” (1989) introduced the term intersectionality. Antidiscrimination law, Crenshaw argued, treats race and sex as separate categories, and so cannot see discrimination against people at their intersection. In DeGraffenreid v. General Motors (1976), Black women sued a company that had hired no Black women before 1964. The court rejected their claim: the company hired white women, so there was no sex discrimination, and it hired Black men, so there was no race discrimination. Black women's distinctive experience fell between the categories.\n\n“Mapping the Margins” (1991) extended the analysis to violence against women of color, distinguishing structural intersectionality, how intersecting inequalities shape women's experience of violence and of the services meant to help them; political intersectionality, how feminist and antiracist politics each marginalize women of color; and representational intersectionality, how cultural images construct them. Crenshaw has described intersectionality as a lens for seeing how forms of inequality operate together, and has criticized uses of the term as a catch-all for complexity.\n\nShe co-edited the reader Critical Race Theory: The Key Writings That Formed the Movement (1995), and through the African American Policy Forum has led campaigns such as #SayHerName, on police violence against Black women, and the defense of critical race theory against the campaigns to ban its teaching after 2020.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimberlé_Crenshaw", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_race_theory", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Critical race theory”" }] }],
  ["land", "Nick Land", "Land", "b. 1962", "British philosopher of the Warwick CCRU and of accelerationism, later associated with neoreaction.", ["The Thirst for Annihilation: Georges Bataille and Virulent Nihilism (1992)", "Fanged Noumena: Collected Writings 1987–2007 (2011)"], ["kant", "nietzsche", "bataille"], { x: "Nick Land was born in Sutton Coldfield, England, in 1962. He took his doctorate at the University of Essex in 1987, with a thesis on Heidegger's reading of the poetry of Georg Trakl, and lectured in philosophy at the University of Warwick from 1987 until he resigned in 1998. At Warwick, with Sadie Plant, he co-founded the Cybernetic Culture Research Unit (CCRU), a collective that produced what it called theory-fiction, mixing philosophy with cybernetics, science fiction, electronic music culture, numerology and the occult.\n\nHis book The Thirst for Annihilation: Georges Bataille and Virulent Nihilism (1992) read Bataille's thought of expenditure and excess as an attack on the human subject and its moral and economic restraint. Land's essays of the late 1980s and 1990s, collected in Fanged Noumena (2011), drew on Kant, Nietzsche, Bataille, Deleuze and Guattari and cyberpunk fiction, in prose that became increasingly experimental.\n\nIn these essays Land treated capitalism as the driving force of modernity, a process of deterritorialization that dissolves traditions, states and the human itself, and he criticized the anthropocentrism of philosophy and of left politics. He advocated accelerating this process, toward a technological singularity. The position later became known as accelerationism, a term coined by Benjamin Noys in 2010 to criticize it. With the CCRU Land introduced the concept of hyperstition, ideas that bring about their own reality by circulating as fictions.\n\nAfter leaving academia Land moved to Shanghai, where he has lived since, writing for local publications and online. From around 2012, in a series of essays called The Dark Enlightenment, he became a foundational figure of neoreaction, a movement opposed to democracy and egalitarianism. His later writings, including on what he called hyper-racism, have been widely criticized as racist, and he has been seen as one of the intellectual inspirations of parts of the alt-right.\n\nLand's early work influenced Mark Fisher, Ray Brassier and others associated with speculative realism and with left accelerationism, such as Nick Srnicek and Alex Williams, who sought to redirect acceleration toward postcapitalist ends. Fisher argued that Land's greatest impact was on music and art more than on academic philosophy.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Land", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["lewisgordon", "Lewis R. Gordon", "Lewis Gordon", "b. 1962", "American philosopher of existence, race and Africana thought.", ["Bad Faith and Antiblack Racism (1995)"], ["sartre", "fanon"], { x: "Lewis R. Gordon was born in 1962. He studied at Lehman College of the City University of New York and took his doctorate at Yale in 1993. He has taught at Purdue, Brown, Yale and Temple, where he was Laura H. Carnell Professor, and is professor of philosophy and Africana studies at the University of Connecticut. He has served as president of the Caribbean Philosophical Association and held a Nelson Mandela Visiting Professorship at Rhodes University in South Africa.\n\nBad Faith and Antiblack Racism (1995) brought Sartre's existential phenomenology to the analysis of racism. Antiblack racism, Gordon argued, is a form of bad faith: the self-deceiving flight from freedom and from the full humanity of others, in which a group of human beings is made into a category of being that is supposed to have no point of view of its own. Racism is thus an existential and phenomenological problem as well as a social and political one.\n\nFanon and the Crisis of European Man (1995) and later What Fanon Said (2015) made Gordon one of the leading interpreters of Frantz Fanon. With Existentia Africana (2000) and An Introduction to Africana Philosophy (2008) he developed Africana existential philosophy, the study of questions of identity, freedom and liberation arising from the experience of the African diaspora, with W. E. B. Du Bois and Fanon as central figures.\n\nGordon introduced the concept of disciplinary decadence, the process by which an academic discipline treats its own methods as complete and closed, forgetting that they are human creations open to revision. Against it he has called for a teleological suspension of disciplines and for shifting the geography of reason, recognizing the Global South and the African diaspora as sites of theoretical thought in their own right.\n\nHis later books include Freedom, Justice, and Decolonization (2021) and Fear of Black Consciousness (2022), which examines Black consciousness as a political and existential achievement and the reactions it provokes.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Gordon", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["fricker", "Miranda Fricker", "Fricker", "b. 1966", "British philosopher of epistemic injustice.", ["Epistemic Injustice (2007)"], [], { x: "Miranda Fricker was born in 1966. She took her doctorate at Oxford in 1996, supervised by Sabina Lovibond and Bernard Williams, and taught at Heythrop College, Birkbeck College and the University of Sheffield and at the CUNY Graduate Center before joining New York University in 2022, where she is Julius Silver Professor of Philosophy and co-directs the New York Institute of Philosophy. She is a Fellow of the British Academy and has served as president of the Eastern Division of the American Philosophical Association.\n\nEpistemic Injustice: Power and the Ethics of Knowing (2007) introduced the concept of epistemic injustice: a wrong done to someone specifically in their capacity as a knower. Fricker distinguished two forms.\n\nTestimonial injustice occurs when prejudice leads a hearer to give a speaker less credibility than they deserve. Her central example is the trial of Tom Robinson in Harper Lee's To Kill a Mockingbird, in which a Black man's testimony is disbelieved by a white jury because of racial prejudice. Such credibility deficits wrong the speaker by undermining them as a giver of knowledge, and they also deprive others of what the speaker knows.\n\nHermeneutical injustice occurs when a gap in the collective resources for understanding social experience leaves someone unable to make sense of, or communicate, an important part of their experience, because their group has been marginalized from the practices that generate shared meanings. Her example is the experience of sexual harassment before the concept was named, when women who suffered it could not articulate what was happening to them as a distinct wrong.\n\nFricker proposed corrective virtues of testimonial justice and hermeneutical justice for hearers. The concept has been taken up widely, in social epistemology, feminist philosophy and the philosophy of race, and in medicine and health care, where it is used to analyze the treatment of patients' testimony. Her later work has examined blame and forgiveness and the ethics of Bernard Williams.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_Fricker", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["noys", "Benjamin Noys", "Noys", "b. 1969", "British critical theorist.", ["The Persistence of the Negative (2010)", "Malign Velocities (2014)"], [], { x: "Benjamin Noys is a British critical theorist, born in 1969. He studied at Brunel University and at the University of Sussex, where he took his doctorate, and is professor of critical theory at the University of Chichester.\n\nHis first book, Georges Bataille: A Critical Introduction (2000), was followed by The Culture of Death (2005), on the politics of death and bare life in contemporary culture. The Persistence of the Negative: A Critique of Contemporary Continental Theory (2010) argued that much recent continental philosophy, including the work of Deleuze, Derrida, Latour and Negri, shared a commitment to affirmation, of life, desire, networks or creative potential, which Noys called affirmationism. Against it he defended the critical force of negativity, as a resource for thinking resistance and the break with capitalism.\n\nIn that book Noys coined the term accelerationism to name, critically, a current of thought that he traced from Marx's remarks on the progressive role of capital through Deleuze and Guattari's call to accelerate the process, Lyotard's Libidinal Economy and the writings of Nick Land: the idea that the way beyond capitalism is to intensify its own dynamics of deterritorialization. The name was then taken up by the movement's advocates, both left accelerationists such as Nick Srnicek and Alex Williams and figures on the far right.\n\nMalign Velocities: Accelerationism and Capitalism (2014) developed his critique, arguing that accelerationism expresses a fantasy of identification with capital's speed and machinery, and that its promise of liberation through intensification repeatedly turns into an aesthetic of violence and despair. He also edited Communization and Its Discontents (2011), on debates in the ultra-left about immediate communism, and has written critically about cryptocurrency.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Noys", srcName: "Wikipedia" }],
  ["srnicek", "Nick Srnicek", "Srnicek", "b. 1982", "Canadian theorist of platform capitalism and post-work politics.", ["“#Accelerate” (2013, with Williams)", "Inventing the Future (2015, with Williams)", "Platform Capitalism (2016)"], [], { x: "Nick Srnicek is a Canadian theorist, born in 1982. He studied psychology and philosophy, took a master's degree at the University of Western Ontario in 2007 and a doctorate in international relations at the London School of Economics in 2013, and is a lecturer in digital economy at King's College London. With Levi Bryant and Graham Harman he co-edited The Speculative Turn (2011), an influential collection on speculative realism and new materialism.\n\nWith Alex Williams he wrote the “#Accelerate: Manifesto for an Accelerationist Politics” (2013), which launched left accelerationism. Against Nick Land's embrace of capitalist acceleration, they argued that the left should accelerate technological development while redirecting it toward collective, postcapitalist ends, repurposing the platforms of capitalism and embracing modernity.\n\nInventing the Future: Postcapitalism and a World Without Work (2015), also with Williams, criticized what it called folk politics: the left's attachment since the 1960s to local, immediate, horizontal and small-scale action, such as protests and occupations, which they argued could not challenge global capitalism. Taking the long-term strategy of neoliberalism's Mont Pelerin Society as a model, they called for building a new left hegemony around four demands: full automation of work, a shorter working week, a universal basic income, and a weakening of the work ethic. They later dropped the label accelerationism.\n\nPlatform Capitalism (2016) analyzed how digital platforms such as Google, Facebook, Uber and Amazon became a dominant business model, extracting and controlling data as a raw material, and distinguished advertising, cloud, industrial, product and lean platforms. After Work: A History of the Home and the Fight for Free Time (2023), with Helen Hester, turned to domestic labor and the reduction of work in the home, and Silicon Empires (2025) examined the competition over artificial intelligence.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Srnicek", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inventing_the_Future", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Inventing the Future”" }] }],
  ["alexwilliams", "Alex Williams", "Alex Williams", "", "British political theorist.", ["“#Accelerate” (2013, with Srnicek)", "Inventing the Future (2015, with Srnicek)"], [], { x: "Alex Williams is a British political theorist and a lecturer in digital media and society at the University of East Anglia. His work concerns the future of left politics, hegemony and contemporary forms of digital power.\n\nWith Nick Srnicek he wrote the “#Accelerate: Manifesto for an Accelerationist Politics” (2013), which launched left accelerationism, and Inventing the Future: Postcapitalism and a World Without Work (2015). The book criticized the folk politics of the contemporary left, its preference for local, immediate and small-scale action, and called for a long-term counter-hegemonic strategy modeled on the success of neoliberalism, organized around four demands: full automation, a shorter working week, a universal basic income and a weakening of the work ethic.\n\nHis own book, Political Hegemony and Social Complexity (2020), develops a theory of hegemony for complex societies, drawing on Gramsci and complexity theory to ask how political projects can shape social systems no one can fully control. With Jeremy Gilbert he wrote Hegemony Now: How Big Tech and Wall Street Won the World (And How We Win It Back) (2022), which analyzes the rise of platform and financial power and the prospects for a democratic left.", src: "https://www.versobooks.com/authors/1954-alex-williams", srcName: "Verso Books, author page", srcs: [{ src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inventing_the_Future", srcName: "Wikipedia, “Inventing the Future”" }] }],
  ["bhattacharya", "Tithi Bhattacharya", "Bhattacharya", "", "Historian and theorist of social reproduction.", ["Social Reproduction Theory (ed., 2017)"], ["vogel"], { x: "Tithi Bhattacharya was born in India and is a professor of South Asian history at Purdue University. Her first book, The Sentinels of Culture (2005), grew out of her doctoral work on the British-educated middle class of nineteenth-century Calcutta, and Ghostly Past, Capitalist Presence (2025) is a social history of fear in colonial Bengal. She was one of the national organizers of the International Women's Strike of March 8, 2017.\n\nShe edited Social Reproduction Theory: Remapping Class, Recentering Oppression (2017), with a foreword by Lise Vogel and contributions by Nancy Fraser, David McNally, Susan Ferguson, Cinzia Arruzza and others, which did much to revive social reproduction theory. The volume builds on Vogel's argument that the oppression of women is rooted in the reproduction of labor power. Most Marxist work, it argues, has concentrated on production; social reproduction theory studies the unpaid and paid work that produces and sustains people, in households, schools, hospitals and even prisons, and how gender, race and sexuality shape that work.\n\nOn this account, the activities that make and remake life are a condition of capital accumulation, and their organization ties economic exploitation to social oppression. The volume presents this as a unitary framework, an alternative to theories that treat class, gender and race as separate systems to be added together, and draws out its implications for organizing beyond the workplace, in struggles over health care, education, housing and public services.\n\nWith Cinzia Arruzza and Nancy Fraser she wrote Feminism for the 99%: A Manifesto (2019), which links feminism to anticapitalist and antiracist politics against a liberal feminism of individual advancement.", src: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tithi_Bhattacharya", srcName: "Wikipedia", srcs: [{ src: "https://www.plutobooks.com/9781786801586/social-reproduction-theory", srcName: "Pluto Press, Social Reproduction Theory" }] }],
  ["jager", "Anton Jäger", "Jäger", "", "Historian of political thought, writing on populism, capitalism and democracy.", ["Hyperpolitik (2023; English, Hyperpolitics)"], [], { x: "Anton Jäger is a Belgian historian of political thought who writes on populism, capitalism and democracy, in scholarly work and in essays for journals such as New Left Review and The Point.\n\nHis best-known book, Hyperpolitik (2023), published in English as Hyperpolitics (Verso, 2026), reworks essays that first appeared in those journals. It measures modern Western politics along two dimensions: politicization, individual political acts such as protesting, voting or posting, and institutionalization, membership in parties, unions, clubs and churches.\n\nJäger divides the history since the Second World War into four eras. In the age of mass politics, intense political engagement ran through strong mass organizations. The post-politics of the 1990s combined low engagement with hollowed-out institutions. Anti-politics, a period of revolt against the political class, followed. The present era of hyperpolitics is an intensified anti-politics: social media generate constant cycles of outrage and mobilization, and almost everything is treated as political, yet without durable organization the politicization has few lasting consequences.\n\nHe attributes this to market pressures and the professionalization of parties: neoliberal policies reduced working people's income and free time for civic life, convenient consumption made staying home more attractive than joining groups, and parties turned toward donors and consultants and away from members. He suggests that institutions might be rebuilt through everyday activities such as care work and neighborhood organizing, while remaining pessimistic about states increasingly insulated from popular pressure.", src: "https://publicseminar.org/2026/06/review-hyperpolitics/", srcName: "Public Seminar, review of Hyperpolitics (2026)" }],
].map(([id, n, sn, y, d, w, i, more]) => ({ id, n, sn: sn || n, y, d, w, i, ...(more || {}) })); // more: { x (a longer account), src, srcName }
